Ceviri.ekle("en","ekokuma",{
"nasil-osman1":{"baslik": ["How was Osman Ghazi regarded? A shepherd, or a warrior ghazi?", "b3e81b94"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nThe most reliable contemporary witness about Osman Ghazi is the Byzantine historian Pachymeres, who presents him as the most daring of the leaders raiding Byzantine territory. The warriors (alps) and ghazis of the frontier would gather under the banner of whichever leader was most successful in raids for booty; what set Osman apart from the other alps was his ability to rally them and their nökers (companions-in-arms) around himself. He distributed the places he conquered to his comrades as homelands; he consulted men learned in Islamic law, such as Edebali and Dursun Fakih, asking how newly taken places should be organised. According to Ottoman tradition, the testament he left to his son Orhan counsels him to abide by the Sharia, to look after those under his command and to be generous.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe criticism directed at Osman concerns his lineage rather than his character. In the 1380s Kadi Burhan al-Din, to belittle him, referred to him as the son of a boatman; Timur too, in the letter he wrote to Bayezid I the Thunderbolt, sought to demean the Ottoman sultan as descended from a family of Turkmen boatmen. When the ancestry of the dynasty turned into a diplomatic dispute in the time of Timur's son Shah Rukh, the court historians wrote a genealogy linking Osman to the line of the eldest son of Oghuz Khan. No contemporary source reports any personal fault; it is related that in his last years, because of gout in his foot, he left affairs to Orhan.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nThe chronicles (Aşıkpaşazade, Neşri) rest largely on the lost work of Yahşi Fakih, son of Orhan's imam; checking the place names has shown that this narrative contains accurate information. Yazıcıoğlu Ali writes that the frontier lords assembled and elected Osman khan according to Oghuz custom. Modern historians diverge: M. Fuad Köprülü regards the Kayı as the first ethnic nucleus of the state; Paul Wittek, however, argues that the dynasty had nothing to do with the Kayı tribe and that the genealogy reaching back to Oghuz Khan was produced for political ends in the reign of Murad II. The family trees in the chronicles also disagree with one another.\n\n■ HOW WAS HE REGARDED\nHalil İnalcık, the author of the article, opposes both extremes. Those who portray him as a shepherd are mistaken: Osman was a warrior ghazi (alp gazi) who, on the frontier, organised Turkmens and 'garibs' (strangers who had left their homeland) for holy war (ghaza). On the other hand, to look at the legendary material in the chronicles and treat the whole early period as fable, declaring it a 'black hole', is in his view also taking the easy way out. İnalcık's conclusion is this: the chronicles are incomplete but not empty; when they are tested against the contemporary Byzantine witness and endowment deeds, Osman can be traced as a real frontier leader.", "1a8dc1b8"], "kisa": ["The Ottoman chronicles portray him as a khan of Oghuz Khan's line, chosen by the lords; his enemies, by contrast, belittle him as a 'boatman's son'. The only contemporary witness we have is a Byzantine historian.", "a01f8ea6"]},
"nasil-osman2":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Osman II? As an early reformer, or as an inexperienced and stingy youth?", "53642cb1"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nLocal and foreign sources present him as brave, proud, envious of his ancestors' victories, and extremely skilled in the use of arms and in horsemanship. Before Khotyn he personally made great efforts in the assaults on the Polish-Cossack fortifications. Breaking with the model of a sultan cut off from the people and shut away in his palace, he adopted the type of a ghazi ruler who sought to become one with the people. An Arabic elegy written in the reign of Murad IV stresses that after the holy war he wished to perform the pilgrimage, which none of his ancestors had done, and that he had no other ulterior motive. The poems he wrote under the pen name Farisi reflect a high literary power.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe same sources remember him as stingy, harsh by nature and a man not loved by the army or the ulema. In the campaign against Poland he was sparing in distributing gratuities to the troops and cut off the arpalıks (revenue grants) of the ulema; his having the soldiers' pay counted out one by one for three days angered the leading Janissaries. His impulsive behavior on the march disturbed the standing army (kapıkulu) and the viziers. At Khotyn, rivalry among the pashas and administrative incompetence prevented success. His intention to make the pilgrimage was not welcomed by the ulema; nor did his marriage to the daughter of the Shaykh al-Islam Esad Efendi improve relations between them. When the revolt began, with the inexperience of his youth, he was unable to keep a cool head.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nThe English ambassador Thomas Roe writes that his mingling with the people in ordinary clothes and raiding taverns and boza houses did not befit the majesty of a ruler and made him a man who was not feared but despised; according to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, some Ottoman sources also say this in a veiled way. Hüseyin Tugi, who witnessed the events, wrote from the standpoint of the standing army; the information he gave passed into later chronicles and fed the image of Osman as a great reformer. Karaçelebizade and Peçevi connect his intention to make the pilgrimage with the resentment born of the failure at Khotyn. Intentions such as moving the capital or abolishing the Janissaries, on the other hand, are according to the TDV propaganda of his opponents that seeped into the sources. Venetian reports relate that by marrying four freeborn women he wished to break down the tradition of the harem.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nFeridun Emecen's judgment is measured: the young sultan was easily taken in by the words of those around him and may have intended to carry out certain reforms, perhaps in order to revive the brilliance of the eras of Selim the Grim and Suleiman the Lawgiver. But the identity of a great reformer and innovator ascribed to him is bound up with the political messages of later centuries. The majesty he lost among the people is clearly visible in the conduct of the army and the ulema toward him. His tragic end was an event such as had never before been seen in Ottoman history.", "56482276"], "kisa": ["Modern historical writing remembers him as a great reformer who fell victim to the Janissaries; the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, however, says that this 'reformer' label is bound up with the political messages of 19th- and 20th-century historians. His contemporaries find him brave but stingy and harsh.", "888ffe09"]},
"nasil-osman3":{"baslik": ["What did you make of Osman III? Just and thrifty, or a man of strange temperament?", "7672334b"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nAccording to the TDV article, the point on which contemporary historians agree about Osman III is that he hated bribery and lying. That he tried to be thrifty, to act justly and to concern himself closely with the needs of the people is recounted along with illustrative incidents. It is stated that he took strict measures against banditry on the pilgrimage route and against corruption in the pious foundations (waqfs), and sent decrees against the unruly levends in Anatolia and Rumelia. He was present in person at the scene of the Hocapaşa (1755) and Cibali (1756) fires; two-thirds of the walled city burned. He had the Nuruosmaniye Complex, begun by Mahmud I, completed and opened it in 1755, had the İhsaniye quarter established in Üsküdar and gave the order for the first lighthouse at Ahırkapı. The budget continued to show a surplus; the first treaty with Denmark was signed in his time. He wrote his imperial rescripts (hatt-ı hümayun) in a fine and legible naskh hand.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe same contemporaries also write that he lacked “teennî”, that is, the patience that does not act in haste: he would decide suddenly and regret it afterwards. In his three-year reign he made six new appointments to the grand vizierate and four to the office of Shaykh al-Islam. The sixty-three-day grand vizierate of Bıyıklı Ali Pasha ended in execution; Hekimoğlu Ali Pasha escaped execution only through the plea of the queen mother (valide sultan) and was sent into exile. Behind the scenes of all these changes appears the chief eunuch of the Abode of Felicity, Ebülvukūf Ahmed Agha, whom he had known since his years in the kafes. The official chronicler records that on his tours in disguise he had several non-Muslims who did not obey the dress prohibitions hanged; as for the bans he introduced on women's clothing and on migration to Istanbul, the sources of the age report that they could hardly be enforced.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nAccording to what TDV reports, the historian Şem'dânîzâde Süleyman Efendi attributes the frequent changes of office to the sultan's strange temperament and to the fact that no one could behave in a way that suited his disposition. The official court chronicler Hâkim Mehmed Efendi records the harsh punishments on his tours in disguise and the finer points of the tobacco ban; İsmâil Ziyâî writes that he went about in the dress of the ulema (religious scholars) and introduced himself as “Osman Agha of Edirne”. Some Western sources attribute his death to an operation on a tumour in his thigh. As for the widespread tale that he wore shoes studded with silver nails in the harem so as not to see the concubines, according to the author of the article no source for it has been found.\n\n■ WHAT DID YOU MAKE OF HIM\nThe author of the article, Fikret Sarıcaoğlu, does not read the frequent changes of grand vizier merely as ill temper: he considers that they may also be thought of as an attempt to reduce the role of the office of grand vizier, which had gained excessive weight in the preceding period. He writes that it is not possible to determine the mark left on his personality by the very long years spent in the kafes; he nevertheless adds that it is possible to look for traces of his years of confinement in ordinary behaviour such as mingling with the people in disguise or eating on the road the fruit he had bought on an outing. He also finds the reports that the heir apparent, Prince Mehmed, had been poisoned on his orders to be unfounded, on the grounds that the man alleged to have been responsible became grand vizier and a prospective imperial son-in-law in the following reign. The portrait that emerges: the honest but impatient sultan of a short, quiet reign.", "359631ab"], "kisa": ["A sultan who hated bribery and lying, was thrifty and took an interest in the people; but also one who changed six grand viziers in three years, made sudden decisions and regretted them afterwards. Contemporary historians agree on both.", "b6f0acbc"]},
"nasil-selim1":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Selim the Grim? As a shedder of blood, or as a just ruler?", "10596620"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nOttoman sources remember him as a man who followed affairs of state closely, wanted whatever he planned to be done at once, and was extremely disciplined and determined; he was true to his word, courteous by nature and elegant in speech. He disliked pomp and dressed simply. He read a great deal and had a deep interest in history: in Egypt he had Kemalpaşazade translate Ibn Taghribirdi's history. He knew Persian very well and has a divan (collected poems) of his Persian verse. He made it a principle to take the opinions of scholars and statesmen on political matters. He had the body of the Mamluk sultan Qansuh al-Ghawri, whom he had defeated at Marj Dabiq, found and buried in Aleppo, and had prayers recited for his soul. From Tabriz he sent about a thousand men of learning and art to Istanbul.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe same sources also write that he had a harsh temperament that did not shrink from shedding blood. He took the throne by deposing his father through the pressure of the Janissaries; afterward he had his nephews put to death, and then his brothers Korkut and Ahmed. He had some members of a Mamluk embassy killed on suspicion of an assassination plot and sent the ambassador back with grave insults. In the Divan (imperial council) he often grew angry with the pashas and sometimes walked out of the meeting. The historian Celalzade Mustafa Çelebi, who was in his service, reports that on his deathbed Selim told Pîrî Mehmed Pasha that he had committed much oppression. In western sources he is known above all for his mercilessness and bloodshed.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nAccording to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, although western sources find him merciless, some ambassadorial reports bring his justice to the fore. The accounts of his close confidant Hasan Can passed into the Tâcü't-tevârîh of the latter's son, Hoca Sadeddin; Kemalpaşazade, İdris-i Bitlisi, Celalzade and Âlî also include anecdotes about his deeds. His fame gave rise to the genre called 'Selimname', written specifically in his name. The TDV corrects two widespread beliefs: the claim that he had 40,000 Kizilbash killed in Anatolia is not true; according to recent studies, severe punishments were meted out mostly to Safavid propagandists and the members of the dervish lodges that supported them, and some were exiled. The story that he took over the caliphate at a ceremony in Hagia Sophia also has no historical value.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nAccording to Feridun Emecen, the author of the article, the eight-year reign is a turning point in Ottoman history. His averting of the Safavid threat and his bringing to the fore a strict Sunni outlook heralded an important transformation in political and social life; his putting an end to the Mamluk Sultanate was the first step in a project to gather the Islamic world under a single banner. Emecen notes that some of the anecdotes about him have been passed on by certain authors as historical fact, and that most of them must have arisen from his distinctive conduct, which could not be compared with that of the other sultans.", "3d108e8f"], "kisa": ["Westerners wrote of his mercilessness, some ambassadors of his justice. On his deathbed he himself told his vizier that he had committed much oppression, but that he had done so for the good of the state and the people.", "1b171831"]},
"nasil-selim2":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Selim II? As magnanimous, or as dissolute?", "e443d100"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nAccording to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, the sources of the period present him as a generous, magnanimous ruler who did not wish to hurt anyone's feelings. He liked to have scholars and poets around him; he showed great respect to the Shaykh al-Islam Ebussuud Efendi. The poems he wrote under the pen names Selimi and Talibi are counted among the fine examples of divan literature. Like his ancestors he was fond of hunting, could draw the stiffest bows and was a skilled archer. Mimar Sinan's Selimiye Mosque in Edirne, the repair of Hagia Sophia and the porticoes of the Masjid al-Haram in Mecca are his works. He closely followed the work on the new fleet, which was built in a short time; he greeted the capture of Tunis with joy.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe same sources also remember him as fond of pleasure and amusement and a regular at drinking gatherings. Âlî Mustafa Efendi writes that while he was a prince he had with him more than twenty-seven poets, boon companions and conversationalists, and that he spent his days in poetry, music, eating and drinking, and conversation. Whereas his father often went out to the Friday procession, he neglected it; Fresne-Canaye of the French embassy saw him leave the palace for Friday prayers only twice in three months. After the Janissary revolt in the first days of his reign, he left all affairs to Sokollu Mehmed Pasha. Before his death, according to Selaniki, he had repented of drink and revelry.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nBusbecq, who was gathering information during the struggle among the princes, describes him as a man resembling his mother, addicted to drinking, not at all courteous, lazy and liked by no one; the soldiers, he says, used to mock him. In 1553 the Venetian Navagero calls him miserly, dissolute and two-faced. Fresne-Canaye writes that he saw the sultan in the garden at Seraglio Point amusing himself with his acrobats, mutes and dwarfs, his cheeks swollen from drink. S. Gerlach attributes his death to his drinking an excessive amount of water after eating a great deal of mutton sausage; another account reported by the TDV is that he fell ill when he suddenly gave up drinking. In Âlî Mustafa Efendi's portrayal, by contrast, there is a sultan who stood out for his imposing bearing and who spoke with a lisp.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nThe point stressed by Feridun Emecen, the author of the article, concerns the institution more than the personality: Selim II is the first sultan born in Istanbul who ascended the throne, and the first Ottoman ruler who went on no campaign at all during his eight-year reign. According to Emecen, his not appearing before the people at the Friday procession and not going on campaign at the head of the army began a sharp change in the conception of the sultanate. Even so, Emecen adds that although he left affairs to Sokollu, he did not refrain from closely following and intervening in the matters that caught his attention.", "5af6b1bf"], "kisa": ["Ottoman sources remember him as a generous, magnanimous ruler who did not wish to hurt anyone's feelings; western ambassadors call him 'miserly, dissolute, two-faced'. As the first sultan never to go on campaign, he also changed the conception of the sultanate.", "bd1cac6e"]},
"nasil-selim3":{"baslik": ["What did you make of Selim III? The renewer of his nation, or a ruler “as pliant as water”?", "25ee85fe"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nAccording to the ambassadors' impressions reported in the TDV article, Selim III was a man of calm manner, mild character and charitable disposition; he was a ruler who worked with great seriousness for the state to regain its former strength, who had devoted himself to his nation and believed that he had been sent for its salvation. The printing press was an important occupation of his: he himself determined the books to be printed and rewarded the craftsmen. He had Vauban's works translated and printed, and took enough interest in the science of war to write a treatise on rockets and cannon. He had a military engineering school established in Hasköy (1795) and large barracks in Levend and Üsküdar; he developed the Selimiye district. According to the author of the article, the reform programme was not only military but comprehensive enough to embrace all branches of civil administration. He was a poet with a divan (collected poems) under the pen name “İlhâmî”, a Mevlevi and the patron of Şeyh Galib; according to the music section of the TDV article, no Ottoman sultan's love of music and skill as a composer reached his level.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe same article openly sets out his weaknesses. His mild temper, tolerance and forgiving nature were human virtues; but for a statesman embarking on radical reform they were a serious weakness that endangered the lives of those who had set out with him. He did not show a firm line of character and was described as “su gibi meyyal” (as pliant as water); in time no one feared him any longer, and he lost his majesty. In 1807, without using the trained troops at his disposal, he gave up everything without a fight; in the hope of remaining on the throne, he abolished the New Revenue Treasury with his own hand and delivered his reform team to the executioner. He himself, after leaving the throne, said with remorse, “My forbearance is the cause of all this.”\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nAccording to what TDV reports, in 1789 the Prussian ambassador Diez saw him as superior to his nation in his qualities and destined to be its renewer, but added that long years were needed for a state that had been declining for a hundred years. The Prussian ambassador Knobelsdorf (1792) gives the number of the circle conducting the reform work as forty; Olivier argues that this “inner cabinet” divided authority. As for Zinkeisen's comment that he ought to have left matters to a strong grand vizier, the author of the article finds it invalid, a judgement made under the impact of the defeat. After he was killed, his virtues were long recounted like a legend in the coffee houses of Istanbul.\n\n■ WHAT DID YOU MAKE OF HIM\nThe verdict of the author of the article, Kemal Beydilli, is twofold. On the one hand: time showed that, despite his good intentions, he lacked the firmness of character, perseverance and foresight needed to sustain this great undertaking for years; the general opinion is that he did not have the personality to see his task through. On the other: before fifteen years had passed, people began to concede that Selim, whom the opposition had attacked in harsh words, had been right; the suppression of the Greek revolt in the Morea within a few months by Egyptian forces trained in the Western manner laid before everyone's eyes the value of the army he had wished to create.", "a7537d09"], "kisa": ["An enlightened reformer devoted to his nation; but a sultan who, because of his mildness, delivered those who had set out with him to the executioner and in the end abandoned his work with his own hand. That he had been right, however, was conceded before fifteen years had passed since his killing.", "291d1355"]},
"nasil-suleyman1":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Suleiman the Lawgiver? As the sultan of a golden age, or as the hero of a myth?", "2ed36da2"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nContemporary Ottoman and western sources describe him in similar terms. In 1520 a Venetian ambassador depicts him as an amiable and good-natured young man; in 1553 another Venetian ambassador writes that he had won renown for being very just, that he did wrong to no one, and that he was more devoted to his religion than his ancestors had been. The Süleymannames praise him as a ghazi sultan, a just ruler, a protector of Islam and a patron of literature and art; they describe him as generous, refined, modest and dervish-like in manner. The historian Celalzade Mustafa Çelebi stresses three of his qualities: his justice, his being a protector of the people, and his being a conqueror on a world scale. He was not impulsive; he reached his decisions by discussing them with his viziers and convening consultative councils. He wrote poetry under the pen name Muhibbi; in his time poets such as Baki and Fuzuli and scholars such as Ebussuud enjoyed patronage. Even the Safavid Shah Tahmasp had greeted his holy war with admiration.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nHe held the interest of the state above everything and did not hesitate to sacrifice his family for its sake: the deaths of his sons Mustafa and Bayezid are cited as examples of this. According to a Venetian source, his making Hürrem his wife through a formal marriage contract, contrary to custom, did not please the people; most Ottoman historians write that the faction made up of Hürrem, Mihrimah and Rüstem Pasha played a role in the affair of Prince Mustafa, and they accuse the sultan of having fallen under their influence. His making it commonplace for viziers to be sons-in-law of the dynasty later became the target of sharp criticism by Koçi Bey. The financial strain of long campaigns and a religious hardening fed social reaction; an anonymous treatise written in the 1550s and Lutfi Pasha's Asafname describe the decay in the institutions already in those years.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nLater generations regarded his era as an ideal age; this idea grew stronger in years of crisis and in time turned into the concept of a 'golden age'. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, 19th-century Ottoman historians, with the tendency to idealize that they had learned from their European colleagues, saw the age of the Lawgiver as a kind of Age of Felicity (the era of the Prophet). The TDV considers a weak contrivance the count found in some sources seeking to give him a mystical image: 'ten viziers, ten scholars and ten poets of the tenth sultan'.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nAccording to Feridun Emecen, the author of the article, this age truly left its mark on the 16th century: the Ottomans became the world-scale empire of Europe, assumed a decisive role in the European political balance, and the Lawgiver won a place among the rulers of his time that bore no comparison with theirs. But Emecen says that the myth that formed around the epithet 'the Lawgiver' has overshadowed a dispassionate view; this period, in which the great victories have been brought to the fore, should be assessed with a cooler approach.", "0b293611"], "kisa": ["Even when his grandson was on the throne, people looked back on his era with longing. But the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam reminds us that a myth was built around the name 'the Lawgiver' and that social tension and decay also began in those same years.", "ea80b6f6"]},
"nasil-suleyman2":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Süleyman II? As a well-meaning, dervish-like man, or as a fragile sultan in the hands of centers of power?", "65ec4372"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nThe sources describe him as a well-meaning sultan, far from dissipation, fond of bestowing favors, pious and dervish-like. He came to the throne amid the devastation that followed the rout at Vienna; although he was ill, he went on campaign in a litter as far as Sofia. When Niš fell, the palace aghas and the ulema demanded the dismissal of the grand vizier; he pointed out the danger of changing the commander-in-chief while the enemy was facing them and rejected this. He supported Köprülüzade Fazıl Mustafa Pasha and stood behind his financial measures; in this period Niš and Vidin were recovered, as was Belgrade after an eight-day siege. The Sancakburnu fortress, destroyed in the İzmir earthquake, and the Eyüp Sultan Mosque, damaged by fire, were repaired in his time. As a prince he took calligraphy lessons from Tokatlı Ahmed Efendi and showed proficiency in the thuluth and naskh scripts.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nBecause of his long life of imprisonment he could not receive a good education; he had a fragile nature and was for the most part under the influence of the centers of power in the palace. His placing, despite Köprülüzade's warning, a woman steward (kethüda) at the head of the Harem after his mother's death and assigning her a has (crown revenue grant) was a practice without precedent. When some of the women and aghas in the Harem said that Mehmed IV, too, had been taken to Istanbul and then deposed, he was seized with apprehension and wished to stay in Edirne; he was persuaded only through a consultative council. The first years of his reign passed in turmoil, in which brigands such as Yeğen Osman defied him and Skopje in the Balkans was burned and looted.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nThe sources report that when people came to raise him to the throne, he thought he was going to be killed, asked permission to perform his prayers, and said that rather than dying every day for forty years he considered it better to die one day sooner. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam interprets his giving the grand vizier, as he welcomed him on his return from Belgrade, his own fur robe, dagger and aigrette and praying for him in tears as a reflection of his helplessness in the face of the news of failure coming from the front and of his state of mind. The sources record that before his death he received the grand vizier alone and prayed in tears for the army's victory. Physically he is described as resembling his ancestors, with an aquiline nose and a large build.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nThe picture drawn by Abdülkadir Özcan is one of understanding mingled with sorrow: a sultan who bore the marks of forty years in the 'kafes' (the princes' confinement), well-meaning but lacking the education and the will to carry the burden. The bright moments of his reign sprang less from himself than from his trust in Fazıl Mustafa Pasha. His reign, which lasted three years and eight months, ended in Edirne with the dropsy from which he had long suffered; he left no children.", "d4b7f846"], "kisa": ["The sultan who lived for forty years in the 'kafes' (the princes' confinement) and came to the throne thinking he was being led to his death went on campaign in his short reign with his ailing body and saw Belgrade recovered. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam remembers him as well-meaning but uneducated and open to influence.", "25dde147"]},
"nedim-lale-devri":{"baslik": ["Nedîm comes to the fore as the pioneer of Tulip Era poetry", "d3c40dbb"], "metin": ["Throughout the Tulip Era of 1718-1730, Nedîm developed a new style of poetry with his simplicity of expression and witty manner. This style, which grew up in the circle of Nevşehirli Damad İbrahim Pasha, is considered the greatest representative of the \"localization\" current in divan poetry — in place of abstract conceits, the daily life of Istanbul and its culture of entertainment entered poetry.", "608e220a"], "sanatci.ad": ["Nedîm (original name Ahmed)", "60c38afb"], "sanatci.hayat": ["He was born in Istanbul, probably in 1092 (1681); his father was Kadı Mehmed Efendi, grandson of a kazasker (chief military judge), and his mother belonged to the Karaçelebizâde family. He died in 1143 (1730).", "040eee15"], "sanatci.onem": ["In TDV's description, \"the poet who created a style in divan poetry known by his own name\" — with his plain and witty manner he became the best-known name of the Tulip Era.", "8d247a98"]},
"nedim-olumu":{"baslik": ["The death of Nedîm during the Patrona Halil revolt", "06bf909e"], "metin": ["Nedîm lost his life during the Patrona Halil revolt of 1730; according to the sources, he died either after falling ill from the terror of the revolt or by falling from the roof of his house while trying to escape — the sources diverge on the details of how he died. With his death the brightest voice of the Tulip Era in poetry also fell silent; the era itself ended with the same revolt.", "d255366d"], "sanatci.ad": ["Nedîm (original name Ahmed)", "60c38afb"], "sanatci.hayat": ["He was born in Istanbul, probably in 1092 (1681); his father was Kadı Mehmed Efendi, grandson of a kazasker (chief military judge), and his mother belonged to the Karaçelebizâde family. He died in 1143 (1730).", "040eee15"], "sanatci.onem": ["In TDV's description, \"the poet who created a style in divan poetry known by his own name\" — with his plain and witty manner he became the best-known name of the Tulip Era.", "8d247a98"]},
"nefi-idami":{"baslik": ["Nef'î is executed because of his satires", "5e459c60"], "metin": ["Nef'î, known for his satires, was executed in 1635 on the orders of Murad IV because of his collection of satires, Sihâm-ı Kazâ (Arrows of Fate). Grand Vizier Bayram Pasha handed him over; Nef'î was strangled in the palace woodshed and his body thrown into the sea — the enmities he had earned with his pen brought about his end.", "8999f9d1"], "sanatci.ad": ["Nef'î (original name Ömer)", "6231c83f"], "sanatci.hayat": ["He is estimated to have been born around 980 (1572) in the Pasinler (Hasankale) district of Erzurum; he came from the family of a sanjak governor (sanjakbey). He died in 1044 (1635).", "21fd5d24"], "sanatci.onem": ["In TDV's description, \"a divan poet famous for his satires and odes\" — with his powerful and assertive style he is considered the most remarkable name in Ottoman satirical literature.", "d42cd3f1"]},
"olum-ibrahim-1648":{"baslik": ["The killing of Sultan İbrahim (1648)", "8c6a80d8"], "metin": ["Sultan İbrahim was deposed on 8 August 1648 by an alliance of the military corps and the ulema, on grounds of extravagance and a crisis of government. Ten days later, on 18 August 1648, according to Katib Çelebi's account, he was strangled to death by the executioner in the presence of the Shaykh al-Islam, the grand vizier and the agha of the Janissaries — on the basis of the fatwa that had been obtained. His seven-year-old son Mehmed IV succeeded him; power passed in practice into the hands of the senior queen mother, Kösem Sultan.\n\nThis is a certain fact, carried out by hands outside the DYNASTY (the alliance of the military corps and the ulema). Note: TDV's 'Kösem Sultan' entry writes, on the authority of Karaçelebizade, that Kösem Sultan is stated to have had a hand in this execution too — this additional claim remains at the level of report.", "14169b66"], "kisa": ["Ten days after his deposition he was strangled by the executioner before the Shaykh al-Islam, the grand vizier and the agha of the Janissaries.", "c0809abc"]},
"olum-kosem-sultan-1651":{"baslik": ["The killing of Kösem Sultan (1651)", "80b4b460"], "metin": ["The senior queen mother Kösem Sultan — a figure who, although tied to the dynasty not by blood but through marriage, in practice directed state policy during the reigns of three sultans (Murad IV, İbrahim, Mehmed IV) — was killed in the Harem on 2-3 September 1651 by the chief lala (başlala) Uzun Süleyman Agha and his men, acting on the orders of Mehmed IV's mother Turhan Sultan.\n\nThis is a certain fact, carried out by hands outside the DYNASTY (but from the highest echelon of the palace); it is described clearly in TDV as the result of a ruthless power struggle. This death is regarded as the effective end of the period called the 'Sultanate of Women'.", "674aac94"], "kisa": ["The most powerful woman of the reigns of three sultans was strangled in the Harem by the men of her daughter-in-law Turhan Sultan — the effective end of the 'Sultanate of Women'.", "472e4d8d"]},
"olum-osman-ii-1622":{"baslik": ["The murder of Osman II (1622)", "28b7b320"], "metin": ["Osman II (Young Osman) was deposed by the Janissaries and sipahis, who rose in revolt on 20 May 1622 when word got out of his plan to disband the Janissary corps and, under the pretext of going on the pilgrimage, to raise a new army. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, the sultan was put on a pack horse 'in a dreadful manner, bareheaded and in a wretched state', taken first to the Orta Mosque, where Mustafa I was, and in the afternoon of the same day to Yedikule (the Seven Towers), where he was strangled. According to some historians, to prove his death his ear and nose were cut off and shown to Mustafa I's mother.\n\nThis is a certain fact, carried out by hands outside the dynasty, which TDV plainly describes as 'the first case in Ottoman history of a sultan being murdered in a military rebellion' — not a report but a documented execution.", "8aefa77b"], "kisa": ["The first case of a sultan being openly murdered in a military rebellion — put on a pack horse, he was taken first before the former sultan and then to Yedikule.", "11beb0aa"]},
"olum-sehzade-mehmed-osman-ii-1621":{"baslik": ["The strangling of Prince Mehmed (1621)", "956b60a2"], "metin": ["To secure his throne, Osman II had his brother Prince Mehmed killed on 12 January 1621 (18 Safar 1030) — this is a fact confirmed in both TDV's 'Osman II' and 'Mustafa I' entries, not a report. The decision was taken as soon as it was resolved to set out on the campaign against Poland (Khotyn): he did not want to leave behind him a brother who might covet the throne.\n\nA notable detail: the Shaykh al-Islam Hocazade Esad Efendi refused to give a fatwa for this decision, taken at the urging of the Kızlar Agha (chief harem eunuch) Süleyman Agha; the fatwa for the execution was finally given by the kazasker (military judge) of Rumelia, Taşköprizade Kemaleddin Mehmed Efendi. By contrast, Osman did not touch his uncle Mustafa I, whose sanity was being questioned in the same period — TDV states that this was probably because his uncle was already thought to pose no threat to the throne (on account of his mental condition).\n\n⇒ In other words, his 'attitude towards his uncle' and his 'having his brother killed' are two separate events, and TDV does not confuse the two: removing his uncle from the throne was the decision not of Osman but of the leading statesmen (1618); having his brother killed, on the other hand, was Osman's own direct order (1621).", "91bc4ece"], "kisa": ["Before setting out on campaign, so as to leave no rival behind him, he had his brother strangled — one Shaykh al-Islam refused to give a fatwa, and a kazasker took on the task.", "5d7014d8"]},
"osman2-hac-niyeti-katl-1622":{"soru": ["Why did Young Osman's intention to make the pilgrimage cost him his life?", "20f7a968"], "baslik": ["The torn fatwa and Yedikule", "dd96def6"], "metin": ["When Osman II began preparing for the pilgrimage five months after returning from Khotyn, rumours spread that he would gather troops from Anatolia to do away with the Janissaries and would move the capital; the TDV regards these largely as the propaganda of his opponents. According to an account in Venetian reports, when the Shaykh al-Islam Esad Efendi could not persuade him to build a mosque instead of going on the pilgrimage, he issued a fatwa that for the sultan to rule with justice was better than the pilgrimage; it is also alleged that the sultan, spurred into action again by a dream, tore up the fatwa. Seized in the revolt that broke out at the news that the tents had been moved across to Üsküdar, the sultan was strangled at Yedikule on 20 May 1622. Some historians write that, as proof of his death, his ear and nose were cut off and shown to Sultan Mustafa's mother.", "a73f72ac"], "not": ["The tearing of the fatwa and the detail of the ear and nose appear in the sources with the qualification \"it is alleged\" or \"according to some historians\"; the killing itself is certain.", "38bf59fb"]},
"osmanli-halifesini-taniyan-tanimayan-cografyalar":{"ad": ["Where in the world was the Ottoman caliph recognised — and where was he not?", "ba416c76"], "metin": ["First, the concept. 'To recognise' means two different things here. The first is legal: having the Friday sermon (khutba) read in the caliph's name and gaining legitimacy through his confirmation. The second is spiritual: regarding the caliph as the head of Islam and showing him respect, expecting help from him in times of distress, collecting donations in his name. What was at issue for the Ottoman caliph in the 19th century was largely the second — because most Muslims were by now subjects of other states.\n\nWHY the caliphate came to the fore. In the reign of Abdülhamid II care was taken, in official and private correspondence, in newspapers and in state ceremonies, to give prominence to the titles of caliph and amir al-mu'minin (commander of the faithful), because of their religious and political connotations, in place of titles such as sultan and padishah. Any questioning of the legitimacy of the Ottoman caliphate met with a fierce reaction; newspapers were published in Istanbul, Cairo and London to propagate this legitimacy, treatises on the caliphate were commissioned and distributed throughout the Islamic world, and influential ulama and sheikhs of Sufi orders were invited to Istanbul and entertained there.\n\nTHE CLEAREST example: Aceh (Sumatra). A delegation from the sultanate of Aceh, which was seeking help against Portuguese attacks, came to Istanbul in 1565; upon the death of Suleiman the Lawgiver at Szigetvár, the Acehnese offered their allegiance to Selim II, and a defence treaty was signed in 1567. Along with the ships and soldiers he sent, Selim II also dispatched a copy of the khutba to be read there. From that date until the beginning of the 20th century this copy of the khutba was read in the Friday sermon every week in Aceh. Nor was the bond one-sided: the Acehnese sent material aid during the Crimean War, and their sultans proudly wore the decorations sent by the Ottoman caliph. What best shows the reality of the bond is the reaction of the other side: in the five-article ultimatum it delivered to Aceh on 26 March 1873, the Netherlands demanded that relations with the caliph in Istanbul be broken off and that the crescent and star, which it regarded as the symbol of Islam, be removed from the flag of Aceh. When the Acehnese did not accept, war was declared.\n\nINDIA. The activities among the Muslims of India mostly took the form of organising aid campaigns for the Ottoman State and sending a message to Britain by increasing the religious influence of the sultan-caliph. There were very many initiatives and successes were achieved; the name of Abdülhamid II spread to the remotest corners of the country. From the war of 1877-78 onwards, the Muslims of India tried at every opportunity to put pressure on the British government to get it to take a stance in favour of the Ottomans. It is also known that during the National Struggle a considerable amount of financial aid reached Turkey from the Muslims of India and Central Asia.\n\nIRAN — the sectarian barrier and the attempt to overcome it. In earlier centuries the difference of sect had been a negative factor between the two countries. In the second half of the 19th century the fact that both were exposed to the Russian threat made rapprochement a necessity; there were mutual congratulations and exchanges of gifts, special care was shown for the holy places, and the necessity of Islamic unity (ittihad-ı İslam) was discussed at the highest level. Mozaffar al-Din Shah visited Istanbul in 1900. But an alliance somehow never came about. Abdülhamid II's expectation was highly optimistic: a union in which the rulers of the Iranians would continue to reign in Iran while command of the army would rest with the office of the caliphate. Jamal al-Din al-Afghani was invited to Istanbul and charged with bringing about a Shi'i-Sunni rapprochement; he had the Iranians form a society, and in a short time 600 letters were sent and about 200 replies received; however, the Iranian government's anxiety and the killing of Naser al-Din Shah by one of Afghani's disciples brought these efforts to nothing.\n\nMOROCCO — an open rejection. At one point the Sultan of Morocco said that he too was a caliph; a letter rejecting this claim was sent to him from Istanbul. The non-recognition of the Ottoman caliphate in the west of North Africa was not an exception but a long-established state of affairs: the Almohads and the Hafsids too had shown that they were rivals of the caliphate in the east by using the title of amir al-mu'minin.\n\nTHE ARABIAN Peninsula — objection from within. The British tried to spread among the Arabs the claim that the caliphate belonged to Quraysh, and the Ottoman government resolutely opposed this. The claim had a basis: the hadith collections contain reports that the caliphate belongs to Quraysh and that caliphs must be of Quraysh. However, the same source also records that there are doubts about the authenticity of these reports — especially those which give the words caliph and caliphate a technical meaning and can be directly connected with later struggles for power.\n\nCENTRAL Asia and the Far East. What was done in these places was more in the nature of making things known and establishing contact: making known the existence of a strong Muslim state under the caliph's rule, interceding with the states concerned over the injustices the Muslims there suffered, sending delegations, and dispatching teachers and religious books. The Ottoman consul (şehbender) in Java raised with the Dutch government the fact that Muslim merchants were obliged to pay more tax than Christian merchants. The 1886 report of Ali Galib Bey, consul in the Indonesian islands, examines the geographical, religious and demographic structure of the region and lists what needed to be done for the policy of the caliphate. Similar reports also came from the consuls in India, Iran and Central Asia. Russia and the Netherlands were disturbed by these activities; in India the consul Hüseyin Kâmil Bey was declared 'persona non grata'.\n\nHOW much of it was real. The political strength of this bond should not be exaggerated — and the one saying so is not the opposing side but the source itself. Statements attributed to Abdülhamid II such as 'A single word from the caliph would be enough to raise the whole Islamic world' should be understood not as reflecting the realities of the period but as a bluff directed at the colonial powers; the sultan openly admitted this during the First World War. Allegations that secret agents were systematically sent out or that organisations with military aims were set up in Islamic countries could not be confirmed by the inquiries of British governments. On the other hand, it cannot be denied that a common consciousness had awakened: the solidarity shown in the war of 1877-78, in the Armenian events of the 1890s, in the Ottoman-Greek War of 1897 and in the Hejaz railway project is an indication of it.\n\nTHE FINAL act. The emphasis placed on Islamic unity in order to win support from the Islamic world in the Tripolitanian and Balkan wars reached its highest level with the proclamations of jihad in the First World War — but by that time hardly any independent Islamic world remained. After the abolition of the caliphate on 3 March 1924, Islamic unity disappeared from the agenda as a policy.", "d08fee10"], "kisa": ["A khutba read every Friday in Sumatra and Morocco's own claim to the caliphate fit into the same century. Recognition was not a legal tie but in most places a spiritual allegiance — and for precisely that reason it is hard to measure.", "eba91a5f"], "not": ["The question of which country 'recognised' the caliph cannot be given an answer in terms of international law in the present-day sense: in the 19th century a large part of the Muslim population were already subjects of other empires, and the bond was formed not between states but between communities and Istanbul. Cases such as Aceh, which can be measured by the continuity of the khutba, are exceptions; in most places the measure is the trace left by donation campaigns and embassy reports. Malaysia and Pakistan did not exist at that time under their present names; the lands of the former lay within British Malaya and those of the latter within British India.", "49fe877d"], "bag": ["For the place of the caliphate in domestic politics see the card on the three political courses; for the call to jihad during the war, the card on the Great Jihad (Cihad-ı Ekber).", "39c2d3e4"]},
"osmanli-ingiliz-ticaret-mekanizmasi-1580":{"oneri": ["An ahidname gave English merchants the SAME rights as the French and the Venetians", "a25e246c"], "sebep.b": ["The diplomatic initiative undertaken with the Grand Vizier Sokollu Mehmed Pasha by William Harborne, the English representative sent to Istanbul in 1578, so that English merchants could enter the Ottoman market on equal terms with the French and the Venetians", "b4b5efcb"], "sonuc.b": ["With the signing of an ahidname dated 1580, English merchants gained the right to benefit to the same degree from the commercial capitulations previously granted only to the French and the Venetians", "da943f36"], "bag": ["The general history of the capitulations (privileges) and the debate over 'weakness or instrument?' are already treated in the related further reading card id:kapitulasyon-zaaf-mi-arac-mi; this card does not repeat that debate but only explains the concrete mechanism of a single ahidname (on whose initiative it came about and what equality it secured).", "2642cb26"], "metin": ["An ahidname was a market opened not by war but by negotiation: guarantees of life and property and customs terms were granted to the subjects of the other state, and in return both sides gained from the expansion of trade. The position England won with this ahidname prepared the ground, within a few decades, for the rise of great trading companies such as the Levant Company.", "50efb883"]},
"osmanli-toplumunda-ermeniler-1461-1923":{"ad": ["From the Conqueror to Lausanne: the Armenians in Ottoman society", "0c65f510"], "metin": ["The millet system. According to the TDV article on the millet, in the Ottoman Empire a 'millet' was not an ethnic but a religious unit; the expression “millet-i Ermeniyân” (the Armenian millet) occurs in documents of the 16th to 18th centuries. The beginning of the Armenians' millet status is disputed: the generally accepted view is that Sultan Mehmed the Conqueror appointed the metropolitan Hovakim, whom he had brought from Bursa, as patriarch in Istanbul in 1461. In the 16th and 17th centuries, although there were the catholicosates of Echmiadzin, Sis and Aghtamar and the Patriarchate of Jerusalem, the Armenian Patriarch of Istanbul was the head of the millet of the Ottoman Armenians. The article writes that the Syriac Orthodox community was also organised together with the Armenians for a time, because both churches were opponents of the Council of Chalcedon.\n\nTheir place in SOCIETY. According to the same article, the millet system was not a class system; within every millet there were privileged people. Examples of this among the Armenians are the vardapets and the 'amira' group that served the state. The tax-paying reaya, on the other hand, included everyone, Muslim, Christian and Jew; the difference lay in fiscal obligations (such as the jizya). The TDV's city articles also show the Armenians' settlement in Anatolian towns: Evliya Çelebi, in the 1640s, counts 70 Muslim and 7 Armenian quarters in Erzurum, and 9 Muslim and 3 Armenian quarters in Van.\n\nSPLIT into three (1830-1850). According to the TDV articles on the millet and on tehcir, Armenians who converted to Catholicism under the influence of Catholic missionaries came under pressure from the mother church; after the war of 1828-29, Mahmud II recognised the Catholic Armenians as a separate millet in January 1830, and in 1850, under British pressure, the Protestant Armenians were also deemed a separate millet. The TDV article on the millet also records that in 1820 an uprising of the Gregorian Armenians against Patriarch Boghos was harshly suppressed by the state. In the interpretation of the article on tehcir, the Armenian nation was thus divided into three mutually hostile communities — this is the article's own assessment.\n\nTHE TANZIMAT and reform. According to the TDV article on the Reform Edict, after the edict of 1856 each community was given its own regulations in 1862; the regulations given to the Armenian community, while preserving the powers of the patriarch, established a national assembly of 400 members — the article says that in the manner of its establishment and election this assembly “practically resembled the Chamber of Deputies”. According to the article on the millet, the Tanzimat brought lay merchants and officials into the millet assemblies and the election of the patriarch; by an imperial decree dated 1 March 1857 it was agreed that in provincial places where Armenians and Catholics were numerous, they would take precedence in protocol; after the Tanzimat the Greek patriarch was placed on an equal footing with the other religious heads.\n\nTHE CONSTITUTIONAL ERA. According to the TDV article on Abdülhamid II, in the parliament of 1877 the Armenian and Greek deputies asked that their own languages also be official languages; in 1909 the four-man delegation that conveyed to Abdülhamid the decision on his deposition included the Armenian Aram, a member of the Senate (ayan).\n\nLAUSANNE and after. According to the TDV article on Lausanne, Articles 37-44 of the treaty granted certain rights to the non-Muslim minorities living in Turkey. The size of the Armenian community in Turkey in the Republican period and the present state of its institutions were not found in the sources consulted.", "7bea4362"], "kisa": ["In the Ottoman Empire the Armenians were organised as a 'millet', that is, as a religious community. This system functioned for four hundred years; in the 19th century the division of the community into three, the reform edicts and the intervention of the great powers changed it fundamentally.", "06798265"], "not": ["The economic role of the Armenians in the classical Ottoman period (banking and money-changing, the mint, trade networks) appears in the TDV articles consulted only at the level of the 'amira' and the 19th-century Christian middle class (the article on the Ottomans); no separate source was read for details. The history of the Armenians before the Ottomans (the Bagratid Kingdom, Vaspurakan, the Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia) falls outside the scope of this card; the TDV article on Van writes that in 1021, 40,000 Armenian families migrated with their princes to Sivas and Kayseri.", "297f84c5"], "bag": ["For how the community turned into an international question in the 19th century, see the card 'Chronology of the Armenian question'.", "7e56a88b"]},
"osmanlicilik-islamcilik-turkculuk-uc-tarz":{"ad": ["Ottomanism, Islamism, Turkism: three answers to the same question", "b906d781"], "metin": ["What was the question. The nationalism that spread after 1789 had put imperial structures under the threat of breaking up into national states. The Serbian and Greek revolts, and the turn of the Christian subjects in the Balkans toward separatism with European support, left Ottoman statesmen face to face with a single question: what can hold together so many different elements?\n\nOTTOMANISM — common citizenship. The first of the answers was a common Ottoman identity that did not recognise differences of religion and descent on the official plane. The main principle of the concept is summed up in the remark Mahmud II is reported to have made in 1826: I distinguish the Muslims among my subjects in the mosque, the Christians in the church and the Jews in the synagogue; there is no other difference between them. Ottomanism was not a saving policy on its own but the social model of the Tanzimat project. With the Tanzimat edict of 1839 and the Reform Edict of 1856, the equality of Muslims and non-Muslims was proclaimed for the first time; the jizya was abolished, mixed courts were established, and schools were opened to everyone. Article 8 of the 1876 Constitution translated this into the language of law: all individuals who are subjects of the state are called 'Ottoman', without exception, whatever their religion or denomination.\n\nBUT neither side embraced it. The non-Muslims regarded the Reform Edict not as an edict of brotherhood but as one of privileges, and pursued their national aspirations; the Muslims opposed it, saying that the right of the 'ruling nation', won with the blood of their ancestors, had been lost. As the edict was put into practice, bloody events broke out in Maraş, Jeddah, the Balkans, Lebanon, Damascus and Crete.\n\nISLAMISM — the axis of solidarity. In the mid-1870s, when territorial losses in the Balkans and the migration of Muslim refugees made the population of Anatolia predominantly Muslim, a policy that rested the survival of the state on the Muslim element and on Islamic solidarity came to the fore. This was the Union of Islam (ittihâd-ı İslâm), which had been influential since the 1860s and became the basic policy in the reign of Abdülhamid II. Islamism was also an intellectual movement: to defend the Islamic world against European colonialism and to answer the intellectual challenge of the West with a more rational interpretation of religion, more in keeping with the age.\n\nTURKISM — the latest to start. Cultural Turkism gained momentum in the reign of Abdülhamid II: the work of Hungarian Turcologists, the decipherment of the Orkhon inscriptions by Vilhelm Thomsen in 1893, and the newspaper Tercüman, which İsmail Gaspıralı published in Bakhchisaray from 1883 onwards. However, because of the censorship of the period there was no serious debate on political Turkism that was reflected publicly; that debate began in the Young Turk publications printed abroad. The newspaper Türk, which began to appear in Cairo on 5 November 1903, was the first organ in this field.\n\nTHE FIRST text to set the three side by side. Yusuf Akçura's 'Three Types of Policy' (Üç Tarz-ı Siyâset) was published in the same newspaper (14 April 1904 and following issues). Akçura lists three options — pan-Islamism, Ottomanism and a Turkish nationalism based on race — and says that under the existing conditions the most practicable is Turkism. He describes Ottomanism as an attempt to produce a new nationality united by a common homeland, as in the United States of America, and considers working for it a wasted effort. The replies given by Ali Kemal and Ahmed Ferid made the debate one of the empire's basic intellectual questions.\n\nTHEY did not succeed one another. When the Second Constitutional Era was proclaimed in 1908, Ottomanism was emphasised anew; within a few months the union of Crete with Greece, the annexation of Bosnia and Herzegovina by Austria-Hungary and Bulgaria's declaration of independence weakened that emphasis. As Turkism drew closer to the Committee of Union and Progress, this time it was the opposition parties that defended Ottomanism. The loss caused by the Balkan Wars of 1912-13 put a definitive end to the discourse of Ottomanism. There was no unity within Turkism either: Babanzade Ahmed Naim divided the Turkists into two groups, 'pure Turkists' and 'Turkist-Islamists'. In 1913 Ziya Gökalp, under the heading 'Turkification, Islamisation, Modernisation', proposed thinking of the three together.", "7bca66f0"], "kisa": ["All three were answers to the question 'how can this state remain standing'; they did not succeed one another, but lived intertwined for a long time. It is an article of 1904 that first set all three in a single list.", "68c4ece2"], "not": ["None of these three names belongs to a single organisation or a single group; the same person could defend two of the three at once at different periods of his life. The neat sequence of 'first Ottomanism was tried, when it failed they moved to Islamism, and when that failed too they moved to Turkism' is a scheme constructed afterwards; the sources show that the three lived intertwined for a long time.", "959c807c"], "bag": ["For the proclamation of the Second Constitutional Era and the turning of the first parliament into a struggle among the different elements, see the cards on the constitutional period; for the outward face of the Union of Islam policy, see the card on the recognition of the caliphate.", "ecb7b259"]},
"p76f-arnavut-ihtida":{"ad": ["Why did the Albanians and Bosnians choose Islam, while the Serbs and Bulgarians did not?", "2884816c"], "metin": ["Conversion in the Ottoman Balkans cannot be tied to a single cause, but two poles stand clearly apart. After the conquest, the Serbian, Bulgarian and Greek communities were able to maintain their own church organisations within the millet system — the Patriarchate of Peć, the Archbishopric of Ohrid and the Greek Orthodox Patriarchate in Istanbul; community courts, tax collection, schools and the appointment of priests remained in the hands of this hierarchy. Such a framework meant an institution that carried identity, and it made changing religion both unnecessary and costly.\n\nThe situation of the Albanians was different: they were divided between Catholics in the north and Orthodox in the south, with no free-standing 'Albanian church' in between; society was organised largely as mountain tribes. In the first century, moreover, the Ottoman administration opened not a path of coercion but a path of participation — it is recorded that throughout the fifteenth century timars were granted to Christians as well. The transformation was seen first among the military and administrative elites and became widespread over time.\n\nThe result can be read in the ranks of the state: at least thirty-two grand viziers are known to have been of Albanian origin; Albanians long held a prominent place in the corps of the standing army (kapıkulu). In other words, conversion worked not as exclusion from the centre but as a way of entering it.\n\nThe case of the Bosnians is the disputed sibling of this one. It was long argued that members of the 'Bosnian Church' were under both Catholic and Orthodox pressure and therefore converted to Islam more readily; today this thesis is disputed and is not considered sufficient on its own. What is certain is that in Bosnia, too, there was no strong, sheltering framework of a national church.\n\nA word of caution: this is not a question of 'who was more loyal'. The same Albanian communities were the ones that rose in revolt when the centralising policy of tax collection and disarmament hardened; in 1908 the highlanders had opposed the collection of their weapons. Changing religion did not mean political loyalty.", "15d5e15a"], "kisa": ["Who became Muslim in the Balkans is explained not by coercion but by the resilience of institutions: peoples with a church hierarchy that remained standing kept their religion, while for those without one the path to conversion stayed open.", "f67e1306"]},
"p76f-arnavutluk-istiklal":{"ad": ["Did Albania become independent without fighting?", "22c9ca83"], "metin": ["On 28 November 1912 Ismail Kemali proclaimed the independence of Albania at Vlorë. The proclamation was made in the middle of the Balkan War, in the weeks when the Ottoman army was disintegrating in Rumelia.\n\nThe impression that 'they became independent without fighting' is correct in one respect: there was no war of liberation waged by the Albanians against the Ottomans that ended in independence; independence was the product not of a military victory but of diplomatic recognition.\n\nBut to leave the picture at that would be misleading. At the time of the proclamation, the lands where the Albanians lived were the theatre of operations of three neighbouring armies; Serbian, Montenegrin and Greek forces were advancing from the north, the east and the south. The new state's real struggle was waged not against the Ottomans but against this partition, and at the table of the great powers. Independence was the effort of a movement without an army to save its land by making use of external balances of power.\n\nFor this reason, the proclamation of 1912 and the drawing of the borders are not the same thing: the proclamation was a matter of a single day, whereas the border became a bargaining process that lasted for years, and a significant part of the lands where Albanians lived remained outside the new state.", "aa9ce6ba"], "kisa": ["Independence was obtained not through a war won against the Ottomans but by holding on at the negotiating table against the neighbours who came to divide up the land as the Ottomans withdrew.", "7c663186"]},
"p76f-balkan-bozgun":{"ad": ["How did four small states win? The causes of the Balkan rout", "eee0b1a1"], "metin": ["In a year, 1912, during which the Balkan states were signing treaties of alliance with one another, the Sublime Porte failed to read these preparations in time. What is more, trusting in assurances that the likelihood of war was remote, one hundred and twenty trained battalions in Rumelia were demobilised. The war began at the moment when the army was at its weakest.\n\nThe second cause lay within the army: during the war, differences of political opinion within the army played a major part in the defeat. An army whose officer corps is split into political camps also loses unity of command at the front.\n\nThe third is geography: Rumelia is a long, narrow area with many fronts; when four states pressed from different directions at the same time, it was not possible to rush reserve forces from one front to another.\n\nThe surrender of Salonica is the most striking scene of this picture. The city fell on 23–24 October 1912 without meeting serious resistance; Tahsin Pasha surrendered to the Greeks at Salonica with his army of 35,000. This moment, in which it was not an army that took the surrender of a city but a city that took the surrender of an army, was long debated by contemporaries as well.\n\nShortages of equipment, money and trained officers are also counted; but without the three factors above, these alone do not explain a collapse of such speed against four states.", "1b7a5ca7"], "kisa": ["The army went to war having demobilised one hundred and twenty of its trained battalions and split into political camps within itself; as for the formation of the alliances, it failed to notice it in time.", "fd2b3416"]},
"p76f-cihad-ilani-1914":{"ad": ["Why did the proclamation of jihad not have the expected effect?", "2bbcd795"], "metin": ["In November 1914, through fatwas issued by the Shaykh al-Islam, Muslims were called in the name of the caliph-sultan to jihad against the Entente powers. The real addressee of the call was not the Ottoman subjects but the Muslim population in the British and French colonies; Germany had pinned great hopes on this move.\n\nThe expected general uprising did not take place. Foremost among the reasons is the position of the call: the proclamation of jihad had been made as an ally of another Christian state — Germany. Having, on the one hand, a call to jihad against Christian states and, on the other, an alliance with a Christian state made it easier for the call to be read as a political rather than a religious instrument. The counter-propaganda of the Entente powers worked on precisely this contradiction.\n\nThe second reason is structural: although the feeling of attachment to the caliph was strong among the Muslim communities under colonial administrations, in most places there was no organised structure to put this attachment into action.\n\nThe limit of the political influence of the caliphate was seen even more sharply two years later: the Emir of Mecca, Sharif Husayn, rose against the Ottomans with British support. In the very region where the call to jihad was expected to be strongest, the opposite happened.\n\nNote: the exact date on which the fatwas were read out differs between sources; for this reason the month is given here and the day is left as disputed. As for the question 'Had jihad been proclaimed before in the capacity of caliph?', no record that would answer it could be found (bulunamadı / not found) in the source consulted.", "30d113d6"], "kisa": ["The call made in the capacity of caliph aimed to raise the Muslims under colonial rule in revolt; the fact that the call itself had been made on the side of one alliance caused it to be read from the very start as a political move.", "8f038d70"]},
"p76f-goeben-breslau":{"ad": ["Goeben and Breslau: two ships presented as purchased", "aaf9008a"], "metin": ["Fleeing the pursuit of the British navy, the Goeben and the Breslau entered the Dardanelles on 10 August 1914. Warships taking refuge in the port of a neutral state had to be seized; instead, the ships were presented as having been purchased by the Ottoman State, joined the navy under the names Yavuz and Midilli, and entered service under the command of the German admiral Souchon.\n\nThe effect of this solution on domestic politics was also strong. The Ottoman public had only recently reacted to Britain's withholding, at the delivery stage, of two dreadnoughts — Sultan Osman and Reşadiye — for which money had been collected from the people. The joining of two German ships to the navy was received as compensation for this loss.\n\nThe military consequence was soon seen: while the actual command of the navy was in the hands of a German admiral, the operation carried out in the Black Sea determined the state's entry into the war.\n\nThe later fate of the ships — the Yavuz's long years of continued service in the Republican period and the loss of the Midilli during the war — is outside the scope of this entry and requires a separate source.", "8e2984a0"], "kisa": ["Two German cruisers let into the Straits on 10 August 1914 were presented as purchased when the law of neutrality required their seizure; they joined the navy under the names Yavuz and Midilli, but their command remained with the German admiral.", "87e240a9"]},
"p76f-istanbul-antlasmasi-1913":{"ad": ["The Treaty of Istanbul: the only piece that could be recovered from the Balkan disaster", "80018fe5"], "metin": ["The falling-out among the allies in the Second Balkan War gave the Ottoman State the opportunity to cross beyond the line drawn by the Treaty of London. The text that turned this de facto situation into law is the Treaty of Istanbul of 29 September 1913, signed with Bulgaria.\n\nThe treaty of twenty articles returned Edirne to Ottoman control; a semicircular piece of territory 30 km in diameter around the city was also included in this arrangement. The Maritsa (Meriç) River was fixed as the frontier.\n\nThe importance of the treaty lies not in the size of the territory but in its direction. Throughout the Balkan Wars all movement had been in one direction — loss. This text is the only point at which that direction could be reversed, and it forms the basis of present-day Turkey's border in Thrace.\n\nNor was the treaty limited to territory: reciprocal population arrangements between the two countries and the rights of the Muslims remaining in Bulgaria were also written into the text. Kavala and Dedeağaç, however, were left to Bulgaria — that is, the outlet to the Aegean could not be recovered.", "1b92ea98"], "kisa": ["The treaty of twenty articles dated 29 September 1913 returned Edirne to the Ottomans; the Maritsa (Meriç) River became the frontier.", "94ee7855"]},
"p76f-italya-libya-amac":{"ad": ["Why did Italy attack Tripoli — and why was Libya left to the very last in the colonial race?", "bb0d509e"], "metin": ["Italy's target was no accident: the province of Tripoli and the sanjak of Benghazi, lying between Tunisia and Egypt, were the only piece of North Africa still attached to the Ottomans. Failing to obtain a share in the partition of Ottoman lands under the 1878 Treaty of Berlin was regarded as a lasting deficiency for Italy; the following thirty years were spent on a policy of filling this gap.\n\nThe official justification was the rhetoric of 'bringing civilisation': the claim that these lands had not developed under Turkish rule was put forward to justify the policy of acquiring colonies. This is a language repeated in all the colonial ventures of the period, and it is not a cause but a pretext.\n\nThe answer to the question 'Was Libya left to the last because it was more barren?' is partly yes, but this was not decisive. The grain and olive plains of Algeria and Tunisia, and Egypt's cotton and the Suez Canal, were sources of revenue that the Libyan coast could not offer; this carries weight in the order of events. The real difference, however, lies in the calendar: when France and Britain made these moves, each was a colonial state a century old, whereas Italy was a state that had only just completed its unification and had only recently built its navy. For the last remaining piece, the last remaining candidate reached out.\n\nNor was the timing a coincidence: the attack came at a moment when a significant part of the Ottoman forces was occupied with the rebellion in Yemen.", "04596083"], "kisa": ["Libya was left to the end not because it was barren but because Italy was late. When Algeria passed to France in 1830, Tunisia again to France in 1881 and Egypt to Britain in 1882, Italy was still a newly united state.", "7a8ac91d"]},
"p76f-kapitulasyon-ilga":{"ad": ["If they could be abolished unilaterally, why were they not abolished earlier?", "dacd6bed"], "metin": ["The first privileges did not arise as a sign of weakness. The Ottomans granted the first capitulation to the Genoese in 1352, when they first crossed into Rumelia; the earliest surviving text of an ahidname is dated 19 Jumada al-Awwal 789 (7 June 1387). It was a unilateral favour granted by a strong state in order to draw trade to its own ports.\n\nThe scope of the exemptions widened over time: exemption from customs and internal trade duties, the trial of foreigners by their own consuls, immunity from jizya and haraç, and a guarantee of free navigation at sea. While the state was strong, these were a choice; when it weakened, each became a chain.\n\nThe Tanzimat statesmen had diagnosed this clearly: it was believed that the first and fundamental step towards the recovery and independence of the state was to get rid of the capitulations. Attempts were also made — in 1867, when foreigners were granted the right to acquire real estate, the condition of being subject to Ottoman courts was introduced; in 1869 the nationality law sought to close off the path of gaining exemption by changing nationality; in 1890, when Germany agreed to their abolition, this attitude angered the other states.\n\nThey all ran into the same wall: the European powers absolutely refused any change on the matters of internal taxes and of having an interpreter present in court. So the answer is this — they were not abolished earlier because these rights were bound to bilateral treaties and the other side would not give them up; unilateral abolition would not yield results as long as an order that required sitting at the same table with those states remained standing.\n\nOn 9 September 1914 the Ottoman State announced by an imperial decree that it had annulled the privileges which the financial, economic, judicial and administrative capitulations gave to foreigners. The justification was presented as neutrality in wartime conditions. The capitulatory states protested against the unilateral and arbitrary abolition of their treaty rights — that is, the abolition was applied in practice but not recognised in law. Recognition came nine years later with the Treaty of Lausanne of 24 July 1923; this treaty obliged the allied states to accept the abolition of the capitulations.", "dabe6110"], "kisa": ["The idea of abolition was born long before 1914 and was tried at least three times; each time the obstacle was not Ottoman will but the refusal of the states that held the privileges. What changed in 1914 was not the will but the balance.", "26013803"]},
"p76f-karadeniz-baskini-sebep":{"ad": ["The Black Sea Raid: was it a strategic move, or a way of shutting the door?", "1b948c8d"], "metin": ["The bombardment of the ports of Sevastopol and Novorossiysk on 29 October 1914 by the navy under the command of Admiral Souchon caused Russia to declare war on the Ottoman State on 2 November 1914. The declarations of the other Entente powers followed within days.\n\nIt is difficult to defend the military logic of the operation. There was no superiority of force in the Black Sea that would neutralise the Russian navy; the bombardment of the ports did not open the way for a landing, a siege or a land operation. What was obtained was not a defensible military position but a political result.\n\nFor this reason the operation is generally read not as a military but as a political move: to fulfil the requirements of the secret alliance signed three months earlier, to neutralise by a fait accompli the wing of the cabinet that wished to remain neutral, and to put entry into the war beyond debate. The widespread view that the decision was taken while being concealed from the cabinet as a whole also supports this reading; however, this is not a point on which there is full agreement, and it calls for a separate study based on the internal correspondence of the period.\n\nThe most honest answer to the question 'Did the Ottomans know what they were doing?' is in two parts: those who ordered the operation most probably knew and wanted its result — war; the rest of the state, however, was not prepared for this result. A four-year war that would be fought on five separate fronts began after a two-day naval operation.", "7521e7ce"], "kisa": ["The bombardment of Russian ports on 29 October 1914 brought no military gain; what it brought was an irreversible declaration of war — and that was most probably what was intended.", "19d9e91c"]},
"p76f-karadeniz-baskini-zarar":{"ad": ["How much damage was done in the raid, and where was the Russian Black Sea Fleet at that moment?", "be9cf341"], "metin": ["The targets of the operation are on record: on 29 October 1914 the Ottoman navy bombarded the ports of Sevastopol and Novorossiysk on Russia's Black Sea coast.\n\nAn account of the damage inflicted — the number of ships sunk, the installations destroyed, the loss of life — is not found in the source used. This does not mean that there was no damage; it only means that the figures that would answer this question must be sought in a separate source. It is right to show the gap rather than to invent figures.\n\nThe same applies to the position and response of the Russian Black Sea Fleet at the time of the operation: this source does not record where the fleet was that day or how it responded.\n\nThe only result that can be measured is political, and it is large: the raid caused Russia to declare war on the Ottoman State on 2 November 1914. In other words, the balance sheet of the operation is kept not in tonnage but in the number of fronts opened.", "0e0a7716"], "kisa": ["The targets are known — Sevastopol and Novorossiysk — but this source contains no account of the damage inflicted; the measurable result of the operation is not material damage but the declaration of war.", "1a5e70b8"]},
"p76f-libya-direnis":{"ad": ["The cities fell, the province did not: what happened in Tripoli, Benghazi, Derna and Tobruk?", "7d6976c0"], "metin": ["The Italian navy seized the coast in the very first weeks of the war: Tobruk was occupied on 8 October, the city of Tripoli on 9 October, Derna on 16 October and Benghazi on 21 October 1911. Anyone looking at the map would have thought the war was over; it was not.\n\nThe Ottoman State could not send a regular army to the region. It did not have naval superiority; a significant part of its forces was busy suppressing the rebellion in Yemen; and, most important, Britain and France blocked the passage of soldiers and supplies through Egypt and Tunisia. In other words, Libya was besieged not geographically but politically.\n\nThe course chosen in response was for volunteer officers to infiltrate the region in disguise and organise the local forces. Enver Bey reached the region on 1 December 1911; Mustafa Kemal served on the eastern front, in the Derna area. The main body of fighters, however, was supplied by the Sanusi order: the tribes were mobilised by the call to jihad of Shaykh Ahmad al-Sharif, while Sulayman al-Baruni played a similar role on the Tripoli side.\n\nThe result can be measured: the Italians were hemmed in on the coast, and even in 1915 they could control only the coastal settlements. What ended the war was not a military collapse in Libya but a new war that broke out in the Balkans; by the treaty signed on 18 October 1912 in the town of Ouchy, Tripoli and Benghazi were left to Italy with a proviso of autonomy.", "317f854d"], "kisa": ["Four ports were lost in thirteen days; even so, the Italians remained on the coastal strip until the end of the war, while the interior stayed in the hands of Ottoman officers and the Sanusi organisation.", "35be0ca3"]},
"p76f-londra-antlasmasi":{"ad": ["The Treaty of London: Rumelia lost with a single signature", "9e591a83"], "metin": ["A single article of the Treaty of London of 30 May 1913, which ended the First Balkan War, redrew the entire map: the Midye-Enez line was accepted as the Ottoman-Bulgarian frontier. This is a line joining Midye on the Black Sea to Enez on the Aegean and passing just west of Istanbul.\n\nThe division was made as follows: Edirne and Thrace were left to Bulgaria, Salonica and southern Macedonia to Greece, northern Macedonia to Serbia, and Silistre to Romania.\n\nTwo features of this treaty set it apart from the other territorial losses in Ottoman history. The first is its speed: the lost lands slipped away not over centuries but within eight months. The second is its nature: these were not distant colonies but the geography in which the state had been founded, the core regions that supplied its army and its administrative cadre.\n\nThe treaty was short-lived. When the allies, dissatisfied with the division, fell out with one another, a second war broke out that same summer, and the Ottoman State recovered part of the territory east of this line that it had consented to give up — including Edirne.", "f6e466c4"], "kisa": ["On 30 May 1913 the Midye-Enez line was accepted as the frontier; the land of Rumelia, held for five hundred years, was divided among four states.", "7d863ac4"]},
"p76f-mahmud-sevket-pasa":{"ad": ["Who was Mahmud Şevket Pasha, and who killed him?", "02731736"], "metin": ["He was born in Baghdad in 1856 and grew up there; for this reason he was known by the nickname 'the Arab'. In 1870 he began his military education in Istanbul, and in 1878 he graduated from the War Academy. He served as assistant to the Prussian Marshal von der Goltz, who was charged with reorganising the Ottoman army; he was thus one of the closest Ottoman counterparts of the German military school.\n\nIn 1908 he was at the head of the Third Army. After the 31 March Incident he directed and commanded the Action Army that marched on Istanbul; the deposition of Abdulhamid II and the accession of Mehmed V were the result of this operation. From 18 May 1909 he established tight control as inspector of three armies. On 25 January 1910 he became minister of war. After the Balkan War, following the overthrow of the government in a coup by the Unionists, he was appointed grand vizier on 23 January 1913.\n\nThe assassination took place five months after he took office: on 11 June 1913, after finishing his work at the Ministry of War, Mahmud Şevket Pasha set out for the grand vizierate; he came under armed attack near Çarşıkapı and was killed.\n\nThe question 'Is it known who killed him?' has two layers. Those who pulled the trigger are known: the attack was carried out by Topal Tevfik and his companions, who were caught within a short time and executed. As for the political will behind it, the source gives a clear verdict: there is no doubt that the Committee of Union and Progress, which wanted to be rid of the pasha, played a role in the assassination.\n\nThe outcome ran counter to the apparent aim of the assassination: Said Halim Pasha became grand vizier in his place, and the committee tightened its hold on power even further.", "cf9fdfd0"], "kisa": ["A soldier born in Baghdad and trained in the German school; head of the army that deposed a sultan, then minister of war and grand vizier. Those who pulled the trigger were caught, while the real responsibility points to a different address.", "ab146a85"]},
"p76f-onikiada-hikaye":{"ad": ["The Dodecanese: which islands, how were they taken, and why were they not given back?", "e426c09d"], "metin": ["The source lists the names of the islands one by one: Patmos (Batnoz), Lipsi (Lipso), Leros (Leryoz), Kalymnos (Kilimli), Kos (İstanköy), Astypalaia (İstanpulya), Nisyros (İncirli), Tilos (İlyaki), Symi (Sömbeki), Karpathos (Kerpe), Halki (Herki) and Kasos (Kaşot). Rhodes is mentioned together with this group, whose name is the 'Dodecanese' ('Twelve Islands'), but it is an island separate from the group itself.\n\nRhodes and the Dodecanese, which had been held by the Knights of Saint John since 1309, were conquered in 1522 by Sultan Suleiman the Lawgiver. The value of the islands lay not in agriculture but in their position: the defence of the Anatolian coast ran along this line.\n\nIn 1912 Italy, on the grounds of protecting its interests in Tripoli and Benghazi, occupied sixteen islands between 28 April and 20 May 1912. The Treaty of Ouchy of October 1912 made Italy's evacuation of the islands conditional on the withdrawal of Ottoman troops from Tripoli. The evacuation never took place: the Italians did not leave the islands, claiming that the Ottoman troops had not withdrawn from Tripoli. The condition turned into the justification for not evacuating.\n\nThe legal outcome came in the last two steps: the Treaty of Lausanne of 24 July 1923 left the islands to Italy without any change; under the Paris Peace Treaty with Italy, signed on 10 February 1947, Rhodes, the Dodecanese and Kastellorizo (Meis) were transferred to Greece.\n\nTwo questions remain open. The population ratios of the islands in the Ottoman period are not given as figures in this entry — it is recorded only that a Turkish population remained on Rhodes and Kos into later periods. The widespread claim that the islands were offered to Turkey during the Second World War does not appear in this source either; that claim should not be taken as true unless it is based on a separate source.", "3ef0495d"], "kisa": ["The islands, which joined the Ottoman realm under Suleiman the Lawgiver in 1522, were occupied within three weeks in 1912; Italy did not evacuate them, saying that 'the Ottoman troops did not withdraw from Tripoli', and the islands never returned.", "2c9dddfb"]},
"p76f-osmanli-alman-ittifak":{"ad": ["Why Germany? The Ottomans' failure to form an alliance with the Entente powers", "7c826187"], "metin": ["On 2 August 1914 a secret treaty of alliance was signed in Istanbul between the Ottoman State and Germany. The date is no accident: it is immediately after Germany's declaration of war on Russia.\n\nThe initial intention of the Ottoman government was neutrality. What upset this intention was not so much the signature itself as the de facto situation that followed: two German warships fleeing the British navy were let into the Dardanelles on 10 August 1914. The law of neutrality required that these ships be seized; instead, they were presented as having been purchased, joined the Ottoman navy under the names Yavuz and Midilli, and remained under the command of the German admiral Souchon.\n\nThe question 'Why was no alliance formed with Britain or France?' has been in the habit of seeking the answer in the Ottomans' preference; yet an alliance has two sides. For a Britain and a France that were in the same bloc as Russia, a binding alliance with the Ottomans directly conflicted with Russia's expectations regarding the Straits and eastern Anatolia. The sources record no concluded treaty of alliance with the Entente powers in this period.\n\nThe secrecy of the alliance is also noteworthy: the treaty was hidden even from the cabinet and was known to only a few people. In other words, the document that prepared the entry into the war had been concealed from the majority of the government that was to implement it.", "e624d801"], "kisa": ["The secret treaty signed in Istanbul on 2 August 1914 bound to war, within three months, a state that had hoped to remain neutral.", "03a25100"]},
"p76f-sisam-ozerklik":{"ad": ["Why was Samos a separate principality? The elected assembly of an island two kilometres off the Anatolian coast", "76980ec0"], "metin": ["After the Greek War of Independence, Samos remained neither an island whose rebellion had been fully suppressed nor a territory left to the new Greek state; a solution in between was found. On 10 December 1832 the island's autonomy was recognised and it was made a vassal principality.\n\nThis was not a status that existed only on paper. From 1834 the island was governed by an Orthodox governor appointed by Istanbul; alongside him was a local assembly, which had thirty-seven members by 1850 and later rose to forty. In other words, the island's internal administration was in the hands of an elected local body, while the centre retained the ties of tax and external representation. This arrangement of governor and assembly lasted until 1913.\n\nThe island's location both explains this privilege and makes it precarious: an island a few kilometres from the Anatolian coast lies both within range of the navy and within the network of population and trade of the opposite shore. Autonomy was a way of holding on to it without losing it.\n\nThe arrangement fell apart with the Balkan Wars: at the end of the Balkan Wars the island of Samos united with Greece (11 November 1912).\n\nThis entry does not fully answer the question of 'which other islands in the Aegean had a different status'; Chios and Lesbos are mentioned as large islands, but no autonomous principality like that of Samos is described for them. The status of the Dodecanese, on the other hand, diverged by an entirely different route, with the Italian occupation of 1912.", "97e1d40d"], "kisa": ["Samos was made a vassal principality on 10 December 1832; for eighty years it was governed by an Orthodox governor appointed by Istanbul together with a local assembly, and on 11 November 1912 it united with Greece.", "8266be52"]},
"p76g-avusturya-bosna-ilgisi":{"baslik": ["Why did Austria-Hungary want Bosnia —and why did it hold it for thirty years under the name of “occupation”?", "738b2c56"], "metin": ["The beginning was a peasant rebellion. According to TDV, the unsatisfactory land conditions in Bosnia in the second half of the nineteenth century gave momentum to a series of peasant uprisings; the rebellion that broke out in 1875 took on a political character and led to the intervention of the great powers. In other words, the Bosnian question came to the table not as a plan of conquest but as an internal crisis.\n\nWhat was given at BERLIN was not a property. TDV's wording is striking: at the Congress of Berlin, Bosnia and Herzegovina were given into the “protection” of Austria-Hungary. The occupation began on 29 July 1878 and was completed on 20 October 1878; the Muslims of Bosnia resisted. But the legal owner of the land did not change: “The rights of the Ottoman State in Bosnia-Herzegovina, which was given to Austro-Hungarian administration in 1878, officially continued until the definitive annexation in 1908.”\n\nWhat this corresponds to on the MAP. This distinction is not a legal nicety; it is a matter that goes straight to the map. For thirty years two separate things were true in Bosnia at the same time: the land was Ottoman, the control Austrian. A map that shows only one of these erases a reality of thirty years. For this reason, in this atlas Bosnia between 1878 and 1908 is drawn with the Ottoman base colour, overlaid with a hatched layer denoting Austrian occupation; after 1908 the base colour passes to Austria. Two different representations, two different legal situations.\n\nWHY occupation and not annexation — and why annexation thirty years later. Part of the answer lies in Vienna's own internal structure. TDV says that “because of” Austria-Hungary's “dual constitution, the status of Bosnia-Herzegovina was uncertain”: both before and after the annexation, the region was under a dual control exercised by the empire's Minister of Finance; at its head was a military governor, and throughout the whole period the administration was conducted in an extremely bureaucratic manner, with police power. In other words, Bosnia could be made part of neither Austria nor Hungary; it was attached to a ministry common to the two. In 1882 a civilian official was appointed to supervise the civil services. The region was divided into six “okrugs”: Banja Luka, Bihać, Mostar, Sarajevo, Travnik and Tuzla.\n\nAfter the ANNEXATION. In 1906 the judicial administration was separated; after the annexation, in 1910, a constitution was introduced with the establishment of an assembly (the “sabor”). In this assembly, made up of seventy-two deputies and twenty-three appointed members, some of the members were religious representatives — on the Muslim side, the reis-ul-ulema, the head of the endowment administration and three muftis.\n\nThe LONG-term legacy. Austrian rule also brought with it the age of nationalism. According to TDV, in this period the Orthodox began to present themselves as Serbs and the Catholics as Croats; the Muslim people, for their part, despite the efforts of the administration, did not much adopt the name “Bosniak” and continued to use the term “Muslim”.", "b1a30c97"], "kisa": ["At Berlin, Bosnia-Herzegovina was given to Austria-Hungary, but not as property: the administration went to Austria-Hungary, while in law the land remained Ottoman. This strange intermediate status lasted thirty years, and Vienna could not reconcile it even with its own internal constitution.", "b7173950"], "bag": ["The card answers the question “Why did Austria want it?” not through a reading of intentions but through the status itself: that what was given was administration and not property, and that Vienna could not fit this into its own dual constitution. This framework comes from TDV's expressions “protection”, “uncertain status” and “dual control of the Minister of Finance”. Strategic motives (the road to Salonica, the encirclement of Serbia) are not mentioned in the sources that were read and therefore do not appear in the card.", "147143a4"]},
"p76g-bagimsizlik-1908-osmanli-tepkisi":{"baslik": ["Bulgaria proclaimed its independence in 1908 — what did the Ottoman State do?", "ebc1ad98"], "metin": ["The timing was no coincidence. In the summer of 1908 the Second Constitutional Era had been proclaimed in Istanbul, and the state was occupied with its own internal transformation. Through this window, two neighbours unilaterally broke a thirty-year legal order within the same week:\n• 5 October 1908 — Bulgaria proclaimed its independence, and Prince Ferdinand took the title of “tsar”.\n• 7 October 1908 — it was announced that Bosnia-Herzegovina was officially Austro-Hungarian territory.\n\nWhat was the OTTOMANS' legal ground? In both cases what the Ottomans held was not actual power but a right. The Principality of Bulgaria had been a principality tributary to the Ottomans since 1878. As for Bosnia-Herzegovina, in TDV's words, “the rights of the Ottoman State had officially continued until the definitive annexation in 1908” — that is, Austria had been administering the territory since 1878, but in law it belonged to the Ottomans. In both cases, 1908 erased this right on paper.\n\nThe OUTCOME was settled at the negotiating table. The matter on the Bulgarian side was closed not by a treaty but by a protocol signed in Istanbul: the Istanbul protocol of 1909. From the Ottoman point of view, the real gain of this protocol was not territory but the status of those left behind. According to TDV, religious life and the pious endowments in Bulgaria were thereafter regulated by three documents: the Istanbul protocol of 1909, the protocol of 1913 that amended it, and the regulation of 1919. The fifth and seventh articles of the 1909 protocol defined the powers of the muftis over the endowments; the sixth article of the 1913 protocol specified that the account books of the endowments would be kept in Turkish.\n\nWhy is THIS important? When a state loses territory, what remains behind is sometimes not a border but a body of community law. The 1909 protocol does exactly this: where the Ottoman State could not recover Bulgaria, it tried to guarantee the endowments, the offices of mufti and the language of the registers of the Muslim population remaining there. In the same period the wave of migration was growing: according to TDV, the wave of migration from the Balkan countries grew still further in 1908–1909 and reached its highest level in 1913 at the end of the Balkan War; after the war 115,883 people applied for resettlement.\n\n⇒ So it is not correct that “there was no reaction at all”; but the reaction took the form not of a campaign or a border correction but of a legal bargain.", "a4eef8cd"], "kisa": ["The Principality, which for thirty years had been tributary to the Ottomans on paper, unilaterally ended that relationship on 5 October 1908. Two days later Austria-Hungary annexed Bosnia-Herzegovina. The two blows fitted into the same week, and both were closed at the negotiating table with a protocol.", "93780436"], "bag": ["The card treats the two events of 1908 together, because both erased the same legal mechanism — the Ottoman right that survived on paper — within the same week. The side of the 1909 protocol concerning endowments and the offices of mufti comes from TDV's section on pious endowments; that section describes the protocol not as a territorial matter but as a matter of community law, and the framework of the card was built on this.", "5df511cb"]},
"p76g-balkan-savasi-bulgar-hafizasi":{"baslik": ["The Balkan Wars in the Bulgarian narrative: an army that reached Çatalca, surrounded on four sides ten weeks later", "69f32142"], "metin": ["First, the greatest success. TDV's description is clear: in the First Balkan War, “the Bulgarians in particular achieved important successes in this war and gained control of the whole of Thrace as far as Çatalca.” The war had begun on 8 October 1912 when Montenegro entered Albania and the sanjak of Novi Pazar; Serbia, Greece and Romania also took their place at Bulgaria's side. Edirne fell on 26 March 1913 after a siege of five and a half months. The Treaty of London, signed on 30 May 1913, contained provisions in favour of Bulgaria and its allies.\n\nTHEN the heaviest loss. TDV continues: a month after the treaty, when the allies could not agree on the interpretation of its articles and the division of the captured territories, the Second Balkan War broke out among them. “Serbia, Greece, Romania and Montenegro, surrounding Bulgaria on four sides, inflicted a great defeat on this country; the Ottoman State, taking advantage of the situation, succeeded in recovering Edirne.” On 10 August 1913 Bulgaria signed the Treaty of Bucharest: Southern Dobruja was left to Romania, and Macedonia was renounced.\n\nIts place in the BULGARIAN narrative. In Bulgarian historiography the defeat of 1913 is called the “first national catastrophe” (Parva natsionalna katastrofa), while the defeat in the First World War and the Treaty of Neuilly of 1919 are the “second national catastrophe”. The balance sheet of Neuilly is set out in TDV: the whole of Western Thrace was lost, the Aegean coast was left to Greece, the Bucharest provision on Southern Dobruja was confirmed, and Bulgaria was condemned to pay reparations of 2.25 billion gold francs over thirty-seven years, 70,825 head of livestock and 250,000 tonnes of hard coal. The link between the two “catastrophe” narratives is this: in order to recover what it had lost in 1913, Bulgaria entered the war in 1915 on the side of Germany and the Ottoman State, and this second attempt ended in a greater loss.\n\nThe internal consequence of the WAR. Defeat did not change borders alone. According to TDV, the defeat in the First World War, the soldiers' mutiny over the rout suffered in the final year and their attempt to call to account those who had taken the state into the war, and the migration into the country of Bulgarians from the lost territories led to political instability and economic hardship. On 29 September 1918 Tsar Ferdinand was forced to ask for an armistice and to abdicate in favour of his son Boris.\n\nThe view from the OTHER side. On the Ottoman side, the balance sheet of the same two years is migration: according to TDV, the wave of migration reached its highest level in 1913 at the end of the Balkan War, and after the war 115,883 people applied for resettlement. The year that one side calls a “catastrophe” is, for the other side, the name of a population movement.\n\nONE border, one treaty. The Ottoman-Bulgarian border was drawn by the Treaty of Istanbul of 29 September 1913; Edirne, Kırklareli and Dimetoka were confirmed as Ottoman. On the other hand, places in the Black Sea and Maritsa (Meriç) sectors such as Ahtapolu, Mustafapaşa, Malko Tırnova and Rezve remained with Bulgaria. In other words, the same treaty recorded, with the same signature, both a recovery and a relinquishment.", "cf5021fc"], "kisa": ["In 1912 the Bulgarian army gained control of the whole of Thrace and reached Istanbul's last line of defence. In 1913 the same army fell out with its allies and was defeated by four states surrounding it on four sides. In the Bulgarian historical narrative these two have names: the “first” and “second” national catastrophes.", "572b0ab4"], "bag": ["The card approaches the question of the “retreat as far as Çatalca” from two directions: the line that marks a retreat on the Ottoman side is, on the Bulgarian side, the farthest point the army reached. The designation “first/second national catastrophe” does not appear in TDV; it is given as a common term of Bulgarian historiography and is marked separately in the source note. All the figures and dates taken from TDV are presented together with their source.", "06fbb90e"]},
"p76g-bulgaristan-kurtulus-anlatisi":{"baslik": ["How does Bulgaria tell of 1878 in its own historical narrative: “Liberation” or “partition”?", "270a4afd"], "metin": ["Two treaties, two maps. The Russo-Ottoman War of 1877–1878 was first ended by the Treaty of San Stefano (Yeşilköy) (3 March 1878), and then the great powers rewrote it with the Treaty of Berlin (13 July 1878). San Stefano envisaged a single, large Bulgaria stretching from the Danube to the Aegean. Berlin divided it into three:\n• Between the Danube and the Balkan Mountains, a Principality of Bulgaria that was tributary (autonomous) to the Ottomans. The centres listed by TDV: Sofia, Niğbolu, Sistova, Rusçuk, Silistre, Varna, Shumen (Şumnu), Lofça, Tırnova.\n• South of the Balkan Mountains, a province of Eastern Rumelia with a separate status. The sanjak centres: Filibe, İslimye, Eski Zağra, Tatarpazarcığı, Burgaz, Hasköy.\n• Macedonia and Western Thrace remained with the Ottomans.\n\nThe name for this in the BULGARIAN narrative. Bulgarian historiography commemorates 3 March as the “Liberation” (Osvobozhdenie); today it is also Bulgaria's national holiday. Berlin, by contrast, appears in the same narrative as an “injustice” and a “dismemberment”: the liberation was left half-finished. For this reason, the unchanging goal of Bulgarian foreign policy after 1878 was to return to the map of San Stefano. In the Bulgarian narrative, two steps are each counted as a “completion”:\n• 1885 — The Principality annexed the province of Eastern Rumelia. Thus two of the pieces that Berlin had separated were united.\n• 5 October 1908 — independence was proclaimed; Prince Ferdinand took the title of “tsar”, and the tributary relationship ended in law.\n\nTwo faces of the SAME event. Within a narrative of liberation there is always also a migration, and this side is weak in the Bulgarian national narrative. The example of Köstendil (Kyustendil) can be measured: according to TDV, during and after the war of 1877–1878, “four-fifths” of the Turks in the town and the region emigrated “never to return”; the vacated places were filled with Bulgarians coming from the mountain villages. While in 1873 the same town was a place where Turks made up about half the population, in the first years of Bulgarian rule only 581 of a population of 10,698 were Turks. According to official Bulgarian statistics, between 1893 and 1902, 72,524 Muslim Turks migrated to Ottoman lands; the wave grew in 1908–1909 and reached its highest level in 1913.\n\nA question for the READER. The same year, the same city, two narratives: in one, a people founding a state; in the other, a people being displaced. Can a historical narrative, while telling of its own liberation, also put the other side's migration on the page? The answer to this question does not vary by country — it varies according to the narrative itself.", "f15d4921"], "kisa": ["In the Bulgarian national calendar, 3 March is a day of liberation. But within the same narrative there is also a loss: the great Bulgaria drawn at San Stefano was divided into three at Berlin four months later. This duality — liberation and incomplete liberation — also explains the foreign policy of the next forty years.", "7da5835e"], "bag": ["This card does not pass judgement; it places two narratives side by side. The duality of “liberation + incomplete liberation” in the Bulgarian narrative can be read directly from TDV's description of Berlin and from the steps of 1885 and 1908; the side concerning migration comes from the sections on Köstendil and on population in the same encyclopaedia. Combining the two is this card's choice, not a single-sentence verdict of either source.", "04851598"]},
"p76i-avrupa-devletler-sistemi-1856":{"ad": ["What was the 'European states system', and what did it mean for the Ottomans to be admitted to it in 1856?", "467dcdc6"], "metin": ["How the order was born. A Europe turned upside down by the Napoleonic Wars was to be restored to its 'old order' through the Congress of Vienna of 1814–1815. In the structure that emerged, the great powers (düvel-i muazzama in Ottoman documents) discussed the continent's problems together at congresses and conferences and did not allow any single state to gain territory on its own in a way that would upset the balance. In historiography this order is called the 'Concert of Europe'. The first face of the order was conservative: the Holy Alliance, formed in September 1815 by the Russian, Austrian and Prussian monarchs, envisaged joint intervention against nationalist and liberal uprisings (TDV calls this the 'system of interventions / Metternich system'). With the accession of Britain and then France, the alliance became quadruple and then quintuple; within it, the 'liberal West' (Britain and France) and the 'conservative East' (Russia, Prussia and Austria) stood face to face.\n\nWhere at the table were the OTTOMANS? Until 1856 they were not at the table but on it. According to TDV's article on the Eastern Question, two of the three congresses and twelve of the twenty-four conferences held by the great powers between 1814 and 1912 concerned the Eastern Question. Because the price of rivalries in other regions could be paid out of Ottoman territory, the question had become the 'safety valve of the general peace of Europe'. TDV reads the attack at Navarino in 1827 within the same framework: international law was not applied to the Ottomans, whom the powers regarded as outside their own system.\n\nPARIS, articles 7 AND 8. In the seventh article of the treaty, the six signatory states declared that the Sublime Porte was 'admitted to participate in the advantages of the public law and system (concert) of Europe'. The eighth article describes how this was to work: if a disagreement endangering relations arose between the Sublime Porte and one of the signatories, the other states were to be given the opportunity to mediate before recourse was had to force. TDV sums up the result as follows: the Ottoman State was 'accepted on paper as an equal member of the European states system and was enabled to benefit from European law'.\n\nThe GAIN and the price were in the same article. The gain was clear: the Ottoman question was no longer one that a single state — that is, Russia, which had established its superiority since 1774 — could resolve on its own. Holland, the Oxford jurist who compiled the treaties, sees the common feature of Paris and Berlin precisely at this point: both give the right to resolve the Eastern Question not to a single state but to the partnership of the great powers. The price also arose from here: a common right meant a common right of intervention. The Reform Edict was mentioned in the text of the treaty; Lebanon in 1860 and Crete in 1866–1869 were the scenes in which Europe, in this common capacity, once again interfered in the internal affairs of the Ottomans.\n\nHow far was the PROMISE kept? According to TDV, it was soon seen that the promises and guarantees given did not work. When France was defeated by Prussia in 1870–1871, Russia announced that it would no longer observe the Black Sea clause of the treaty; the guarantee of territorial integrity did not prevent the great dismemberment in the war of 1877–1878. TDV illustrates what 'benefiting from European law' meant with the answer Bismarck gave at the Congress of Berlin (1878) to the Ottoman delegates who sought their rights on the basis of this law: 'That law is not for you.'", "584ce5ff"], "kisa": ["After 1815 the great powers of Europe set up a system of changing the map not one by one but together, at the negotiating table. For half a century the Ottomans were on the agenda of this table; at Paris they were seated at it for the first time — but, in TDV's words, 'on paper'.", "bbf9550e"]},
"p76i-cerkes-surgunu-dunya-yankisi":{"ad": ["How did the world receive the Circassian expulsion? States, diplomats and historians from 1864 to 2025", "ea9ab603"], "metin": ["First, what it was. The facts themselves (the occupation of 1861-1864, the one-month ultimatum, the figure of 1.2-1.5 million given for those expelled and those who emigrated) and the terminological debate over 'migration, expulsion, genocide' have been explained in separate cards. This card asks only one question: what did the world say?\n\nThe CIRCASSIANS' own appeal. The Circassians who assembled at Sochi on 13 June 1861 asked the Ottoman State, England and France for help. A Circassian petition published by Nazan Çiçek from the British archives ends with this sentence: if our rights are not taken into account and we are wiped off the face of the earth, we shall not give up seeking our rights even in the presence of the creator of the universe.\n\nENGLAND: Seeing but not intervening. Çiçek's study in the Journal of the Faculty of Political Science (SBF) of Ankara University (2009) traces the British attitude through documents. On 19 February 1864 Stevens, the consul at Trabzon, wrote to the Foreign Secretary, Lord John Russell, that three thousand Circassians had arrived in the city in three days, that hundreds of them were about to die of hunger and disease, and that typhus had also spread to the local population. The reports of Bulwer, the ambassador in Istanbul, referred to the Circassians as a 'brave and warlike race' with a vehemence out of keeping with diplomatic style. But London's reply to the request for a loan and ships, repeated by Grand Vizier Âli Pasha on 25 May 1864, was cold: the Treasury stated that there was no legitimate ground for sending government ships to the Black Sea, and that the Sublime Porte could charter British merchant ships with its own money. Çiçek's verdict: although some Russophobe diplomats and a group of intellectuals took up the Circassian cause and influenced public opinion to a certain degree, the British state consistently avoided open conflict with Russia; it thought of the Circassians rather as a strategic 'barrier' against a possible Russian advance towards Eastern Anatolia. The aid actually given remained at the level of the 'ship's biscuit' in the title of the article.\n\nRUSSIA: 'VOLUNTARY emigration'. When French and British diplomats suggested that Russia share in the Ottoman expenses, Prince Gorchakov's reply, according to Çiçek, was this: the emigration was not the result of compulsion but the Circassians' own wish; Russia had no obligation to pay compensation; moreover, the Circassians would increase the empire's Muslim population and supply the Ottoman army with 'fresh blood'. No academic source showing the official position of present-day Russia could be obtained.\n\nTHE OTTOMANS. Sultan Abdülaziz opened the door to the emigrants, and the Refugee Commission (Muhacirin Komisyonu) carried out their settlement; but according to Çiçek, the state lacked the financial means that its promise required, and this inadequacy was a reflection of the structural weakness that made the empire an object of the Eastern Question.\n\nThe FOLLOWING century: recognition resolutions. On 20 May 2011 Georgia became the first state to recognise the conquest of Circassia as genocide; on 9 January 2025 the Verkhovna Rada of Ukraine became the second. Matthew Kelbaugh's article on Caucasian studies (2025) reads both decisions as a strategic politics of memory: for Georgia, limiting Russia's influence in response to the regions lost in 2008; for Ukraine, taking up the causes of the North Caucasus in the midst of war.\n\nTHE HISTORIANS. The term itself is disputed: the TDV says that 'according to many researchers, it was the first ethnic cleansing operation in modern history', while Walter Richmond's monograph uses 'genocide' in its title. This card passes no verdict; it suggests that both the recognition resolutions and the objections to them be read together with their political context.", "a69dfc48"], "kisa": ["In 1864 British diplomats sent reports of the tragedy to London, but the British government gave neither ships nor money; Russia said that the emigration was 'voluntary'. A century and a half later two states recognised the event as genocide — according to historians, these recognitions too have their own politics.", "4221e8b7"]},
"p76i-girit-kibris-rum-mu-helen-mi":{"ad": ["When did Crete and Cyprus become 'Rum' (Greek Orthodox) — and does that tell us whom the islands belong to?", "b47dbaf5"], "metin": ["Crete: population. According to the TDV, Minoan culture lived on the island between 3000 and 1400 BC; from 1400 BC onward 'the invasion movements of the \"Greeks\"' began, with the Achaeans coming from the Peloponnese and then the Dorians. After Rome and Byzantium, the island was held by an Arab emirate between 827 and 961 (it was they who founded Candia). When Byzantium retook it in 961, some of the Muslims left, some were forced to change their religion, and 'the people of the island were completely Christianised'.\n\nCRETE: Sovereignty. In the Crusader partition of 1204 the island was sold to Venice for 100,000 pieces of silver. The TDV describes Venetian rule in terms of heavy taxes: more than twenty uprisings in the 150 years following the first revolt. The Ottomans took Chania in 1645, completed the conquest with the fall of Candia in 1669, and lightened the Venetian taxes. Evliya Çelebi counts some 200,000 inhabitants on the island. In the following period the Muslim population grew rapidly through conversion and transfers from Anatolia; but one detail stressed by the TDV complicates everything: shared life led to 'the spread of Greek among the Muslims', and some Cretan Muslims who migrated to Turkey did not know Turkish. In other words, on this island language did not coincide with religion.\n\nCYPRUS. The TDV's history of Cyprus begins in 395; the island's population in antiquity does not appear in the article consulted (see below). Until 1191 the island was a Byzantine province, and since the Council of Ephesus in 431 it had been governed ecclesiastically by an independent Orthodox archbishopric. In 1191 came the Crusaders, followed by the Lusignan dynasty (1192-1489) and Venice (1489-1571); the Latins placed the Orthodox Church under Catholic domination. After the conquest of 1570-1571 the Ottomans reopened the Orthodox archbishopric, which had been closed, and made the archbishop 'the spiritual leader and political representative of the native Rum people'. The figures compiled by the TDV show that the population fluctuated: 170-175,000 people after the conquest, most of them Orthodox Rum; nearly half Muslim in the first half of the 18th century; according to a count dated 1777, 37,000 Christians and 47,000 Muslims; in the first Ottoman general census, 14,983 Muslims out of a male population of 44,206.\n\nWhere did the claim of RIGHT come from? When Crete was not included in the Kingdom of Greece established by the three protecting powers in 1830, the island rose in revolt again; in 1866 a provisional government proclaimed the island's annexation to Greece. In Cyprus, after 1829, the idea of union with Greece, that is, Enosis, was idealised 'under the leadership of the Church'. In Europe, meanwhile, a sympathy that regarded the Greeks (Rum) of Greece as descendants of the ancient Hellenes (Philhellenism) gave this demand the support of public opinion.\n\nTWO separate things. The claim rests on the population majority and on 19th-century nationalism: 'most of the people are Rum, therefore the island should belong to Greece'. Historical sovereignty, however, is another chain: Byzantium, the Arab emirate, Venice, the Lusignans, the Ottomans; before 1830 there is no Greek state in any of them. Taking the two to be the same question is the mistake into which both sides of the debate fall. One irony is also worth recording: the authority of political representation held by the Orthodox Church of Cyprus, which spearheaded Enosis, had been given to it by the Ottoman administration that rescued it from the Latins.", "998e4521"], "kisa": ["Two separate questions are being confused: who lived on the islands (population, language, religion) and under whose sovereignty the islands were. The answer to the first has for centuries been largely the Orthodox Rum; the answer to the second was never 'Greece' before 1830.", "8c0f39da"]},
"p76i-hersek-isyani-sebepler-1875":{"ad": ["Why did Herzegovina rise in revolt in 1875? Land, taxes, foreign support, ideas, the strength of the state", "7f225ef6"], "metin": ["The event. In 1875 a group of Christian peasants from the district (kaza) of Nevesinje in the sanjak of Herzegovina rose in revolt, citing the heaviness of the taxes; the rebellion soon spread to Herzegovina and then to Bosnia. The possibilities put forward below have been tested against the sources one by one.\n\n1 · The land system and 'more oppression than in the CLASSICAL period'. The strongest evidence is here. In the classical period the timar system had prevented the emergence of a land-based aristocratic class; according to the study by Güran and Uzun in Belleten, over time the state-owned (miri) lands turned into estates (chiftliks), and a significant part of the land in Bosnia passed into the hands of a small number of Muslim landowners (eshab-ı alaka). According to calculations made for Herzegovina, only one third of the produce remained to the cultivator; 'as a result, the burden on the reaya had increased considerably'. The same study reports Halil İnalcık's view: the basic cause of the Christian peasant uprisings after the Tanzimat was the distortion in the tenure of miri land. The Yugoslav historian Djurdjev, who wrote the TDV article on Bosnia and Herzegovina, looks in the same direction: the law of 1859 could not solve the problem between the estate owner and the tenant, 'the far from satisfactory conditions' gave momentum to a series of peasant uprisings, and the revolt of 1875 'had also acquired a political character'. In other words, the oppression came less from the state itself than from the local land system that the state had allowed to take root.\n\n2 · Taxes and the tax farmer. The complaint of the peasants of Nevesinje was the heaviness of the taxes. Here there are two views of the same fact: Çakmak, in an article from Fırat University, writes that the peasants revolted 'using the heaviness of the taxes as a pretext'; Güran and Uzun, however, show with documents that the burden was real and structural. The choice between 'pretext' and 'cause' is the real crux of the debate.\n\n3 · Foreign support. The TDV article on Abdülaziz is categorical: 'Russia, Serbia and Montenegro were supporting the rebels.' The article on the War of '93 adds that after 1856 Russia increased its activity among the Balkan Slavs through a policy of Pan-Slavism and 'exploited' these revolts. The word 'exploited' is important: even the TDV does not say that Russia created the revolt, but that it made use of one that had already arisen.\n\n4 · The ideas of the French Revolution. According to the TDV article on the Eastern Question, the ideas of the Enlightenment and the Revolution, conveyed by groups in contact with the West, gave 'a new direction and force to the internal dynamics of the question'. However, the sources consulted contain no document showing a link between the peasant of Nevesinje and these ideas; this factor is put forward more for the urban and educated classes.\n\n5 · Did the state lack the strength to suppress it? Two pieces of evidence say yes. Financial: according to the TDV, there was a deficit of five million gold liras in the 1875 budget, no possibility of borrowing remained, and on 6 October 1875 half of the debt instalments were cut. Choice: Çakmak writes that at first, instead of suppressing the revolt by force, the state sent counsellors to exhort the rebels. The revolt, having gained time, expanded and opened the door to intervention by the European states.\n\nIN CONCLUSION. The sources do not allow it to be reduced to a single cause: the anger accumulated by the local land system explains the fire, while foreign support and financial collapse explain why the blaze could not be put out.", "76d17d3a"], "kisa": ["The sources mention all five of the five causes, but place the weight differently: a Yugoslav historian looks to the land system, the Ottoman-centred account to Russian, Serbian and Montenegrin support, and economic historians to the one third that reached the peasant's hands.", "49bb618c"]},
"p76i-imtiyaz-bag-mi-durak-mi-1868":{"ad": ["Did privileges bind these communities to the Ottomans, or were they stations on the way to separation?", "648ca2aa"], "metin": ["What 1868 gave. The Cretan Regulation, proclaimed after Âli Pasha's visit to the island, established a joint administration: the district governors (mutasarrıf) of Sfakia and Lasithi were to be Christians and those of Rethymno and Candia Muslims; every Muslim administrator was to have a Christian assistant and every Christian a Muslim one; and the administrative councils were to consist of three Muslim and three Christian members. Taxes were remitted for a few years, and the Christians were exempted from the military exemption tax (bedel-i askerî). TDV's verdict is brief: 'Despite this, the unrest on the island did not end, and Greece ... not content with these privileges either, began to arm.' What followed is known: the Pact of Halepa of 1878, the restriction of 1889, the appointment by the great powers of a Greek prince as commissioner to the island in 1898, and the proclamation of union by the Cretan assembly in 1908.\n\nCOUNTER-examples. Privilege did not end in separation everywhere. In the Mutasarrifate of Lebanon, established in 1861, the region, according to TDV, experienced 'one of the most tranquil periods in its history'; it was not separatists who ended the status but the Ottomans, who unilaterally abolished the protocol during the First World War (July 1915). Samos, for its part, became a vassal principality in 1832 under pressure from the great powers and joined Greece eighty years later, during the Balkan War (1912). The card's comparison (not the verdict of any one source): where separation accelerated, there was across the border a state that spoke the same language and was waiting for their lands.\n\nCould they have remained ATTACHED — those who argued so. The Tanzimat statesmen believed exactly this. TDV gives the main idea of Ottomanism in Âli Pasha's words, 'the fusing of the subjects with one another in matters other than religion and sect', and in Fuad Pasha's words, 'a unity based on the equality of all'. The opposition Young Ottomans also wanted the same goal by a different route: Namık Kemal thought that constitutional government would strengthen Ottoman identity and put an end to separatism.\n\nWHY did it not work? TDV points to resistance from two sides. The non-Muslims saw equality as a policy to which the Turks resorted in order to save the state; they were anxious about losing their exemption from military service, and the men of religion about losing their positions within the community. While the Reform Edict was being discussed, when it was announced that the jizya would be abolished and non-Muslims would be taken into the army, 'the greatest reaction to the decision came from the non-Muslims'. The Muslims, for their part, said that they had lost the position of 'ruling nation' won with the blood of their forefathers; the Young Ottomans regarded the edict not as one of 'brotherhood' but as an 'edict of privileges'.\n\nFOREIGN instigation or internal dynamics? The source gives both answers. TDV's article on the Eastern Question counts Russian policy as the 'main factor'; it writes that in Crete Greece 'incited' the people 'to revolt' and that in 1866 volunteers came from everywhere. But the same article adds that those segments of these peoples who were in contact with the West gave, through the ideas of the Enlightenment and the French Revolution, 'a new direction and strength to the internal dynamics of the question', and that this put even the policy of the great powers in difficulty. The demands of the Cretan Christians in 1866 (tax reduction, schools, a harbour, an agricultural bank) came from within. The Bulgarian example shows that internal separation was not directed against the Ottomans alone: in 1870 the Bulgarians first broke away from the Greek Patriarchate of the Phanar and established their own church.", "dfec3da6"], "kisa": ["Three similar privileged statuses had three different endings: Lebanon remained tranquil for fifty-four years, Samos broke away in eighty years and Crete in forty. The difference is sought not in the privilege itself but in whether or not there was a state waiting across the border.", "2f895832"]},
"p76i-kimdir-murad-v":{"ad": ["Who was Murad V — the heir apparent of the Young Ottomans, sultan for ninety-three days", "779b67a0"], "metin": ["His upbringing. He was born on 22 September 1840; his original name was Mehmed, his father was Abdülmecid and his mother Şevkiefser Kadınefendi. As a prince he received a good education: he studied Turkish, Arabic, French, Ottoman history and the sciences, and learned the piano and Western music from two Italian teachers. The TDV records that he took an interest in painting, music and architecture and produced some works.\n\nAS HEIR APPARENT. When his uncle Abdülaziz came to the throne in 1861, he became heir apparent; he accompanied him on his journeys to Egypt (1863) and Europe (1867). He spent most of his time at his farmhouse at Kurbağalıdere in Kadıköy. In these years he established contact with the Young Ottomans, who advocated constitutional government; he exchanged ideas with Şinasi, Namık Kemal and Ziya Pasha 'on constitutional government, democracy and liberty', and communicated with Midhat Pasha through Ziya Pasha and his doctor, Kapoleon Efendi. On 23 October 1872 he joined the Masonic organisation. For the opposition, his was the name of the 'liberal' sultan to be put in Abdülaziz's place.\n\nHis ACCESSION. In view of the Balkan crisis that began with the Herzegovina revolt of 1875, the Bulgarian uprising and the killing of two consuls in Salonica, the opposition group decided to depose Abdülaziz. Murad Efendi had obtained from his banker part of the money distributed during the demonstrations of the softas (theological students) in May 1876 and had sent it to Midhat Pasha. The coup was brought forward to the night of 29-30 May; the heir apparent, unaware of this, feared that he was going to be arrested and did not want to leave his apartments. In a storm and downpour, he was taken to the quay in a carriage that soldiers with fixed bayonets stopped several times, from there by boat to Sirkeci, and from there to the Serasker's Gate (the War Ministry), where he received the oath of allegiance. The TDV writes that although the official communiqué spoke of 'unanimous agreement', the affair was 'a coup arranged by a few people'.\n\nHis REIGN. He made Ziya Pasha his chief secretary and asked for the exiled Namık Kemal and his companions to be recalled; the request was not carried out. Of the four men who put him on the throne, only Midhat Pasha sincerely advocated constitutional government; the serasker (commander-in-chief) Hüseyin Avni Pasha controlled the entrance to the palace and made access to the sultan conditional on his own permission. Ziya Pasha was dismissed within a few days.\n\nThe breakdown of his NERVES. According to the TDV, his ailment had been known 'for a long time', but the shocks came one on top of another: the fright of the night of his accession, the discovery of his uncle Abdülaziz dead on 4 June, and Çerkez Hasan's attack on the council of ministers on the night of 15-16 June. The course of the illness, the fatwa of deposition and the doctors' reports are not repeated in this card; how contested the TDV leaves the label 'mad' is explained in a separate card. He was deposed on 31 August 1876; he had remained on the throne for ninety-three days.\n\nTwenty-eight years AT ÇIRAĞAN. There were three attempts to put him back on the throne: in November 1876, a committee of four who tried to enter the palace disguised as women; in April 1877, the Cleanti Scalieri-Aziz Bey committee; and on 20 May 1878, the Çırağan Incident, which ended with the killing of Ali Suavi. After that his guard was tightened. He died of diabetes on 29 August 1904 and was buried beside his mother in the Cedid Havatin Tomb at the Yenicami (New Mosque).\n\nHis CHARACTER. The TDV says that he had 'a gentle nature', but does not hide his weaknesses either: his excessively Europeanised manners and his Freemasonry were not welcomed by his siblings; it is recorded that 'his greatest weakness was his excessive fondness for drink and his extravagance'. Part of the money plundered from the palace on the day of his accession was given to his banker for his debt, which reached 1 million.", "274bb950"], "kisa": ["He was an heir apparent who played the piano, read French and discussed constitutional government with Namık Kemal and Şinasi; the opposition staged a coup to put him on the throne. The night of the coup and the three weeks that followed broke his nerves; he spent twenty-eight years at Çırağan.", "e4d8e05c"]},
"p76i-suriye-lubnan-topluluklari-1860":{"ad": ["Who lived in Syria and Lebanon in 1860? A map of the communities", "39c98858"], "metin": ["First, a key. Most of the Christian churches in the region split according to the sides they took on the nature of Jesus at the councils of 431 (Ephesus) and 451 (Chalcedon); those that later attached themselves to Rome received a separate name. TDV's article on the Chaldeans puts this in a single sentence: of the same people, those who adopted the Monophysite belief are called Syriacs, those who accepted the views of Nestorius are called Nestorians or Assyrians, and those who had been Nestorians but became Catholic and attached themselves to Rome are called Chaldeans.\n\nSUNNI and Shiite Muslims. The majority in the cities and plains. According to TDV's article on Lebanon, the Sunnis are concentrated on the coast, and the Shiites in the Biqaʿ Valley and in the south.\n\nThe DRUZE. They emerged in the early eleventh century from an Ismaili circle that believed in the divinity of the Fatimid caliph al-Hakim bi-Amr Allah; when persecuted in Egypt, they withdrew to Wadi al-Taym in Lebanon. They are a closed community that accepts no one from outside; TDV writes that the Druze, who practised taqiyya (dissimulation) for self-protection, had 'shown themselves as Muslims until the year 1860'. Their homelands are the central part of Mount Lebanon and Jabal al-Druze.\n\nThe MARONITES. A Syriac community born in the monastery of Saint Maron, who had lived on the banks of the Orontes River; when they accepted the decrees of Chalcedon, they came into conflict with the neighbouring churches and withdrew to the mountains of Lebanon. The contact they established with the Crusaders brought them closer to Rome; TDV counts it, among the Eastern churches, as 'the only church that has had a Catholic character from the beginning'. In 1860 thousands of Maronites were killed.\n\nGREEK Orthodox (Rum). Members of the Orthodox church inherited from Byzantium; according to TDV's article on Lebanon, they were mainly in the coastal cities. The name 'Rum' denotes not a lineage but means 'Roman'.\n\nSYRIAC Orthodox (Süryân-i Kadîm, Jacobites). The Monophysite branch of the Patriarchate of Antioch, one of the oldest communities of Christianity. When some of them attached themselves to Rome in 1782, those who remained used the name 'Kadîm' ('Ancient') to distinguish themselves.\n\nNESTORIANS (Assyrians). The Church of the East, of the East Syriac tradition, which followed the view of Nestorius (428), the Marash-born patriarch of Constantinople; the tribes of the Hakkâri and Urmia mountains call themselves Assyrians.\n\nCHALDEANS. Nestorians who attached themselves to Rome; the name is of Western origin. The region of Nineveh and Mosul.\n\nEASTERN Catholics. The 'Uniate' churches that kept their own rites and recognised the authority of the pope: the Melkites of the Byzantine rite (Greek Catholics), Syriac Catholics and Armenian Catholics.\n\nARMENIANS (Gregorian). According to TDV's article on the millet, the Ottoman Armenians were not a single millet: they were organised as two millets, Gregorian and Catholic; after the recognition of Protestantism in 1850 they were divided into three millets.\n\nPROTESTANTS. They were a new element. The American Board, founded in Boston in 1810, and the Presbyterian mission organisation opened schools and churches in the Middle East and Anatolia; according to TDV's article on the millet, after 1850 some Armenians too went over to the Protestant community.\n\nNUSAYRIS. The community of Latakia and the Jabal Ansariyya mountains; after 1516 it lived under Ottoman administration under the supervision of local shaykhs, and its revolts in 1854 were suppressed.\n\nYAZIDIS. A community that arose around Adi b. Musafir, whose tomb is at Lalish north of Mosul; they live in Sinjar, Mosul and Hakkâri. According to TDV, for a period the Ottomans considered them neither Muslims nor People of the Book, and the community withdrew into itself in the mountainous regions; in 1872, upon their payment of the military exemption tax, the state recognised them as People of the Book.", "f7a56f17"], "kisa": ["On the same mountain and in the same city lived a dozen communities set apart from one another by details of church, sect and language. Most of their names indicate not an ethnic identity but the side taken in a theological dispute of the fifth century.", "bc66b7e5"]},
"p76i-vatan-yahut-silistre-1873":{"ad": ["Why did a night at the theatre end in exile? 'Vatan yahut Silistre' and Namık Kemal", "1b3e1489"], "metin": ["What the play was about. According to the TDV, this first play by Namık Kemal revolves around the love between a young man and a young girl who, following her beloved who has gone to the front as a volunteer in the Turco-Russian war of 1853, dons a soldier's uniform and joins the defence of Silistra; it stages the self-sacrifice shown by soldiers for the homeland in a besieged redoubt. The figure of the heroine comes from a childhood memory of the author: the adventure, spread by word of mouth, of a girl who in the years of the Crimean War disguised herself as a soldier and joined the army in pursuit of her beloved. As in all of Namık Kemal's plays, the heroes sacrifice their personal happiness for the sake of their duty to society and the homeland.\n\nWhat happened that night. Before the performance, the newspaper İbret had explained the concept in a leading article entitled 'Vatan' (Homeland), and the day before it had also published an article describing the role of the theatre in the life of society. On the night of Tuesday, 1 April, the cries of 'Long live the homeland! Long live Kemal! Long live the homeland's Kemal!' that rose in the Gedikpaşa Theatre spilled into the street; at midnight a crowd carrying lanterns crossed the Unkapanı Bridge, marched to the newspaper's office in Galatasaray and, not finding the author there, left him a message of gratitude. The demonstrations were repeated at the second performance on 3 April.\n\nThe STATE'S answer. On 5 April the government shut down İbret for good. On 6 April Namık Kemal, Ebüzziya Tevfik, Nuri, Bereketzade İsmail Hakkı and Ahmed Midhat were arrested at the theatre; without being brought before a court, they were sent into exile with confinement in a fortress on the very day of Sultan Abdülaziz's decree of 9 April, which accused them all of 'engaging in harmful publications and activities'. Namık Kemal's lot was Famagusta; the exile lasted thirty-eight months.\n\nWhy were the PEOPLE roused? The source consulted does not offer a direct sentence of cause; but it gives the background. Until then 'vatan' had been a word describing the place where a person was born; according to the TDV, Namık Kemal gave it 'a new and different content' and became 'the first poet to turn the concepts of homeland, nation and liberty into a matter of passion and to impart them to society in the most effective way'. What the audience applauded was the dissident author off the stage as much as the soldier on it; the fact that the government's reaction was directed at the author and his newspaper rather than at the play itself shows this.\n\nWHAT did he stand for? He joined the society that was secretly organised in 1865 to establish a constitutional government and later took the name Young Ottomans; in the newspaper Hürriyet, which they published in Europe, the issue he dwelt on most was the establishment of constitutional government. The TDV lists his main causes as follows: constitutional government, social and individual liberty, especially freedom of thought, the reform of institutions and keeping pace with European civilisation. The Western author he embraced most was Montesquieu.\n\nHis CHARACTER. He was born in Tekirdağ in 1840, lost his mother early, and spent his childhood in the places to which his grandfather, an official, was posted. The TDV records, in his replies to the government's warnings, 'his courageous character that did not give way in the face of pressure'. He turned his exile into a productive period: he wrote most of his theatrical works and the novel İntibah in Famagusta. He died on Chios on 2 December 1888; his body was transferred to Gallipoli, to the burial ground of the tomb of Süleyman Pasha.\n\nHis influence on A generation. According to the TDV, it has become a shared conviction that Turkish society became conscious of the concept of 'homeland' thanks to this play. His patriotic poems addressed readers 'almost alone and without rival' until Tevfik Fikret and Mehmet Emin; his political ideas had an important influence on the realisation of the Second Constitutional Era. Whereas in the 1890s it took courage to mention his name in the press, after 1908 his plays were staged one after another everywhere. The TDV views this second wave, too, with reserve: it notes that a literature of praise arose instead of scholarly study.", "82c7db0c"], "kisa": ["On the night of 1 April 1873 the audience at Gedikpaşa spilled into the street crying 'Long live the homeland! Long live Kemal!'; four days later the newspaper was shut down, and eight days later its writer was sent to Famagusta. The play was a love story, but its real hero was the concept of 'vatan', the homeland.", "95f0aaa7"]},
"p77a-18-mart-nusret-seyid":{"baslik": ["18 March 1915 — The minelayer Nusret and Corporal Seyid", "04afff6b"], "metin": ["On the morning of 18 March 1915 British and French battleships entered the Dardanelles. The aim was to silence the forts, clear the minefields and bring the fleet into the Sea of Marmara and from there to Istanbul. By evening three battleships had sunk, several had been put out of action, and the fleet had withdrawn. Three elements produced this result: the forts, the field batteries and the mines.\n\n■ HOW THE STRAIT WAS DEFENDED\nThe strait had been mined from August 1914 onward; by the end of December there were nine lines of mines, and the main lines began at the narrowest part of the strait, off Soğanlıdere-Dardanos. Concealed and mobile howitzer batteries protecting the lines had been placed along the shores. The idea was simple: big ships can pound the forts from a distance, but to clear the mines the minesweepers have to come close; and a small minesweeper that comes close is easily hit by the batteries. The shortage of mines was so great that mines which the Russians had laid in the Black Sea and which had drifted with the current were collected and brought to the Dardanelles.\n\n■ NUSRET'S 26 MINES\nNusret was a small minelayer. In Erenköy Bay, where the Entente ships manoeuvred, it laid 26 of the last mines in hand. What was critical was the place and the direction: the mines were laid not at right angles to the strait like the other lines, but parallel to the shore; for this reason it was not thought that there could be mines there. The sources differ on the date of the laying: the TDV article and Yaşar Semiz place it on the night of 17/18 March; Cemalettin Taşkıran, however, writes that it was done on the night of 7-8 March. What is certain is that these mines did their work on 18 March.\n\n■ THAT DAY\nThe French battleship Bouvet sank within a few minutes as it was returning from Erenköy Bay; at first it was thought to have been hit by a shell fired from the shore, but from the statements of the survivors it was understood that it had struck a mine. Irresistible also struck a mine; Ocean, which went to its aid, struck a mine in the same area, and the two ships sank one after the other. According to the TDV, the fleet lost 35 per cent of its strength; Bouvet, Océan and Irrésistible, along with two destroyers and seven minesweepers, were sunk, and seven battleships, including Gaulois and Inflexible, were put out of action. Cevad Pasha, commander of the Dardanelles fortified zone, who directed the defence, came to be called \"the hero of 18 March\".\n\n■ CORPORAL SEYID\nThe name of Corporal Seyid does not appear in the TDV articles on the Dardanelles; the academic source that relates the story is Taşkıran. According to his account, Corporal Seyid was one of the soldiers of the Rumeli Mecidiye Fort. Only one of the battery's four guns remained intact, and the hoist that carried the shell to the breech of the gun had also broken down. Corporal Seyid lifted the shell, weighing about 275 kilos, onto his back and loaded it into the gun, and according to Taşkıran he hit Ocean with the third shot. When it was later wanted to photograph him for the War Magazine (Harp Mecmuası), he could not lift the shell again; the photograph known today was taken with a wooden mock-up. The same Taşkıran also writes that Ocean finally struck a mine; Semiz, for his part, attributes the sinking of Ocean to the mine alone. In other words, Corporal Seyid's hit was the damaging of a ship; what sank the ship was the mine.\n\n■ CONCLUSION\n18 March showed that the strait could not be forced by the navy alone. The Entente powers thereupon decided on a landing, and on 25 April 1915 they put troops ashore on the Gallipoli peninsula. The naval victory was the beginning of a land war that would last eight months.", "10f33030"]},
"p77a-canakkale-amac-bedel-onem":{"baslik": ["Gallipoli (Çanakkale) — aim, cost and significance", "e73ac9e2"], "metin": ["Gallipoli (Çanakkale) is one of the most debated operations of the First World War, because it was a move that, had it succeeded, would have changed the map of the war.\n\n■ THE AIM\nAccording to the TDV, with this operation Britain and France were aiming at several things at once: to establish a direct link with Russia and supply it with arms and materiel, to remove the Ottoman pressure on the Suez Canal and the route to India, to take the German and Austrian armies in Central Europe from the rear, and to draw the Balkan states that had not yet entered the war to their side. The advocate of the idea was Winston Churchill, the British First Lord of the Admiralty: forcing the strait with the navy, taking Istanbul and putting the Ottomans out of the war.\n\n■ THE PHASES\nThe first bombardment was carried out on 3 November 1914, before there was yet an official declaration of war. After 19 February 1915 the outer forts were pounded. On 18 March the fleet tried to pass through the strait and was defeated. On 25 April landings were made at Arıburnu and Seddülbahir; Mustafa Kemal's throwing the 57th Regiment into a counter-attack without waiting for orders confined the landing at Arıburnu to a narrow coastal strip. The new landing made at Suvla at the beginning of August was stopped at Anafartalar and Chunuk Bair (Conkbayırı). In November 1915 Lord Kitchener, after seeing the front, decided on evacuation; the Entente forces withdrew from Anafartalar and Arıburnu on the night of 19-20 December, and from Seddülbahir on 8-9 January 1916.\n\n■ THE COST\nAccording to the figures given by the TDV, the Entente powers sent nearly half a million soldiers to Gallipoli (410,000 British, 79,000 French); the total losses of the British forces alone were 213,980. On the Ottoman side about 700,000 people took part in the fighting. Because the units were used piecemeal, it is difficult to establish the losses, and the figures range between 190,000 and 350,000; the number of dead given by the General Staff on the basis of official records is 213,882. The TDV particularly stresses that the Turkish nation lost a great many educated people in this battle.\n\n■ ITS SIGNIFICANCE FOR WORLD HISTORY\nAccording to the TDV, the failure at Gallipoli changed the course of the war and caused it to be prolonged, paved the way for the collapse of Tsarist Russia and led to a change of government in Britain. As long as the strait remained closed, the Western allies could not get aid to Russia by the shortest route; the TDV article on the First World War writes that the failure of the Dardanelles campaign also had a share in the outbreak of the Russian Revolution of 1917. In October 1915 Bulgaria entered the war on the side of the Central Powers; with the defeat of Serbia, a land link was established between Germany and the Ottoman State.\n\n■ ITS MEANING FOR THE OTTOMANS\nAccording to the TDV, this success was a great military victory that wiped away the sense of humiliation left over from the Balkan War, and it was a new light for the Islamic world and for oppressed nations. It gave rise to a broad literature, from Mehmed Âkif's poem \"To the Martyrs of Çanakkale\" to a folk song.", "233d5041"]},
"p77a-dogu-anadolu-isgal-kurtulus":{"baslik": ["The occupation and liberation of Eastern Anatolia (1918)", "40e5d88e"], "metin": ["On 24 February 1918 the Russians withdrew from Trebizond. This withdrawal was the result not of a military defeat but of the Russian army's disintegration from within.\n\n■ WHY THE RUSSIANS WITHDREW\nRevolution broke out in Russia in 1917; in November 1917 the Bolsheviks seized power. According to Nurcan Yavuz, the greatest effect of the revolution was seen on the Caucasus front: soldiers stopped obeying orders, and officers began withdrawing with their units to Kars and Tiflis to await orders. In December 1917 Russia moved to an armistice with Germany and its allies; on the Caucasus front too, the Armistice of Erzincan was signed on 18 December 1917 between Vehib Pasha, commander of the Third Army, and the Russian Caucasus Army. Under the terms of the armistice, the Russians were to leave the lands they had occupied. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam's article on Erzurum also records this: the revolution that broke out in Russia in 1917 was the beginning of liberation for Erzurum.\n\n■ THE SHADOW OF THE WITHDRAWAL\nAs the Russians withdrew, they left their positions and weapons to the Armenian units in the region. According to the TDV, in and around Erzurum Armenian bands embarked on a great massacre of the Muslim population; the city was burned and destroyed and was reduced to a deserted village. In Trebizond, Pontic nationalist activity gathered pace after the Russians withdrew. For this reason, in most places liberation came not through the armistice but through the advance of the Turkish army.\n\n■ TABLE: OCCUPATION → LIBERATION (from the TDV's articles on the cities)\n· Erzurum: 16 February 1916 → 12 March 1918 (units of Kâzım Karabekir)\n· Muş: 18 February 1916 → a brief liberation in July-August 1916, reoccupation, final liberation on 1 May 1917\n· Bitlis: 1 March 1916 → 8 August 1916\n· Rize: 8 March 1916 → after two years of occupation (day not found)\n· Trebizond: 18 April 1916 → 24 February 1918\n· Bayburt: 1916 (according to Türkman, 16 July) → day not found\n· Gümüşhane: 19 July 1916 → 28 February 1918\n· Erzincan: 24 July 1916 → 26 February 1918\n· Van: under Russian occupation from 1915 onwards (the TDV gives no day) → 2 April 1918\n· Kars (Russian since 1878): ceded to the Ottomans by Brest-Litovsk on 3 March 1918, taken on 25 April 1918\n\n■ READING NOTE\nTwo waves can be seen in the table. Muş and Bitlis were retaken in 1916-1917 by the offensives of the Second Army, before the Russian army had yet broken up. The Black Sea, Erzurum and Erzincan line, by contrast, was liberated only in February-March 1918, after the Russian army had disintegrated. On Muş the sources differ day by day: the TDV writes that it was liberated on 26 July 1916, while Türkman writes that the Russians left the city on 6 August.", "80865e31"]},
"p77a-dogu-anadolu-kurtulus-sebebi":{"baslik": ["What liberated Eastern Anatolia in 1918?", "7eacd932"], "metin": ["In February-March 1918 Trebizond, Erzincan, Gümüşhane and Erzurum were liberated within a few weeks; Van and Kars followed in April. This speed is surprising when compared with the collapse suffered on the same front in 1916. The explanation is a chain of three links, and none of the links is sufficient on its own.\n\n■ 1: THE DISINTEGRATION OF THE RUSSIAN ARMY\nAccording to Nurcan Yavuz, in February 1917 the Turkish army was in a very critical condition: the manpower of the Third and Second Armies was low, they were short of food, and their clothing, equipment and ammunition were inadequate; they were not fit for a major offensive. The outbreak of revolution in Russia prevented Turkey from suffering a new defeat at Russia's hands. The greatest effect of the revolution was on the Caucasus front: discipline broke down and the soldiers began to withdraw. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam's article on the First World War also writes that success in Eastern Anatolia came after the Russian armies abandoned the war as a result of the revolution.\n\n■ 2: THE ARMISTICE — NECESSARY BUT INSUFFICIENT\nThe Armistice of Erzincan, signed on 18 December 1917, provided that the Russians would leave the lands they had occupied. However, according to Yavuz's finding, in practice the armistice served only to let the Russian army withdraw at its ease. As the Russians withdrew, they left their positions and weapons to the Armenian units being organised in the region; in the areas thus vacated, attacks on the Muslim population began. Yavuz's conclusion is clear: the occupied provinces could be liberated not in the peaceful atmosphere of the armistice but through hot war, that is, through the success of the Turkish advance.\n\n■ 3: THE TURKISH ADVANCE\nAccording to the TDV, Kâzım Karabekir, appointed commander of the 1st Caucasian Corps on 31 December 1917, recovered Erzincan, Erzurum and Hasankale despite the severe winter conditions. Erzincan was liberated on 26 February and Erzurum on 12 March 1918. The Treaty of Brest-Litovsk, signed on 3 March 1918, ceded to the Ottomans Kars, Ardahan and Batum, which had been in Russian hands since 1878; but since the region this time remained in the hands of Armenian and Georgian forces, the campaign continued, and Kars was taken on 25 April 1918.\n\n■ VERDICT\nThe principal cause is the disintegration of the Russian army as a result of the revolution: without it, the worn-down Turkish army of February 1917 could not have been expected to retake this front. But the disintegration did not by itself liberate any territory; the Armenian units that filled the vacuum were removed from the region only by the offensive of the Turkish army. The balance of strength and supply that had brought down the front in 1916 was reversed in 1918 with the collapse of the other side.", "e2b93d07"], "kisa": ["What freed the cities of Eastern Anatolia from Russian occupation: the Russian revolution, the armistice or the Turkish army?", "f8c565d4"]},
"p77a-dogu-cephesi-basat-faktor":{"baslik": ["The predominant cause of the losses on the eastern front in 1916", "84cefb31"], "metin": ["The year 1916 was a year of losses on the Caucasus front. The Russian Caucasus Army took Erzurum, Muş and Bitlis in February, Rize in March, Trabzon in April, and Bayburt and Erzincan in July. The sources list several causes; the question is which of them determined the others.\n\n■ THE CANDIDATES\n① The disaster of Sarikamish (December 1914-January 1915): the trained core of the Third Army melted away in a winter operation. ② The shifting of units to other fronts: in October 1915 the experienced 18th Corps was sent to Iraq. ③ Logistics: while the Russian army was supplied by rail, the Turkish army's nearest station was 600 kilometres to the rear. ④ The strengthening of the Russian side: in the autumn of 1915 a new command and abundant materiel arrived at the front. ⑤ Armenian armed activity in the region and Armenians joining the Russian army; the TDV writes that Erzurum was taken with the help of the Armenians.\n\n■ THE PREDOMINANT ONE: THE EXHAUSTION OF MANPOWER AND SUPPLY\nThese five causes do not carry the same weight. The fifth cause was a local facilitating factor; it speeded up the advance of the Russian army, but cannot by itself explain the collapse of the front. The fourth is the strengthening of the opposing side, and it becomes decisive only when combined with your own weakness. The remaining three causes come down to the same thing: the inability to keep on the Eastern front enough men and materiel to cope with the Russian army. Sarikamish destroyed those men; the transfer to Iraq reduced what was left; and the 600-kilometre ox-cart road made it impossible to bring up replacements in time. When the Second Army was sent east after Gallipoli, it had to march 1,000 kilometres from Ulukışla to the front, and was still on the road when the Russian offensive came.\n\n■ THE VERDICT OF THE TDV\nThe TDV article on Sarikamish sums up this chain in a single sentence: the Sarikamish adventure should take its place in history not for its high casualty figures but as an indication of the Ottoman State's military strength being squandered for the sake of its allies' war aims. In other words, the Caucasus front was run on the basis not of the force its own defence required, but of the task that the German war plan expected of it.\n\n■ THE EXAMPLE OF BITLIS\nThe story of Bitlis confirms the same conclusion. The town came under Russian occupation on 1 March 1916 and was retaken on 8 August 1916; what made the recapture possible was the offensive of the Second Army, which had at last reached the east. According to Türkman, from 3 August 1916 onward offensives continued on the Bitlis and Muş fronts in the Second Army's sector, and by 9 August the northern, eastern and western ridges of Bitlis had been taken. When the force reached the front, territory could be regained; as long as it did not, territory was lost. The High Command, too, required that the advance be made only to the extent that supply, and above all provisioning, permitted.", "bf858bba"], "kisa": ["In 1916 Erzurum, Muş, Bitlis, Trabzon and Erzincan fell one after another. What was the predominant factor?", "d5954102"]},
"p77a-erzincan-1916-oncesi":{"baslik": ["The fall of Erzincan (1916) — the places that fell before it", "9f77ba83"], "metin": ["Erzincan was the rear base of the Third Army. According to the TDV, the town was occupied by Russian forces on 24 July 1916 and liberated on 26 February 1918. The fall of Erzincan was not a sudden raid but the final link in the unravelling, one by one, of all the defence lines around it.\n\n■ FEBRUARY-MARCH 1916: BEHIND ERZURUM\nErzurum fell on 16 February 1916. According to Türkman, Russian units took Aşkale during the pursuit and broke off the pursuit there; by the end of February Erzurum, İspir, Aşkale, Muş and Bitlis had passed into Russian hands. The exact day for Aşkale was not found in the sources. Instead of Aşkale, the Third Army held on along the Cibice line, which was better suited to defence. Mamahatun (Tercan) was occupied on 15 March 1916.\n\n■ JULY 1916: THE SUMMER OFFENSIVE\nYudenich's summer offensive enveloped Erzincan from three sides. According to Türkman's chronology, the Russians took Bayburt on 16 July; according to the TDV, Gümüşhane was occupied on 19 July 1916; according to Türkman, Kelkit fell on the evening of 22 July. In the same days the front east of Erzincan was also breached. In short, all the roads leading to Erzincan — from Bayburt to the north-east, from Kelkit to the north-west, from Mamahatun to the east — were in Russian hands a few days before the occupation.\n\n■ RIZE\nRize had fallen much earlier, on 8 March 1916, as part of the coastal operation. It has no direct connection with the fall of Erzincan; it is the coastal arm of the same Russian advance.\n\n■ THE SEQUENCE\nErzurum (16 February) → Aşkale (end of February, day not found) → Rize (8 March) → Mamahatun (15 March) → Trabzon (18 April) → Bayburt (16 July) → Gümüşhane (19 July) → Kelkit (22 July) → Erzincan (24 July 1916). A Turkish report related by Türkman describes the situation in a single sentence: no position remained in the rear that could close off the Erzincan plain, and Erzincan was the life of the army.", "00f8a3a2"]},
"p77a-erzurum-1916-nicin-dustu":{"baslik": ["Why did Erzurum fall in 1916?", "e9d8d3e3"], "metin": ["Erzurum was the lock of Eastern Anatolia. In the war of 1877-78 the Russians had been unable to take the city by fighting and received it only under the armistice. On 16 February 1916, however, the Russian Caucasus Army entered the city. Behind this difference lies a chain stretching from 1914 to 1916.\n\n■ 1: THE GAP LEFT BY SARIKAMISH\nThe Sarikamish Operation of December 1914-January 1915 destroyed the core of the Third Army. According to the most reliable estimates given by the TDV, there were about 30,000 dead, some 20,000 prisoners and thousands of sick and wounded. Hasan İzzet Pasha, commander of the Third Army, who opposed the offensive, had proposed before the operation that the army be withdrawn without loss to the Höyükler line in front of Erzurum. As Sayim Türkman stresses, two years later the army was forced to withdraw to precisely that line; but this time it had no force that had been husbanded.\n\n■ 2: THE BEST UNITS GO TO ANOTHER FRONT\nIn October 1915, while a Russian offensive was expected at any moment, Enver Pasha sent the 18th Corps of Halil (Kut) Bey (the 51st and 52nd Divisions) to Iraq. According to what Türkman relates, Mahmud Kâmil Pasha, commander of the Third Army, saw the units off with the words \"Halil, you will go to Iraq, you will save Baghdad, but Erzurum will fall.\" These divisions were experienced units that had fought the Russians. In Iraq they contributed to the victory of Kut; but the army defending Erzurum lost its most important strength.\n\n■ 3: ROADS AND RAILWAYS\nAccording to the TDV, the Russian army met all its needs by the Tiflis-Gyumri (Gümrü)-Kars-Sarikamish railway. On the Turkish side the nearest railway station, Ulukışla, was 600 kilometres behind the front; supplies were transported by ox-cart and pack animals, and even women and children carried provisions to the front on their backs. The Second Army, sent after Gallipoli, also had to march about 1,000 kilometres from Ulukışla to the Muş-Van region, and was still on the road when the Russian offensive came.\n\n■ 4: THE CHANGE ON THE RUSSIAN SIDE\nIn September 1915 the Tsar appointed his uncle, Grand Duke Nicholas, commander-in-chief of the Caucasus Front; large quantities of arms and equipment were sent to the front. The actual commander of the army was General Yudenich, who had saved the situation at Sarikamish. In January 1916, in the middle of winter, he went on the offensive; after the battles of Azap and Köprüköy the Turkish units withdrew to the Erzurum forts.\n\n■ THE FALL OF THE CITY\nAccording to Türkman, when the Çobandede fort fell on 15 February, the integrity of the defence line was broken; on 16 February 1916, as the Russian Corps was entering through the Kars gate, the Turkish units were leaving through the Trabzon gate. The TDV writes that the Russians entered the city with the help of the Armenians and that the Muslim population migrated with the army into the interior of Anatolia. The Turkish casualties reported by Türkman are 22,000 at Azap, 9,000 in the battle of Erzurum and 4,500 during the retreat; the Russian losses were around 40,000, but Russian manpower could bear this loss.", "a4445a45"]},
"p77a-filistin-bozgunu-1918-nablus-sam":{"baslik": ["The collapse of the Palestine front (1918) — from Nablus to Aleppo", "badd3136"], "metin": ["After Jerusalem was lost in December 1917, the Palestine front held on south of Nablus, along a line stretching from the coast to the Jordan (Sharia) valley. The final British offensive, which began on 19 September 1918, brought this front down within a few days; Damascus was lost on 1 October and Aleppo at the end of October, and a month later the Armistice of Mudros was signed.\n\n■ THE ORDER OF THE FRONT\nAccording to Cemal Kemal, in February 1918 Marshal Liman von Sanders, the former commander at the Dardanelles, had been appointed commander of the Yıldırım Army Group in place of Falkenhayn. From west to east, the front was held by the Eighth Army of Cevad Pasha, the Seventh Army of Mustafa Kemal Pasha and the Fourth Army of Mersinli Cemal Pasha. Mustafa Kemal had returned to the head of the Seventh Army in August 1918; the commander of one of its corps was Colonel İsmet (İnönü), and that of the other was Ali Fuat Pasha (Cebesoy).\n\n■ THE PITCHED BATTLE OF NABLUS\nOn 19 September 1918 Allenby, in coordination with Emir Faisal's Arab army, attacked the positions of the three armies south of Nablus. According to Cemal Kemal, the Eighth Army of Cevad Pasha and the corps of Colonel Refet (Bele) attached to it were annihilated; the two corps of the Seventh Army suffered heavy losses, and İsmet Bey's corps was surrounded. Liman von Sanders was unable to establish a new defensive line in Palestine; the aim now was to escape annihilation, cross to the east of the Jordan and hold Syria.\n\n■ EAST OF THE JORDAN, TO DARAA\nMustafa Kemal had his army face west and brought it across to the east of the Jordan valley, and from there directed it over the Ajloun mountains to the Daraa-Muzeirib line. İsmet Bey's surrounded corps also crossed the Jordan and joined its army at Ajloun. On 25 September the British took Amman; in the south, about 5,000 soldiers of the 2nd Corps, attached to the Fourth Army, who were under pressure from Arab forces, were unable to withdraw and surrendered. On the night of 25-26 September the three army commanders met at Daraa. Since the greater part of the Eighth Army had been annihilated or taken prisoner, Liman von Sanders, considering that Cevad Pasha and Refet Bey were no longer needed, informed them that they should go to Istanbul. Daraa was taken by Arab forces on the night of 27-28 September.\n\n■ DAMASCUS\nMustafa Kemal arrived in Damascus on the evening of 29 September. Liman von Sanders ordered that Damascus be defended under the command of Mersinli Cemal Pasha; but public order in the city had broken down, and Faisal's men were inciting the population to revolt and plundering the supplies. Qadam, the largest station on the Hejaz line, was burning, and the railway to Aleppo had been cut. According to Liman von Sanders, the units coming from the south could be saved only if they passed through Damascus without halting anywhere. On the night of 30 September the exhausted units were led through Damascus and out to the north. According to the TDV, the Turkish forces left Damascus on 1 October 1918, and the city was occupied by Anglo-Arab forces at the beginning of October.\n\n■ ALEPPO AND THE END\nThe retreat continued as far as Aleppo. According to Cemal Kemal, on 26 October 1918 Mustafa Kemal succeeded in halting the British and Arab offensive north of Aleppo; four days later, on 30 October, the Armistice of Mudros was signed. Earlier, on 29 September, Bulgaria had signed an armistice and the overland link between the Ottomans and Germany had been cut; according to the TDV, it was also this that forced the Ottoman government to ask for an armistice.", "e8a33f12"]},
"p77a-kudus-teslimi-1917":{"baslik": ["The surrender of Jerusalem (1917)", "bd3ff725"], "metin": ["Jerusalem remained under Ottoman administration for 400 years. On the morning of 9 December 1917 that administration came to an end with a letter and a key in the hands of a mayor.\n\n■ WHY THERE WAS NO FIGHT\nThe Gaza-Beersheba front had been broken at the beginning of November, and Jaffa had fallen on 16 November. According to İsmet Üzen, the Turks no longer had the strength to defend Jerusalem, and consequently the decision was taken to leave the city without defending it. The grounds for the decision are clearly written in the military documents: on 8 December 1917 the commander of the Seventh Army informed the commander of the 20th Corps that evacuation had been decided upon \"in order to protect a holy city from destruction by the enemy\". The commander of the Yildirim Army Group likewise reported to the High Command that the city had been evacuated on the night of 8/9 December without allowing its historical and religious sites to be destroyed. In other words, Jerusalem was not given up without a fight; a battle had been lost, and it was preferred not to play out the last act of a lost battle among the Dome of the Rock and the Church of the Holy Sepulchre.\n\n■ THE LETTER OF SURRENDER\nOn the morning of 9 December, before leaving the city early, İzzet Bey, the last mutasarrif (district governor) of Jerusalem, left the mayor a letter of surrender and the key of the city. According to the text published by Üzen, the letter stated that for two days howitzer shells had been falling on Jerusalem, held sacred by every nation, that the Ottoman government was withdrawing from the city solely so that the religious sites would not be harmed, and that it was leaving officials behind to protect places such as the Church of the Holy Sepulchre and the al-Aqsa Mosque; it was hoped that the British would act in the same way. The mayor set out towards the British lines with a few policemen; at 8.30 in the morning he came upon the British advance units and handed the letter and the key over to them.\n\n■ 11 DECEMBER: THE CEREMONY\nAllenby entered the city two days later, on 11 December 1917, with a ceremony prepared in advance, and on foot. The proclamation read at the ceremony, which had been prepared by the British government and sent to Allenby three weeks earlier, announced that all the holy places of the three religions would be protected. For this reason two dates are seen in the sources: the surrender on 9 December and the official entry on 11 December.\n\n■ THE REMARK \"THE CRUSADES ARE OVER\"\nThe TDV article on Palestine reports that Allenby entered the city and said that only now had the Crusades ended. Üzen, however, states that remarks of this kind — including those attributed to Allenby or to French generals in Damascus — rest on weak foundations and have so far not been proven. What is certain is that some books published in England after the war openly referred to Allenby's army as \"Crusaders\".\n\n■ CONCLUSION\nAccording to the TDV, the British occupation put an end to Muslim rule in Jerusalem of about 1,200 years, interrupted only by the Crusader occupation. Five weeks earlier, on 2 November 1917, the Balfour Declaration had been published; between 1917 and 1920 the city remained under British military rule, and then under mandate administration.", "caf76626"]},
"p77a-kut-1915-isgal-ve-1916-zafer":{"baslik": ["Kut al-Amara — the occupation of 1915 and the victory of 1916", "46dc1bf6"], "metin": ["In Turkish memory the name of Kut al-Amara is associated with a victory. For this reason it may be surprising that 26 September 1915 is the date of an occupation. Yet there are two separate events and both are true: first the town fell into British hands, then the British army was besieged in that town and forced to surrender.\n\n■ 26 SEPTEMBER 1915: THE BRITISH TAKE KUT\nThe British had occupied Basra at the very start of the war, in November 1914. In late September 1915 General Townshend marched north along the Tigris; the Ottoman force facing him was made up of Turks and Arabs and was under the command of Colonel Yusuf Nureddin Bey. The objective was Baghdad, and Kut al-Amara, on the way, fell into British hands on 26 September 1915. This date is an Ottoman defeat.\n\n■ THE TURNING POINT: SALMAN PAK\nTownshend continued his advance and on 22-26 November 1915 went on the offensive at Salman Pak (Ctesiphon), 30 kilometres from Baghdad. He was stopped here; the British, having suffered heavy losses, fell back to Kut. On 5 December 1915 the Ottoman forces besieged the town. The army that had taken the town was now trapped inside it.\n\n■ 29 APRIL 1916: THE VICTORY\nIn the siege, which lasted about five months, British attempts at relief from outside came to nothing. In the end Townshend surrendered, and on 29 April 1916 the Turkish forces under the command of Halil Pasha entered Kut and took the surrender of the British army of 13,309 men. This is the victory, and it is commemorated on a separate date.\n\n■ WHY THE TWO SHOULD BE REMEMBERED TOGETHER\nThe two dates are the key to each other. The British, who took Kut in September 1915, continued to advance towards Baghdad; when they were stopped at Salman Pak, the place to which they fell back was again Kut. In other words, the loss in September set the stage for the victory in April. A reader who looks only at the date of the victory cannot see how the British got there; one who looks only at the date of the occupation misses the outcome.\n\n■ THE LIMITS OF THE VICTORY\nThe TDV also notes that this military success could not prevent the revolt of Sharif Hussein, which began in the Hejaz in June 1916, and that in 1917 the balance turned again: in February 1917 Kut al-Amara, and on 11 March 1917 Baghdad, fell into British hands.", "99ebb4fa"], "kisa": ["Was Kut al-Amara a victory? Yes — but not in September 1915; it came seven months later, in April 1916.", "72b399da"]},
"p77a-kut-zaferi-nasil":{"baslik": ["How was the victory of Kut al-Amara won? (1916)", "11476cc1"], "metin": ["On 29 April 1916 a British army of 13,309 men surrendered at Kut al-Amara. This result was the product not of a single great battle but of a siege lasting five months.\n\n■ THE BRITISH PREDICAMENT 1: OVERREACHING\nIn the autumn of 1915 General Townshend marched from Basra along the Tigris towards Baghdad. In November 1915 he was stopped at Salman Pak, 30 kilometres short of Baghdad, and withdrew to Kut with heavy losses. The army that had set its sights on Baghdad was forced to take refuge in the town it had captured two months earlier.\n\n■ THE BRITISH PREDICAMENT 2: THE FAILURE OF RELIEF\nOn 5 December 1915 the Ottoman forces besieged the town. According to the TDV, the relief forces sent by the British from the direction of Basra could not break the siege: the four-day offensive launched at Felahiye on 5 April 1916 with new brigades brought from India was fruitless, and the last attempt on 21-22 April was also repulsed. The food in the stronghold ran out. The British tried to drop food into the town by aircraft, but most of the packages fell into the river; the aid did not reach its destination.\n\n■ WHAT WAS DONE RIGHT: HOLDING THE SIEGE, WINNING THE BATTLE OUTSIDE\nThe real success of the siege lay not in assaulting the town but in stopping those who came to relieve it. The Ottoman forces under the command of Halil Pasha, on the one hand, kept the army inside encircled and, on the other, several times threw back on the Felahiye line the relief forces coming from the south. As long as the army inside trusted in help from outside, it did not surrender; when that help did not come, it starved.\n\n■ THE BARGAINING\nOn 26 April 1916 the British commander, General Percy Lake, informed Townshend that he should begin surrender talks. According to the TDV, the British first offered to give up all their weapons and 1 million pounds sterling in return for being permitted to go to India; the Turkish side insisted on unconditional surrender. At the meeting between Halil Pasha and Townshend on 27 April the British raised the indemnity to 2 million pounds sterling; again it was not accepted. On 29 April the protocol was signed and the Turkish forces entered the town.\n\n■ CONCLUSION\nAccording to the TDV, this victory dealt a heavy blow to British plans to take Baghdad. However, in the weeks following the victory the revolt of Sharif Hussein began in the Hejaz; the British prepared anew in Iraq and took Kut al-Amara in February 1917 and Baghdad in March 1917. Kut determined the fate not of a front but of a campaign.", "191ee24f"]},
"p77a-trabzon-1916-sahil-sirasi":{"baslik": ["The occupation of Trabzon (1916) — the Russian advance along the coast", "9b1a2bf7"], "metin": ["Trabzon came under Russian occupation on 18 April 1916. The city was taken not by a surprise attack from the sea but by a land operation advancing along the coast from the east; the Russian navy supported this operation from offshore.\n\n■ THE COAST ROAD: FROM EAST TO WEST\nThe coastal column of the Russian Caucasus Army marched westward from the direction of Batum along the coast of Lazistan. According to Sayim Türkman, the Russians took Hopa in mid-January 1916 after fierce fighting. According to the TDV, Rize was first bombarded by the Russian navy, the government house became uninhabitable, and the town was occupied on 8 March 1916. Türkman writes that when the advance towards the interior began, the navy was no longer able to support the units on the coast, and the pursuit was halted to await reinforcements. The navy was a part of the coastal operation, but it was the land forces that took the towns.\n\n■ TRABZON\nAccording to the TDV, Russian units broke through the defence line at Karadere on 14 April 1916 and reached Yomra on 16 April; that same night the Turkish population of Trabzon evacuated the city. The Russians entered the city on 18 April and held it until they withdrew on 24 February 1918. Among the Russian operational objectives reported by Türkman, the seizure of Trabzon, an important supply base of the Turkish army, was explicitly included.\n\n■ BAYBURT: AFTER TRABZON\nBayburt lay inland, on the Trabzon-Erzurum supply route. Fevzi Pasha (Çakmak) established his headquarters here in March 1916, and the region was defended for months. According to Türkman, in Yudenich's summer offensive the Russians occupied Bayburt on 16 July 1916, that is, about three months after Trabzon. The TDV article on Bayburt says only that it was occupied in 1916 and gives no day.\n\n■ THE SEQUENCE\nHopa (mid-January 1916) → Rize (8 March 1916) → Trabzon (18 April 1916) → Bayburt (16 July 1916). Arhavi lies on the coast road between Hopa and Rize, but the day of its occupation was not found either in the TDV or in the academic source used. Bayburt, for its part, was still in Ottoman hands when Trabzon fell: in the spring of 1916 the front in the Eastern Black Sea region had shifted on the coast to the west of Trabzon, while inland it stood to the east of Bayburt.", "9befd4a3"]},
"p77a-ucuncu-gazze-cephe-yarilmasi":{"baslik": ["The Third Battle of Gaza (1917) — how was the front broken?", "d4b7363b"], "metin": ["In the spring of 1917 Gaza repulsed two great British assaults. In November 1917, however, the third assault broke the Palestine front, and forty days later Jerusalem fell. The difference lay in the lesson the British drew from their two defeats.\n\n■ TWO DEFENSIVE VICTORIES\nAccording to the General Staff data reported by İsmet Üzen, in the First Battle of Gaza on 26 March 1917 the British attacked with 25,000 rifles and 100 guns against 16,000 rifles and 74 guns, and in the Second Battle of Gaza on 19 April with 33,000 rifles and 150 guns against 20,000 rifles and 101 guns. In both they withdrew with heavy losses. The TDV article on Gaza also writes that of the three great clashes in the war, the Turks won the first two and the British the third.\n\n■ THE BRITISH LESSON: OVERWHELMING SUPERIORITY\nAfter the two failures the British commander in Egypt was replaced, and General Allenby arrived in June 1917. According to Erkilet, as reported by Üzen, the British, seeing that even the previous ratios had not been enough, increased their forces still further: on 31 October 1917 they attacked with at least 57,000 rifles against the Turks' 25,000, and with 270 guns against their 206. According to General Staff data, the British were more than twice as strong in infantry and more than eight times as strong in cavalry. The Arab forces of Emir Faisal were also on the British side.\n\n■ THE TIME THE OTTOMANS LOST\nIn July 1917 the Yildirim Army Group was formed under the command of the German Marshal Falkenhayn to retake Baghdad, lost in March 1917. Djemal Pasha, saying that a British attack on Gaza was expected in the autumn, asked for the force to be directed to Palestine; Enver Pasha said that there would be no going back on the Baghdad decision. According to Üzen, until September 1917 it was debated whether the Yildirim forces would go to Iraq or to Palestine; the time spent on these needless preparations became one of the important reasons for the loss of the Third Battle of Gaza.\n\n■ HOW THE FRONT WAS BROKEN\nThe front stretched from Gaza on the coast to Beersheba (Birüssebi) in the east, on the edge of the desert. Allenby's plan was not to attack Gaza head-on but to bring down the eastern end of the front: to take Gaza, Beersheba first had to be taken. On 31 October 1917 a British force of about 17,000 rifles and 180 guns attacked a defence of about 4,400 rifles and 28 guns under the command of Colonel İsmet (İnönü), and towards evening it entered the town. Gaza had been under bombardment for several days; on 1 November the ground assault began. Once the eastern flank had fallen, there was no longer any point in holding Gaza: the Turkish commander, after receiving approval, began evacuating the city from 4 November; the evacuation was completed on the night of 6/7 November, and on the morning of 7 November the British entered the empty city.\n\n■ AFTERMATH\nWith the fall of Beersheba and Gaza, the front had to be pulled back. The British pursuit operation, which began on 7 November, lasted about a month; Jaffa fell on 16 November and Jerusalem on 9 December. According to Üzen, in the fighting between 31 October and 9 December the Turkish losses were about 25,000 and the British losses 18,000.", "0aea8ee1"]},
"p77b-abdulhamid-filistin-herzl-tartismasi":{"baslik": ["Did Abdülhamid II really protect Palestine?", "feb49d1a"], "metin": ["One of the most frequently repeated judgements about Abdülhamid II is that he protected Palestine by rejecting the Zionists' money. The sources confirm the two halves of this judgement separately, and also show the tension between them.\n\n■ WHAT THE OFFER WAS\nZionism became an organised political movement with Theodor Herzl's book The Jewish State, published in 1896, and the first World Zionist Congress, which met in Basel on 29 August 1897. The Basel Programme stated the goal openly: a Jewish home in Palestine secured by public law. Since Palestine was Ottoman territory, the Zionists directed their diplomatic efforts first towards Istanbul. Under Herzl's leadership they twice made approaches to Abdülhamid II: they proposed first to buy Palestine for a certain sum, and then to take on the consolidation of the Ottoman debts. The offer was a promise to wipe out at a stroke the foreign debts, the heaviest burden on Ottoman finances. A similar offer had been made earlier, in 1879, by the British diplomat Laurence Oliphant, and had been rejected.\n\n■ THE FIRST THESIS: HE PROTECTED IT\nThe TDV's article on Abdülhamid counts this subject among the matters on which the sultan stood firm and was partly successful. The offers of money were not accepted. The policy was two-sided: Jews fleeing persecution were given permission to settle in Ottoman lands OUTSIDE Palestine, but the project of a Jewish home in Palestine was not allowed through. The instruments were concrete: Jews were forbidden to buy land in Palestine, and those coming to Jerusalem for pilgrimage were given a temporary permit known as the red slip.\n\n■ THE SECOND THESIS: THE BAN DID NOT HOLD\nThe article on Zionism in the same TDV gives the figures for immigration. Between 1882 and 1903, 20-30,000 people, mainly from Russia, Romania and Galicia, emigrated to Palestine. The Jewish National Fund was established in 1901, and its basic task was to acquire land. Through sales by some Arab property owners and through administrative negligence, Jewish land acquisition increased despite the ban. By 1914 the Jewish population settled in Palestine was approaching 60,000. In other words, the decision of the centre and its implementation on the ground were not the same thing.\n\n■ AFTER 1908\nThere is one more detail that complicates the picture: it was not Abdülhamid who relaxed the restrictions but those who came after him. In the first period of the 1908 Constitutional Era, the Committee of Union and Progress abolished the red slip and freed the purchase of land. Although the restrictions were reimposed after the 31 March Incident, the second wave of immigration, which was more important for the Zionist movement, took place precisely in these years; in 1909 Tel Aviv was founded north of Jaffa. A 1911 memorandum by Süleyman Fethi Bey, the governor (mutasarrıf) of Nablus, reported that Jewish land purchases were continuing while the Muslim population was declining.\n\n■ WHAT CAN BE SAID\nThe sultan's stance of principle is undisputed in the sources: he refused to hand over state territory in return for debt. What is disputed is how far this stance had effect on the ground. The answer given by the sources is that the central ban slowed immigration but could not stop it.", "00feedc1"], "kisa": ["The sultan rejected Herzl's offer to wipe out the debt and restricted settlement in Palestine; but in those same years immigration and land purchases continued despite the ban. Both sentences are true.", "388d1595"]},
"p77b-abdulhamid-ittihad-i-islam-guc-mu-koz-mu":{"baslik": ["Abdülhamid's policy of Islamic unity: a real force or a diplomatic card?", "342dfb59"], "metin": ["Abdülhamid II's policy of ittihad-i Islam (Pan-Islamism) is described in two extreme ways: on one side, a caliph trying to gather the Muslims of the world under a single banner; on the other, a diplomat who frightened Europe without any real power in his hands. The sources confirm part of each.\n\n■ WHY THIS POLICY\nAbdülhamid's reign began with defeat in the War of '93. By the Treaties of San Stefano (3 March 1878) and Berlin (13 July 1878) the state lost at least 5 million inhabitants, half of them Muslim, and close to a third of its territory. The British then established themselves in Cyprus and Egypt, and France occupied Tunis. By the 1880s, as a result of the Balkan losses and the influx of muhacirs (refugees), the share of Muslims in the population had risen to over 70 per cent. The Tanzimat's hope of Ottomanism had suffered a heavy blow; according to the TDV, this picture was understood to mean that the state had no support for its survival other than its Muslim elements. Article 3 of the Constitution of 1876 (Kanun-i Esasi) already defined the sultan, in his capacity as caliph, as the protector of the religion of Islam.\n\n■ WHAT WAS DONE\nIn official and private correspondence, in newspapers and in ceremonies, the titles of caliph and amir al-mu'minin (commander of the faithful) were given greater prominence than those of sultan and padishah. A stand was taken against the separatist ideas spreading among the Arabs with the claim that the caliphate belonged to the Quraysh, and against attempts to make the Khedive of Egypt caliph; in Istanbul, Cairo and London newspapers were published and pamphlets distributed for caliphal propaganda. Use was made of Sufi order sheikhs and tribal chiefs, and religious scholars were sent to distant countries such as South Africa and Japan. The Hejaz Railway, stretching from Damascus to Medina, became the most concrete work of this policy. The Muslims of India organised aid campaigns and put pressure on Britain; according to the TDV, the sultan's name spread to the remotest corners of the country.\n\n■ FIRST READING: A REAL UNEASE\nEurope took this policy seriously. Western diplomats wrote reports to their countries saying that Islam was rising again. From the 1870s onwards Britain followed the debates on Islamic unity closely; the European states repeatedly conveyed to the Sublime Porte the concern they felt at the writings in the press. According to the TDV's assessment, this policy to a certain extent hindered Britain's initiatives in Arabia.\n\n■ SECOND READING: AN EXAGGERATED CARD\nThe TDV's article on Islamic unity gives a noteworthy detail: Abdülhamid wanted to make the European states accept that he had very great influence in the Islamic world, and to this end he also tried the method of spreading exaggerated rumours through intermediaries. Two names stand out: at the beginning of the reign, Süleyman Efendi of Bukhara, sheikh of the Uzbek lodge (Özbekler Tekkesi) in Istanbul, and towards its end, the Hungarian orientalist Arminius Vámbéry. Both met with the sultan, both were in close contact with the British, and both passed on to them alarming information about the sultan's policy. It has been established that these reports in the Western archives were very often untrue or exaggerated. The reports always carried two messages: the sultan can mobilise the Muslims in whatever direction he wishes; the safest way to avert the danger of Pan-Islamism is to stay on good terms with him.\n\n■ CONCLUSION\nThe TDV's verdict is measured: these activities achieved success to a certain extent, but the political and intellectual difficulties encountered always prevented the hoped-for concrete results from being attained. Europe's fear was real; the scale of the fear, however, was partly manufactured in Istanbul. The two readings do not exclude each other: deterrence works as far as the other side believes in it.", "c14693bd"], "kisa": ["The emphasis on the caliphate really did make Europe uneasy; but some of the reports exaggerating the sultan's influence were carried to Europe by the palace circle itself.", "4f5f5cbb"]},
"p77b-alsas-loren-tarih-strateji-ekonomi":{"baslik": ["Alsace-Lorraine: why was this border region, which changed hands four times between 1871 and 1945, so important?", "a48876de"], "sebep.b": ["The annexation of Alsace-Lorraine to the newly founded German Empire at the end of the Franco-German War of 1870-71", "e6b4349d"], "sonuc.b": ["The return of the region to France under the Treaty of Versailles after the First World War", "67341a1e"], "bag": ["The loss of 1871 became the chief cause of the anti-German feeling in France that lasted until 1914; the victory of 1918 brought the region back, but the people who were brought back were no longer the people of 1871.", "b157c638"], "metin": ["■ Where it is and what it covers\nAlsace-Lorraine covers the departments of Haut-Rhin, Bas-Rhin and Moselle in present-day France; it is a region of 13,123 km² between the Rhine and the Vosges mountains. The name itself is a product of war: it was given to the whole of the territories ceded to Germany in 1871.\n\n■ HISTORICAL IMPORTANCE\nIn the 9th century the region was at the centre of Charlemagne's Frankish Empire; it then became part of the German lands of the Holy Roman Empire and remained German territory under various sovereignties until the Thirty Years' War. According to Britannica, the Peace of Westphalia (1648) gave control of the region to France. The region, under French administration, was incorporated in 1871 into the newly unified German Empire on the grounds of its old German ties and its large German-speaking population.\n\n■ STRATEGIC AND ECONOMIC IMPORTANCE\nThe region lies between the Rhine and the Vosges mountains, right on the line of contact between the two countries; the fact that the first clashes of the war (at Sarrebourg and Morhange in August 1914, and at Hartmannswillerkopf in the Vosges in 1915) took place here is a result of this position. Its economic value was also great: according to Britannica, through this loss France surrendered to Germany the region's valuable iron ore deposits, iron and steel works and other industries, and suffered economically as a result. The region's textile production was also important in the war years; the academic source relates that during the war the textile industry collapsed and factories were moved into the interior of Germany.\n\n■ UNDER GERMAN RULE (1871-1918)\nThe region was given the status of imperial territory (Reichsland) and was deprived of effective self-government until 1902. 120,000 German settlers arrived and a policy of Germanisation was applied; thousands of people who regarded themselves as French emigrated. But in time the picture changed: after 1905 the Catholics, disturbed by the secular policy of the French Republic, turned towards autonomy for the region within Germany, and the constitution of 1911 gave the region greater autonomy. According to the academic source, by 1914 most of the population of 1.9 million had become reconciled to their fate; the war was received in the region with indifference.\n\n■ IN THE WAR (1914-1918)\nFrance did not enter the war specifically to recover Alsace-Lorraine, but the region became the most powerful symbol of Entente propaganda: posters and songs called for the liberation of the lost sisters. About 380,000 Alsatians and Lorrainers fought for Germany; because of desertions, in March 1915 the German high command moved them to the Eastern Front and the navy. About 17,500, however, volunteered for the French army. French was banned in the region, and even singing the Marseillaise became grounds for imprisonment. When French units entered the region in November 1918, the population welcomed them enthusiastically, and France regarded this as an unofficial plebiscite.\n\n■ THE RETURN AND AFTERWARDS\nThe region was restored to France in 1919. But the attempts at rapid assimilation, in particular the efforts to replace church schools with state schools and to suppress German-language newspapers, caused problems — the written language of 75 per cent of the population was German. In the 1920s a strong autonomy movement arose. In 1940, with the collapse of France, Germany annexed the region for a second time; in 1945 the region returned to France again. According to Britannica, today the autonomy movement has largely died out; the German dialect called Alsatian continues to be the common language of the region, and French and German are taught together in schools.", "390afddb"]},
"p77b-avusturya-macaristan-dagilisi-1918":{"baslik": ["Why and how did Austria-Hungary break apart?", "e6274e2c"], "sebep.b": ["The independence movements of the Czechs and of the Serbs, Croats and Slovenes in 1918 in a multinational empire, and the economic collapse brought about by the war", "11952496"], "sonuc.b": ["The emergence of Czechoslovakia, the State of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes, Hungary and the Republic of Austria as separate states", "0f2be464"], "bag": ["The Danubian Monarchy, the Ottomans' northern neighbour for centuries, turned into the Republic of Austria on the day the Armistice of Mudros was signed: the ends of the two empires fell side by side on the calendar.", "6d02c555"], "metin": ["■ The Dual Monarchy\nFrom 1804 to 1867 Austria bore the name of the Austrian Empire, and from 1867 to 1918 that of the Austro-Hungarian Empire. According to the TDV, the dual state on equal terms of 1867 was the result of Hungarian nationalists voicing their discontent with Austrian rule through uprisings and forcing Vienna into it. In other words, already in 1867 the empire was a structure divided in two at the demand of its nations.\n\n■ WHY DID IT BREAK UP?\nThe TDV lists two reasons: the Czechs and the Serbs, Croats and Slovenes within the political framework of the empire launched independence movements in 1918, and to this were added the economic difficulties in which the country found itself. On the Balkan side, Serbia's ambitions for a 'Greater Serbia', which it pursued with regard to the many South Slavs in the monarchy, had turned the state, in the words of the TDV, into the 'sick man on the Danube'.\n\n■ OCTOBER 1918: ONE PROCLAMATION AFTER ANOTHER\nOn 18 October 1918 the provisional government formed by the Czechs in Paris proclaimed the independence of Czechoslovakia; on 24 October the Hungarians announced that they had established an independent state. According to the TDV, it was now impossible for the empire, which had entered the process of disintegration, to continue the war. On 29 October the founding of Czechoslovakia was proclaimed in Prague, and that of the State of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes in Zagreb; thereupon the Austrian Germans established the Republic of Austria on 30 October. Emperor Karl's signing of an armistice with the Italians at Villa Giusti on 3 November hastened the break-up still further.\n\n■ WHICH TERRITORY WENT TO WHOM?\n• Czechoslovakia: proclaimed its independence on 18 and 29 October; Austria recognised it by the Treaty of Saint-Germain.\n• Kingdom of the Serbs, Croats and Slovenes (Yugoslavia): the South Slavs who had separated from the empire united with Serbia on 1 December 1918.\n• Romania: Transylvania (including the Banat of Temeşvar) and Bukovina joined Romania on 1 December 1918 by decision of their peoples.\n• Hungary: under the Treaty of Trianon of 4 June 1920 it lost two thirds of its 283,000 km² of territory, excluding Croatia; 60% of its population, among them more than three million Hungarians, were left outside its borders, and its army was reduced to 35,000 men.\n• Austria: by the Treaty of Saint-Germain of 10 September 1919 it recognised the independence of Hungary, Czechoslovakia and Yugoslavia; it agreed not to unite with Germany without the consent of the League of Nations, to abolish compulsory military service and to limit its army to 30,000 men.\n\n■ WHY NOT THREE PIECES, BUT MORE?\nThe break-up did not end with the trio of Vienna, Budapest and Prague: part of the territory joined existing states (Romania, Serbia), and part gave birth to new states (Czechoslovakia, Yugoslavia). On the map these proclamations are gathered into the single day of 11 November 1918; the proclamations themselves are spread between 18 October and 1 December.", "60212b92"]},
"p77b-birinci-kanal-harekati-1915":{"baslik": ["The First Suez Canal Campaign: why did they go to Suez, and why did they turn back?", "97de611a"], "tarih_metin": ["The forces assembled at Beersheba and set out on 7 January 1915 · raid on the canal 26 January - 4 February 1915 · Second Canal Campaign July 1916", "76716f74"], "yer": ["Beersheba (Birüssebi) - Sinai desert - Suez Canal", "35eb405d"], "oncesi": ["Egypt had been an Ottoman province since 1517, but it had been effectively independent since Mehmed Ali Pasha of Kavala and had been under British occupation since 1882; in law, Ottoman sovereignty was deemed to continue. When the Ottomans entered the war, this fiction too came to an end: on 18 December 1914 Britain officially declared a protectorate over Egypt, deposed Khedive Abbas and made his uncle Hussein Kamil sultan in his place. The recovery of Egypt was among the Ottoman war aims. Immediately after the entry into the war, Enver Pasha proposed to Cemal Pasha that he lead an operation against the British in Egypt; in addition to being Minister of the Navy, Cemal Pasha was appointed commander of the Fourth Army and was invested with extraordinary powers in Syria. What was expected of the operation was clear: it was hoped that Mehmed V Reşad's call to jihad would trigger popular uprisings in Egypt. According to an academic assessment, the Ottoman leadership calculated that even the appearance of a small expeditionary force on the banks of the canal could ignite an uprising in Egypt. The role of the German side is visible in the command: the operation was run by a large number of German officers and technicians, and Kress von Kressenstein was Cemal Pasha's chief of staff.", "004afd40"], "akis": ["The forces assembled at Beersheba set out on 7 January 1915, crossed the Sinai desert and reached the canal. The westernmost point of the Ottoman advance was a raid on the canal carried out between 26 January and 4 February. The canal could not be crossed, and the force then withdrew into the desert in good order. The sources consulted do not give the tactical details of the raid (at what point, and with how many columns, the crossing was attempted); this card does not invent those details.", "bd9e8aee"], "sonuc": ["Neither of the two expectations was met: the canal was not crossed, nor did Egypt rise up. For the rest of the war Sinai was placed under British military administration. The operation was attempted once more in July 1916, this time under the command of Kress von Kressenstein. In the meantime extensive railways had been laid in the desert — the work was directed by Meißner Pasha, chief engineer of the Hejaz Railway. The second campaign too was repulsed, and thereafter the Ottoman army went over to defence and retreat in the face of the British advancing into Sinai and towards Gaza. The outcome of the two campaigns also touched other fronts: according to the TDV, when Sharif Hussein rebelled in the Hejaz in 1916, troops could not be sent to the Hejaz because the Canal Campaign was continuing with full intensity. Cemal Pasha's wartime administration in Syria — conscription and the requisitioning of food supplies, together with the blockade and the plague of locusts — laid the ground for a famine in the region that killed hundreds of thousands of people. The Canal Campaign later became the target of criticism and, in the TDV's words, was interpreted as a fanciful venture.", "15852e63"], "tartisma": ["The clear answer that the sources consulted give to the question of why it failed is political: the operation rested on the assumption that the call to jihad would give rise to an uprising in Egypt, and this assumption collapsed. A military answer is not stated directly in the sources. This much can be said — and it is an interpretation: the laying of a railway across the desert before the second attempt shows of itself that the heaviest burden of the first attempt was crossing the desert. There is also a note on the dating: the sources refer to the first campaign as that of February 1915, while the days of the raid on the canal are 26 January - 4 February; the date of 14 January 1915 given to this campaign in the chronology could not be verified in the sources consulted.", "4abb692b"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman Fourth Army", "d7bd07fe"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Cemal Pasha, Minister of the Navy and commander of the Fourth Army · chief of staff the German Colonel Friedrich Kress von Kressenstein", "6c7d4542"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure is given in the sources consulted", "3af0cfca"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["British and imperial forces in Egypt", "8f1b5623"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["General John Maxwell (commander of the British forces in Egypt)", "7be5fa1f"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["about 30,000 at the start of the war; their task was the defence of the Suez Canal", "8c32edb2"]},
"p77b-buyuk-romanya-1918":{"baslik": ["Greater Romania: how did a kingdom that lost the war more than double in size?", "f0621b18"], "sebep.b": ["The disintegration of the Russian and Austro-Hungarian empires in 1917-1918", "a487c27e"], "sonuc.b": ["The union of Transylvania, Bukovina and Bessarabia with the Kingdom of Romania — Greater (United) Romania", "209c5c71"], "bag": ["Romania's reason for entering the war was the lands of the Romanians living in Austria-Hungary; it was defeated in the war, but when the two neighbouring empires collapsed, the people of these lands joined Bucharest one after another.", "cc6c0705"], "metin": ["■ A state born out of the Ottoman world\nThe core of present-day Romania consists of two Ottoman tributary voivodeships: Wallachia and Moldavia, in the Ottoman period the Principalities (Memleketeyn). The two principalities united in 1859; on 9 May 1877 independence was proclaimed, and the independence of Romania, which contributed significantly to the Ottoman defeat at Plevna in the Russo-Turkish War of 1877-1878, was recognised at the Congress of Berlin. In that war Romania, while ceding the south of Bessarabia to Russia, received Northern Dobruja in return. In 1881 it became a kingdom.\n\n■ THE ROMANIANS LEFT OUTSIDE\nWhen the kingdom was established, a large part of the Romanian population was outside its borders. Transylvania had passed to Austria after Karlowitz in 1699 and from 1867 was in the Hungarian half of the Dual Monarchy; here Romanians, Hungarians and Germans lived together. Bukovina, the north of Moldavia, had passed to Austria in 1775, and Bessarabia, between the Prut and the Dniester, to Russia in 1812. Banat, Bihor and Maramureș were also mixed regions. These Romanians outside the kingdom determined the direction of Romanian foreign policy.\n\n■ ENTRY INTO THE WAR AND DEFEAT\nFrom 1883 until 1914 Romania was part of the Triple Alliance, and at the start of the war it declared neutrality. The pro-German King Carol I died in 1914; his successor Ferdinand followed a pro-French line that gave prominence to the principle of the union of all Romanians. According to the academic source, the most important reason for joining the war was to gain the Romanian lands in Austria-Hungary. In 1916 Romania joined the Entente; but the attack on the Habsburgs backfired, and the greater part of the country was occupied with German support and remained under occupation until the autumn of 1918. The Ottoman side of the matter is also interesting: the Ottoman leadership wished to keep Romania standing as a counterweight to Bulgaria, even after Romania had gone over to the enemy camp and been crushed.\n\n■ 1918: FROM DEFEAT TO UNION\nAt the end of the war the Russian and Austro-Hungarian empires collapsed. In the words of the TDV, Transylvania (including the Banat of Temesvár), Bukovina and Bessarabia, each by the decision of its native people, united under the flag of the Kingdom of Romania, and Greater Romania, or more correctly United Romania, came into being (1 December 1918). The academic source sums up the result in a single sentence: although Romania lost the war, by making use of the situation and its consequences it succeeded in establishing a nation state more than twice the size of the former one.\n\n■ AFTERWARDS\nGreater Romania lasted twenty-two years. In 1940, as a result of the Hitler-Stalin agreement, Bessarabia and Northern Bukovina passed to the Soviet Union; the present borders of Romania were determined by this loss. In place of Bessarabia, the Soviet republic that was the forerunner of present-day Moldova was established.", "3e6eb8eb"]},
"p77b-istanbulun-resmi-isgali-1920-sebep-sonuc":{"baslik": ["16 March 1920: Why was Istanbul officially occupied, and what did the occupation change?", "b4cb4dd2"], "sebep.b": ["The successes of the National Forces (Kuva-yi Milliye) against the French in the south, and the proclamation of the National Pact (Misak-ı Milli) by the last Ottoman parliament", "fa4f7a36"], "sonuc.b": ["The opening of the Grand National Assembly of Turkey in Ankara", "03467f37"], "bag": ["The occupation was carried out to cut the parliament and government in Istanbul off from Anatolia; Mustafa Kemal, for his part, read it as a proclamation that Ottoman sovereignty had in effect come to an end.", "85c2078c"], "metin": ["■ Background: from de facto to official occupation\nAfter Mudros, Istanbul was already under the control of Entente forces; what made 16 March 1920 different was that the occupation was officially proclaimed and all institutions were seized.\n\n■ THE CAUSES — STEP BY STEP\n① In south-eastern Anatolia the French found themselves in difficulty against the National Forces (Kuva-yi Milliye). On 13 February 1920 the Entente powers sent the Ottoman government a note stating that unless the attacks were stopped, Istanbul would be officially occupied. According to the TDV article on Sèvres, this note was delivered one day after the opening of the London Conference, and Lloyd George and Lord Curzon argued that if the Turks were to be made to behave in future, it was essential to seize territory and hold it as a pledge.\n② The last Ottoman Chamber of Deputies answered this on 17 February by ratifying the National Pact.\n③ On 21 February the British staged a military show of force in Istanbul; on 1 March the High Commissioners announced that they would not accept the parliament's decision that Damad Ferid Pasha be tried before the High Court, and demanded that the National Forces be condemned. When the government refused, Ali Rıza Pasha was forced to resign on 3 March.\n④ The government of Salih Pasha, formed on 8 March, consisted entirely of nationalists. Thereupon Istanbul was officially occupied on 16 March.\n\n■ THE DAY OF THE OCCUPATION AND THE PROCLAMATION\nThe occupiers seized all institutions; they arrested deputies and intellectuals who supported the national movement, headed by Rauf Bey and Vasıf Bey, and exiled them to Malta. The British also raided the parliament. The occupation proclamation of the three great powers is striking: they said that they had come not to destroy the sultan's authority but to strengthen it, that the occupation was temporary and that they intended to leave Istanbul to Turkey, but they added the warning that this decision might change if large-scale disturbances or massacres broke out.\n\n■ THE CONSEQUENCES\n① On 18 March the parliament suspended its work, and on 11 April it was dissolved by imperial decree.\n② On 19 March Mustafa Kemal Pasha issued a circular on behalf of the Representative Committee: with the occupation of Istanbul, Ottoman sovereignty had ended; the Representative Committee was formally taking over the administration of the country; telegraphic communication with Istanbul was forbidden; and elections would be held for an assembly with extraordinary powers in Ankara. As the TDV observes, just as the occupation of Izmir had hastened the organisation of the National Forces, so the occupation of Istanbul hastened the process of founding a new state in Anatolia.\n③ In Istanbul, meanwhile, a hardening in the opposite direction began: Damad Ferid Pasha, who became grand vizier again on 5 April, declared the leaders of the National Forces rebels; the fatwa of the Shaykh al-Islam Dürrizade Abdullah Efendi, which held it obligatory to kill them, was distributed; and the Anzavur and Düzce revolts were supported.\n④ On 23 April 1920 the Grand National Assembly of Turkey opened in Ankara and declared itself the sole representative of the nation; some of the deputies moved from Istanbul to Ankara.\n⑤ The most far-reaching consequence came two and a half years later. The decision of 1 November 1922 on the abolition of the sultanate ruled that the Istanbul government was deemed to have passed into history for ever as of 16 March 1920. In other words, the Grand National Assembly of Turkey accepted that, in law, the Ottoman sultanate had ended on the day of this occupation.", "ae136e00"]},
"p77b-kafkas-cephesi-ve-kafkas-islam-ordusu":{"baslik": ["The Caucasus front 1914-1918: from Sarıkamış to Baku", "5fe140a7"], "tarih_metin": ["November 1914 - November 1918 · order to form the Caucasus Army of Islam 5 April 1918 · capture of Baku 15 September 1918", "37de1155"], "yer": ["Eastern Anatolia (Köprüköy, Sarıkamış, Erzurum) - South Caucasus (Ganja, Goychay, Salyan) - Baku - Dagestan", "40cafd92"], "oncesi": ["The front opened with the Ottoman navy's bombardment of Russian ports on 29-30 October 1914 and Russia's declaration of war. At the beginning of November Russian forces crossed the border and reached Köprüköy; the Third Army threw them back with a counter-attack, and this success strengthened Enver Pasha's belief in a swift victory. Then, in December 1914, came the Sarıkamış encircling operation, and the Third Army broke up in an operation in which it was left without supplies in the snow in winter (details in the card on Sarıkamış). Throughout 1915 the front remained relatively quiet. An academic assessment summarises the Ottoman objective in the Caucasus as follows: expansion of territory and influence was seen as a means of increasing security against Russia; there are accounts that explain this by ideology (Pan-Islamism, Pan-Turkism), but the real motive was long-term geopolitical security.", "0e00350a"], "akis": ["① Erzurum (1916): in December 1915 the Russian commander Yudenich planned a pre-emptive blow against Erzurum. The general assault on the forts began on 10 February 1916; the Ottoman units abandoned the encircled fortifications, and on 16 February the Russian forces entered Erzurum. The academic source gives Ottoman losses as 10,000 dead and wounded and 5,000 prisoners. In 1916 the Ottoman forces were pushed back towards Central Anatolia. ② The collapse of Russia (1917-1918): the February Revolution halted the Russian advance, and the army disintegrated. On 18 December 1917 an armistice was signed at Erzincan with the Transcaucasian Commissariat. By the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk the Ottoman state recovered not only the lands it had lost since 1914 but also the three Caucasian provinces it had lost in 1878. As Russian authority in the region dissolved and Georgian, Armenian and Azeri states emerged, the Ottoman leadership saw both an opportunity and a threat: to establish buffer states that would keep Russia as far as possible from the border, and to prevent the birth of a strong and hostile state on its eastern flank. For this reason it advanced beyond the Brest-Litovsk line despite Germany's sharp objection. ③ The Caucasus Army of Islam (1918): it was formed by Enver Pasha's order of 5 April 1918, and his brother Nuri Pasha was placed at its head. The name was chosen deliberately: since Enver Pasha's expansionism clashed with Germany's interests in the Caucasus, the intention was to give the impression that the army had been formed of Azerbaijanis; in fact the force consisted mainly of Ottoman units, and its size was not enough for it to count as an army. On 28 May 1918 the Azerbaijan Democratic Republic was proclaimed; Article 4 of the treaty signed at Batum on 4 June gave Azerbaijan the right to request military assistance from the Ottomans. Nuri Pasha and his staff arrived in Ganja at the end of May and set up headquarters; the 5th Caucasian Division arrived in June. Local participation was less than expected — at the end of June the Azeri corps numbered 500-600 men — and on 11 July those born in 1894-1899 were called up. Between 16 June and 2 July the Red Army offensive was repulsed at Goychay and Salyan. At the end of July the Bolshevik administration in Baku fell, and the Centrocaspian Dictatorship established in its place invited in the British. The Army of Islam's attack of 30 July was repulsed with heavy losses on 5 August; General Dunsterville arrived in Baku on 17 August. In the final assault, made after reinforcement by the 15th Division, Baku was taken on 15 September 1918. Afterwards Dagestan (20 September), Derbent (5 October) and Petrovsk (8 November) were seized.", "bd624210"], "sonuc": ["The gain was short-lived. After the Armistice of Mudros, signed on 30 October 1918, the Ottoman forces began to evacuate the region, and British forces entered Baku. According to the academic assessment, the Ottoman state obtained no significant gain from this expedition. The Azerbaijan Democratic Republic, for its part, came to an end with the Red Army's occupation on 27 April 1920. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam's article on Azerbaijan records the meaning of this brief period in another sentence: for the first time in history a Turkic state had been established under the name of Azerbaijan; in December 1990 Azerbaijan, having removed the words Soviet Socialist from its name, adopted the flag of the 1918-1920 republic as its official flag.", "5dcb3db0"], "tartisma": ["① From whom did it take Baku? The TDV's article on Azerbaijan writes that Baku was taken from the hands of the Russians; the academic source, however, sets out in detail that in the summer of 1918 the city was first in the hands of the Bolshevik Baku Soviet and from the end of July in those of the Centrocaspian Dictatorship, and that the British Dunsterforce unit took part in the defence. The two accounts do not contradict each other, but they differ in resolution. ② The date of the British occupation: the TDV's article on Azerbaijan writes that the British occupied Baku in November 1919; this date is a year out of step with the withdrawal that came immediately after the armistice (October 1918) and also appears inconsistent within the article itself — for this reason the card has given no day. ③ The aim: the Caucasus campaign is often explained by the ideal of Turan; the academic assessment does not accept this and identifies the real motive as security against Russia and a belt of buffer states.", "a762427f"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman state · in 1918 together with the Azerbaijan Democratic Republic", "6e14ba3c"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["1914-15 Enver Pasha · 1918 Nuri Pasha, commander of the Caucasus Army of Islam (Enver Pasha's brother); 5th Caucasian Division Mürsel Pasha, 15th Division Süleyman İzzet Bey", "86aebfab"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["11,564 other ranks and 448 officers in the final assault on Baku (academic source)", "d1aab985"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["1914-1917 Russian Caucasus Army · in 1918 the Baku Soviet, then the Centrocaspian Dictatorship and the British Dunsterforce", "6d308524"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["Yudenich (1914-1917) · General Lionel Dunsterville (1918)", "680cbb53"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["the army of the Baku Soviet about 18,000 in June 1918 (academic source)", "22897f66"]},
"p77b-kimdir-fahreddin-pasa-medine-mudafii":{"baslik": ["Fahreddin Pasha, defender of Medina: the commander who did not surrender even after the war was over", "faf3317e"], "metin": ["■ Who he was\nHis original given name was Ömer; he was born in Rusçuk and, after the Surname Law, took the surname Türkkan. After the War of '93 he moved with his family to Istanbul; he graduated from the Military Academy in 1888 and from the Staff College in 1891. In the Balkan War he played a part in the defence of Çatalca and the recapture of Edirne. When the First World War began, he was commander of the 12th Corps at Mosul; in 1915 he became deputy commander of the Fourth Army. The TDV records that in this period he was charged with the settlement of the Armenians subjected to deportation and with the suppression of the Urfa, Zeytun, Haçin and Musa Dagh uprisings.\n\n■ GOING TO MEDINA\nOn the news that Husayn, the Sharif of Mecca, had come to an agreement with the British and was preparing to revolt, Cemal Pasha sent him to Medina; he reached the city on 31 May 1916 and reported that the revolt would begin within a few days. On 3 June Sharif Husayn and his sons rose in revolt, destroying the railway and telegraph lines around Medina; the attack on the guard posts on the night of 5-6 June was repulsed. In a short time Jeddah (16 June), Mecca (7 July) and Taif (22 September) fell; of the great centres of the Hejaz only Medina remained in hand. Fahreddin Pasha was appointed commander of the Hejaz Expeditionary Force, and by 29 August 1916 he had established a 100-kilometre security belt around the city.\n\n■ THE SIEGE\nNo reinforcements came: according to the TDV, troops could not be sent to the Hejaz because the Canal Campaign was continuing. The Hejaz Railway, which linked Medina to Syria, was dynamited incessantly. When it was decided to evacuate the Hejaz in part, Fahreddin Pasha proposed that the sacred relics at the Prophet's tomb be transferred to Istanbul against possible plunder; the relics, thirty pieces, were sent under the protection of 2,000 soldiers. When Al-Mudawwara Station, between Tabuk and Mada'in, fell, the city was completely surrounded, and hunger and disease set in. The academic source estimates the civilian population of the city at about 75,000; the pasha gave priority to the needs of his soldiers, some 3,000 men; part of the population left, and part was forced into evacuation. His reply to Istanbul, which proposed evacuation to him, is famous: I cannot lower the Turkish flag from the citadel of Medina with my own hand; if you are going to evacuate, send another commander here.\n\n■ AFTER THE ARMISTICE\nOn 30 October 1918 the Armistice of Mudros was signed; under its Article 16 the Ottoman units in the Hejaz were to surrender. Fahreddin Pasha would not agree. He did not reply to the British torpedo boat that communicated the terms of the armistice, and he imprisoned the captain sent by the Sublime Porte; nor did he listen to Haydar Molla, the Minister of Justice, who brought the order to surrender bearing the sultan's signature. According to the academic source, this resistance also put the Ottoman government in a difficult position: Istanbul was protesting that the British were continuing their occupation in northern Syria and Iraq despite the armistice, while Medina showed the Ottomans to be the party violating the armistice. In the end, under pressure from his own officers, he consented to surrender. On the day of the surrender he got into a bed he had had prepared in advance in a madrasa near the Rawda of the Prophet's tomb and said that he would not go anywhere; his deputy commander Necib Bey and those around him took hold of him and carried him to his tent at the Hashemite headquarters (10 January 1919). Sharif Abdullah's forces entered Medina on 13 January — seventy-two days after the armistice.\n\n■ AFTERWARDS\nThe pasha, whom the British called the Turkish tiger, was sent as a prisoner of war to Egypt and on 5 August 1919 to Malta; while he was in exile, the court martial in Istanbul sentenced him to death. Through the efforts of the Ankara government he was freed from Malta on 8 April 1921, came to Ankara on 24 September 1921, and on 9 November 1921 was appointed ambassador to Kabul. He retired as a major general in 1936, died on 22 November 1948 and, in accordance with his will, was buried at Rumelihisarı.", "a7a2349f"], "kisa": ["He defended Medina for two years and seven months and after the armistice heeded neither the British nor the sultan's order to surrender; in the end his own officers forced him to surrender.", "da8130ba"], "not": ["His memory is still disputed. According to the academic source, the siege was long forgotten amid the birth pangs of the successor states; in Turkey, memoirs published after 1960 by his former comrades-in-arms revived him as a Turkish-Muslim hero. In the Arab narrative, the same siege is remembered for the hunger the population suffered and for forced evacuation. In 2017 this divergence went as far as a diplomatic crisis: a message shared by the foreign minister of the United Arab Emirates accused Fahreddin Pasha of maltreating the people of Medina and stealing their property; Ankara thereupon gave the name of Fahreddin Pasha to the street on which the UAE embassy stands.", "db035af2"]},
"p77b-kimdir-mehmed-v-resad-sahsiyet":{"baslik": ["Sultan Reşad: a gentle, mild-mannered constitutional monarch or a colourless figure?", "8c7378bf"], "metin": ["■ A secluded life as a prince\nMehmed Reşad was born on 2 November 1844 at the Çırağan Palace; his father was Sultan Abdülmecid and his mother Gülcemal Kadınefendi. He learned Arabic, Persian and some knowledge of Islamic law, and lived a free life in the time of his father and of his uncle Abdülaziz. When his brother Abdülhamid II came to the throne in 1876, he became heir apparent, and everything changed: Abdülhamid, who had seen the two sultans before him deposed, forbade his brother to meet anyone in the palace other than his own intimates. According to the TDV, this secluded and fearful life affected him deeply.\n\n■ A THRONE THAT CAME THROUGH A COUP\nAfter the 31 March Incident, the General National Assembly (Meclis-i Umumi-yi Milli), in deposing Abdülhamid, decided that Reşad Efendi should be placed on the throne (27 April 1909). Since the entry of the Action Army into Istanbul was regarded as a second conquest of the city, the new sultan was given the title Mehmed the Fifth; the people nevertheless called him Sultan Reşad. At the ceremony of allegiance he said that he was proud to be the first sultan of true liberty. When he came to the throne he was sixty-five years old. On the very first day it became clear where power lay: while the ceremony of allegiance was still in progress, Mahmud Şevket Pasha, commander of the Action Army, had sent the former sultan into exile in Salonica, and neither the sultan nor the grand vizier knew of it.\n\n■ TEN GOVERNMENTS, NINE YEARS\nDuring his nine-year reign the government changed ten times. Under the constitution the head of the executive was the grand vizier, who was responsible to parliament; the sultan appointed the grand vizier but bore no responsibility for the conduct of government. The focus of politics shifted from the palace to the central headquarters of the Committee of Union and Progress. Reşad tried to act within this framework: to get to know his country he went to Bursa and İzmit; from 5 to 26 June 1911 he made a tour of Rumelia that took in Salonica, Skopje, Pristina, Kosovo and Monastir, and on the plain of Kosovo, where the tomb of Murad Hüdavendigâr (Murad I) stands, he performed the Friday prayer with a large congregation; this tour had a positive effect, especially on the Albanians. But at critical moments his will did not prevail: although he wished to save Damad Salih Pasha, in the face of the Unionists' threat he was compelled to approve the death sentence. After the triumvirate of Talat, Enver and Cemal came to dominate the government, some of the appointments made by the sultan were rejected.\n\n■ THE WAR HE DID NOT WANT\nOn the day the secret alliance with Germany was concluded (2 August 1914), parliament went into recess, and the triumvirate dragged the country into the war without the knowledge even of some members of the party and the cabinet. Reşad proclaimed this fait accompli, which he did not want at all, with a proclamation on the same day, and with the proclamation of the great jihad (cihad-i ekber) he called all Muslims to war. When Istanbul was for a time threatened with occupation, the government decided to move to Eskişehir; as the sultan was making his preparations, he wished to take his elder brother Abdülhamid with him as well. Abdülhamid not only did not go but also told his brother not to go either, saying that if Istanbul were abandoned it would never be possible to return. Reşad stayed; by a fatwa of the office of the Shaykh al-Islam he received the title of ghazi.\n\n■ AS A HUMAN BEING\nThe TDV describes him as a gentle, compassionate, devout and courteous ruler. He had not received a regular education, but his grounding in Eastern culture was good, and he is said to have known Persian. He had joined the Mevlevi order at a young age and spent his years as a prince reading the Masnavi. He loved to read Ottoman history and the legendary deeds of his ancestors, and he wrote poetry; the ghazal he wrote on the victory at the Dardanelles passed from mouth to mouth and was set to music. He suffered from diabetes and had undergone a prostate operation. He died on 3 July 1918, before seeing the Armistice of Mudros, and was buried in the tomb he had had built at Eyüp during his lifetime.\n\n■ TWO READINGS\nThe first reading sees him as a conscious constitutional ruler. The answer he gave to those who criticised him is the basis of this reading: if I am to interfere in everything under the constitutional regime, what was my brother's fault? To the opposition too he had said that he regarded all deputies equally as representatives of the nation and that he was an impartial constitutional ruler. The second reading sees the same conduct as weakness: the TDV writes that his colourless and timid personality prevented harsh clashes, but that, lacking political training and experience, he was unable to be the arbiter of a dynamic and anarchic political life. What he said to those who criticised his submission to the Unionists — that he acted in this way for the survival of the monarchy, for otherwise they would proclaim a republic — shows that both readings may be true: his reticence was a fear as much as a principle.", "f194bb70"], "kisa": ["Coming to the throne at the age of sixty-five after living under surveillance throughout his elder brother's entire reign, Reşad understood being a constitutional ruler as not interfering in everything; his reign passed in the shadow of the Unionists.", "ddd168ea"]},
"p77b-misak-i-milli-nedir":{"baslik": ["What is the National Pact (Misak-ı Milli) — and why are its borders so often misdescribed?", "9d1e34ab"], "metin": ["■ When, and who wrote it\nThe National Pact (Misak-ı Milli) is also called Ahd-i Milli or Peyman-ı Milli. Its preparation began at the congresses of Erzurum and Sivas. Mustafa Kemal Pasha advised some members of the last Ottoman Chamber of Deputies, which opened on 12 January 1920, to prepare a programme in line with the resolutions of the congresses, and sent Rauf Bey a text of eight articles. A committee formed in the parliament combined the different texts.\n\n■ TWO DATES: 28 JANUARY AND 17 FEBRUARY\nThe text was signed by 121 deputies at a private meeting held in the parliament on 28 January 1920. The TDV makes a point of correcting one error: some sources write that the text was adopted that day in a secret session of the parliament, but the parliament held no session at all that day. Because the text concerned foreign affairs, it was kept secret until it had been translated and examined. At the session of 17 February 1920 Şeref Bey, deputy for Edirne, read out the text; the document, adopted unanimously, was published under the name Misak-ı Milli, translated into French and sent to all governments and parliaments.\n\n■ THE SIX ARTICLES — IN SUMMARY\n① The future of the places with an Arab majority that remained under occupation at the time of the armistice of 30 October 1918 would be determined by the free vote of the population; all those parts WITHIN AND OUTSIDE the armistice line inhabited by an Ottoman-Muslim majority form a whole that does not admit of separation for any reason.\n② For Kars, Ardahan and Batum (the Three Sanjaks, Elviye-i Selâse), a new plebiscite may be held if necessary.\n③ The legal status of Western Thrace should be determined by the free vote of its inhabitants.\n④ Provided that the security of Istanbul and the Sea of Marmara is protected, the joint decision on opening the Straits to world commerce is valid.\n⑤ Minority rights will be respected, on condition that the Muslims in neighbouring countries also enjoy the same rights.\n⑥ Full independence is essential, as against restrictions that obstruct political, judicial and financial development — that is, the capitulations.\n\n■ THE REAL DEBATE: WITHIN AND OUTSIDE\nIn the text sent by Mustafa Kemal, as at the Sivas Congress, only the Muslim-majority territories WITHIN the armistice line were deemed indivisible. The committee, taking Wilson's principles as its basis, inserted the phrase WITHIN AND OUTSIDE; it thereby proclaimed that the occupied places had not been given up either. In a letter to Rauf Bey, Mustafa Kemal pointed out that this wording departed considerably from the principle of the boundary; Rauf Bey replied that the basis of the frontier was nationality, and that Turkish cities such as Sulaymaniyah and Kirkuk had been included in this way. As the TDV observes, most works on the subject give no place to the word outside and create the mistaken impression that today's borders are the borders of the National Pact.\n\n■ WHAT HAPPENED\nThe National Pact met with a hostile reaction; a month later Istanbul was officially occupied and the parliament dissolved. But in Ankara the document became the basic principle of the new state, an ideal whose full realisation was to be worked for. Some of its aims were not achieved: Batum was left to Georgia by the Treaty of Moscow of 1921; Syria and Lebanon remained with France under the Ankara Agreement of 1921; at Lausanne (1923) Iraq, Palestine, Cyprus, the Aegean islands and Western Thrace remained outside the border. Hatay joined in 1939. The TDV's summary: in the National Pact the aim was set broadly, and, as circumstances required, what could be obtained was accepted.", "8a776543"], "kisa": ["The National Pact is a peace programme of six articles; it covers not only the area within the armistice line but also what lay outside it. Today's borders are not the whole of it, but the part that circumstances made it possible to obtain.", "2edacad7"]},
"p77b-mustafa-kemal-samsun-yolculugu-1919":{"baslik": ["The steamer Bandırma: why and how did Mustafa Kemal go from Istanbul to Samsun?", "fbc04a0d"], "sebep.b": ["The report given by the British on the disorder in the Samsun district and the Istanbul government's decision to send a commander with authority there", "f3cc781f"], "sonuc.b": ["Mustafa Kemal's landing at Samsun and the organisation of national resistance in Anatolia — Havza, the Amasya Circular", "58ad4618"], "bag": ["The official mission and the real aim were not the same: in the words of the TDV, both the sultan and Mustafa Kemal viewed salvation in different dimensions and with different understandings.", "8b7f1d2f"], "metin": ["■ Why Samsun\nAfter the Armistice of Mudros, Greek organisations in the Eastern Black Sea region acting on the dream of a Pontic state (such as the Pontus Athletic Club and the Holy Anatolian Greek Society) stepped up their activities. The Muslim population reacted to the attacks of the Greek bands, particularly those based on Mount Nebyan, and local defence organisations arose. On 21 April 1919 the British submitted a report calling for this disorder to be prevented. The government of Damad Ferid Pasha decided to send a commander with authority there. According to the TDV, the ostensible reason for Mustafa Kemal's going to Samsun was the clashes there between the Greek and Muslim bands.\n\n■ THE APPOINTMENT: A COINCIDENCE, SOME SUSPICIONS\nHis name was proposed by his friends at the Ottoman General Staff. The suspicion that he was a Unionist was dispelled by the Minister of the Interior, Mehmed Ali Bey, and the Minister of the Navy, Avni Pasha. The irony is that Mustafa Kemal's name also appeared in a British report of 28 February on the list of those whose banishment from Istanbul or dismissal was demanded; for this reason Ferid Pasha consulted Ryan, the interpreter of the British High Commission, and was satisfied on receiving the reply that the name aroused no suspicion. The decree appointing him inspector of the troops of the Ninth Army was approved by the sultan on 30 April 1919. He was given authority to ensure public order in the region, to collect ammunition and weapons, to disband the councils (şura) and, for this purpose, to enter into relations with the commanders and civil administrators in the region — a broad authority that enabled him to travel around Anatolia.\n\n■ THE SEND-OFF\nOn the evening of 14 May he was received by the grand vizier, and on the following evening by the sultan at the Yıldız Palace. Pointing to the Entente fleet anchored in the Bosphorus, Vahdeddin said: The service you are about to perform now may be more important than all the rest; Pasha, Pasha, you can save the state. The TDV finds exaggerated the claim that the sultan gave him a large sum of money: the amount mentioned is raised from 100,000 liras up to 400,000 gold pieces, whereas the money given was an allowance similar to those given at that time to other governors and commanders for internal security.\n\n■ THE VOYAGE\nOn the day after the occupation of İzmir, he left Istanbul aboard the steamer Bandırma with the seventeen officers of his suite. The steamer passed out of the Bosphorus into the Black Sea, made its way eastwards along the Anatolian coast and reached Samsun on the morning of 19 May 1919. The voyage lasted only a few days, but the place he was going to was another world: with the news of the occupation of İzmir still fresh, he was landing at a port on a coast where there were Greek bands and British officers.\n\n■ AFTER SAMSUN\nThe next day he reported to the grand vizierate in two telegrams the state of occupation in the region and the reaction against the occupation of İzmir. In his first report, on 22 May, he conveyed information about some forty Greek bands in the region and about their officers' meetings with British officers: the British had said that the protection of a foreign state was necessary for salvation, and they had been told in reply that Turkishness would not tolerate foreign intervention. Realising that he could not work in safety in Samsun, on 24 May he moved his headquarters to Havza. His relations with the government soon deteriorated. The Amasya Circular, which he signed on the night of 21-22 June with Ali Fuat, Rauf and Refet Beys and for which he obtained the assent of Kâzım Karabekir and Mersinli Cemal Pasha, began with the words The integrity of the homeland and the independence of the nation are in danger, and provided for convening a congress at Sivas after that of Erzurum. According to the TDV, this was the first proclamation of the National Struggle.", "eb7432ee"]},
"p77b-sakarya-meydan-muharebesi-1921":{"baslik": ["The Battle of Sakarya: the twenty-two-day war that turned back at the gates of Ankara", "ed23e755"], "tarih_metin": ["23 August - 12/13 September 1921 (the closing day varies between sources; see below)", "2ab047fa"], "yer": ["East of the Sakarya River, towards Polatlı and Ankara", "0943075c"], "oncesi": ["King Constantine came to Izmir on 13 June 1921 to take command of the army; Greece, which had raised its strength in Anatolia to eleven divisions, rejected the Allies' offer of mediation and went on the offensive on 10 July. Afyon and Kütahya fell; the Turkish army, caught unprepared, evacuated Eskişehir as well on the orders of Mustafa Kemal Pasha and withdrew east of the Sakarya. The Greeks proclaimed this a victory, an atmosphere like mourning prevailed in Ankara, and in the Assembly there was criticism that Mustafa Kemal was not at the head of the army. Mustafa Kemal said that he could accept the office of commander-in-chief on condition that all the powers of the Assembly were given to him for three months; despite the objections of some deputies, the Law on the Supreme Command was passed on 5 August 1921, and his orders acquired the force of law. Under the ten National Obligations (Tekâlif-i Milliye) orders issued from 7 August onwards, 40 per cent of the people's food and clothing and 20 per cent of their means of transport and animals were collected for the army. The diplomatic ground was also shifting: on 9 August the Paris Conference decided on neutrality, and Lloyd George acknowledged that Sèvres had been torn up. According to the academic source, the Greek army had also changed its strategy: while part of the northern wing kept the Turkish army occupied, the rest would join the forces in the south and deliver a powerful blow.", "a9939c0a"], "akis": ["The Greek forces moved on 13 August; King Constantine was so sure of himself that he invited the British liaison officers to a banquet he would give in Ankara. The Greek army, encountering the Turkish units west of the Sakarya, launched its attack on 23 August. The commander-in-chief gave his armies the principle of the battle in this order: There is no line of defence, there is an area of defence; and that area is the entire homeland. Not a single inch of the homeland may be abandoned to the enemy until it has been soaked with the blood of its citizens. The plan was to wear the enemy down and finally, concentrating the forces at one point, to deliver the necessary blow. The situation at the front was so critical that preparations began in Ankara to move government offices to Kayseri. In the middle of the battle Mustafa Kemal fell from his horse and broke his collarbone; although ten days' rest was recommended, he returned to the front the next day. According to the academic source, Papoulas could not get past the point that would have taken him to Ankara, and the Greek army was driven back west of the Sakarya by the Turkish counter-attack.", "a53493f3"], "sonuc": ["The Greek units withdrew to the Eskişehir-Afyon line. According to the academic source, the Greek army lost 20,000 soldiers and Papoulas resigned. On 19 September 1921 the Assembly unanimously conferred on Mustafa Kemal Pasha the rank of marshal and the title of gazi; in his speech of thanks he said that the real recipient of the reward was the army. The diplomatic consequence of the victory came quickly: the Ankara Agreement signed with France on 20 October 1921 meant that one of the Allies had for the first time accepted Turkish national demands and officially recognised the Ankara government; the French withdrew from Anatolia. The withdrawal of the Italians also accelerated, and Britain was left as the sole supporter of the Greek army.", "b4595b6b"], "tartisma": ["There are two numerical discrepancies. ① The closing day: two separate TDV articles (National Struggle and Mustafa Kemal Atatürk) write that the battle ended on 12 September with a heavy defeat of the enemy; the academic encyclopaedia of the First World War gives the battle as 23 August - 13 September 1921. According to our source rule, the TDV is the one to be taken as the basis; 13 September, however, is in common use and appears in the chronology. Both days may be dating the process by which the Greek army withdrew west of the Sakarya; the sources consulted do not explain the difference. ② Duration: the TDV says twenty-one days and twenty-one nights, the academic source twenty-two days. The difference appears to arise from whether the closing day and the first day are counted. The sources consulted do not give the numbers or losses of the Turkish forces.", "7cb0ab3c"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Army of the Grand National Assembly of Turkey", "1f84715f"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Commander-in-Chief Mustafa Kemal Pasha · Chief of the General Staff Fevzi Pasha · Commander of the Western Army İsmet Pasha", "d84c039e"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the sources consulted", "3af0cfca"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Greek army", "62d0bc46"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["King Constantine (took command of the army in June 1921) · Commander-in-Chief General Anastasios Papoulas", "9ac5fd78"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["eleven divisions (TDV)", "40a6a303"]},
"p77b-sarikamis-harekati-1914":{"baslik": ["The Sarıkamış Operation: what was its aim, why was it carried out in winter, and how was it lost?", "ea7e64da"], "tarih_metin": ["Order to attack on the night of 19 December 1914 · operation began on the morning of 22 December 1914 · order to retreat on 4 January 1915", "f0c7e368"], "yer": ["Caucasus front — Köprüköy, Oltu, Bardız (Gaziler), the Allahüekber Mountains, Sarıkamış", "33f3efa1"], "oncesi": ["On 1 November 1914 the Russians crossed the border and advanced towards Erzurum; the Third Army stopped them near Köprüköy, and the fighting ceased at the end of November. This was the whole of the task the German headquarters expected of the Ottomans: to tie down the Russian forces on the Caucasus front and prevent their transfer to Europe. According to the TDV, the army had already fulfilled this task. But Enver Pasha believed that weak enemy forces which could not be defeated by frontal warfare could be destroyed by an encircling operation; Bronsart von Schellendorf, the German chief of the general staff of the Turkish armies, and other German officers also supported this idea. The objective was not merely military: the operation order envisaged that the whole of the Caucasus would be freed from the Muscovite yoke. Why in winter? Because it was precisely in winter that the Russians expected no operations — owing to the extreme cold, snow and blizzards they had even reduced their observation. The plan rested on this surprise. The army commander, Hasan İzzet Pasha, however, opposed a winter offensive because of the lack of winter kit and equipment. Hâfız Hakkı Bey, Deputy Chief of the General Staff, who examined the situation on the spot, reported that an encirclement with one corps attacking frontally and two corps going by way of Bardız-Oltu could succeed. On 6 December Enver Pasha left Istanbul aboard the battleship Yavuz and landed at Trebizond, and on 15 December he reached army headquarters at Köprüköy; when Hasan İzzet Pasha resigned on the night of 18 December, he took command himself. The supply picture was bad from the outset: the nearest Ottoman railway station, Ulukışla, was 600 km behind the front, ox-carts and pack animals were not enough, and women and children carried provisions to the front on their backs. The ships carrying 100,000 sets of winter clothing had been sunk by the Russian fleet in the Black Sea. The Russians, by contrast, received everything they needed by the Tiflis-Gyumri (Gümrü)-Kars-Sarıkamış railway.", "00923607"], "akis": ["The plan had three prongs. The 11th Corps, attacking frontally, was to pin down the main Russian force in the Aras valley. The 10th Corps, in the direction of Narman-Oltu-Bardız, and the 9th Corps, in the direction of Pitgir-Çatak-Kötek, were to swing round from the north, cut the road to Kars and encircle the Russian army from the rear. The objective was Sarıkamış: the last station on the railway, the forward supply base of the Russian army and the junction of the lines of retreat to Kars — and, since it lay 50 km behind the front, almost undefended. The operation began on the morning of 22 December with high morale; snow and fog concealed the columns. The 25,000-strong 9th Corps, which Enver Pasha accompanied, turned directly towards Sarıkamış on the second day, as the Kötek road was blocked by snow. The 30,000-strong 10th Corps drove back the Oltu group; the provisions captured at Oltu and Bardız relieved the hunger for a time. The Russians, not realising that they were being encircled, fell upon the 11th Corps with all their strength. On the evening of 25 December, at the Bardız Pass, 4-5 km from Sarıkamış, the vanguard of the 9th Corps drove back with the bayonet a Russian detachment of 2,000 men hastily put together from depot troops and railway workers; the Russians were preparing to burn the station depots and evacuate the town. At precisely this moment the corps commander, Ali İhsan Latif Pasha, halted the attack in order to rest his exhausted units, which were not accustomed to night assaults. According to the TDV, this decision reversed the course of the operation at a stroke. On the night of 24 December the Russians had decided to reinforce Sarıkamış; the relief units entered from the evening of 25 December, and on the morning of 26 December the Russian force in the town had doubled to more than 4,000. Many of the Turkish soldiers who spent the night in the forest froze to death before they saw the morning. The attack of 26 December was halted by the fire of two field guns that happened to be in the town. The 10th Corps, for its part, departed from the plan and headed with one division towards Sarıkamış and with two divisions, in pursuit of the Oltu detachment, towards the northern slopes of the Allahüekber Mountains; in the fourteen-hour climb over the Allahüekber, soldiers fell in the snow with their rifles in their arms and died. On 27 December the Sarıkamış-Kars railway was destroyed near Selim, and the Russian commander Myshlayevsky, falling into despair, handed command to Yudenich and returned to Tiflis. In Istanbul a great victory was proclaimed. Yet on 28 December the encirclement was complete only on paper: the offensive strength had been exhausted by hunger, cold and frostbite, and in Sarıkamış there were now 15,000 Russian combatants and thirty-four guns. The attack, which had got as far as street fighting, was forced to fall back. On 1 January 1915 Yudenich launched a counter-encirclement in the Bardız-Sarıkamış-Eşekmeydanı triangle; Enver Pasha left Sarıkamış on 3 January; on 4 January Hâfız Hakkı Pasha gave the order to retreat.", "0fa2cd1f"], "sonuc": ["Very few were able to return. The commander of the 9th Corps, Ali İhsan Latif Pasha, was taken prisoner together with three divisional commanders and his entire headquarters; according to Yudenich's diary, some 20,000 Turkish soldiers, about 200 of them officers, were taken prisoner. In the records of 8 February 1915 there were 15,808 sick and wounded in the hospitals; typhus claimed many lives — Hâfız Hakkı Pasha too died of this disease at Erzurum (12 February 1915). Russian losses were also heavy: according to Yudenich, 26,000, some of them from freezing. The Ottoman press hid the truth from the public by denying the reports of prisoners. The TDV's own verdict is this: Sarıkamış should take its place in history not through exaggerated casualty figures but as an indication that Ottoman military power was squandered for the sake of the allies' war aims.", "035ce9d5"], "tartisma": ["The sources diverge on three points. ① The number of losses: the TDV's article on Sarıkamış states that the widespread figure of 90,000 martyrs is not correct; the martyrs who, after the snow melted, were buried in mass graves under Russian supervision, with their number inscribed at the head of each grave, amount to 23,000 (18,000 of them around Sarıkamış), and together with those who could not be reached and those who fell in the Aras valley the total is about 30,000. Yet the TDV's article on Enver Pasha writes that the very greater part of an army of 90,000 was lost, either freezing to death or being killed by the Russians; an academic encyclopaedia of the First World War also gives losses of between 78,000 and 90,000 (30,000 of them from freezing) for the whole operation. The difference arises partly from definition: on one side, martyrs who were buried; on the other, total casualties including prisoners, the sick and the missing. ② The starting day: the article on Sarıkamış has the operation begin on 22 December, while the article on Enver Pasha says 18 December (18 December is the day on which Enver Pasha took command). ③ Responsibility: the TDV names the critical mistakes — the halting of the attack on the night of 25 December and the 10th Corps' departure from the plan; the article on Enver Pasha, however, places the main burden on his excessively aggressive venture.", "bc995a51"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman Third Army", "9939f780"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Minister of War Enver Pasha (took over command of the army in person on 18 December) · corps: 9th Ali İhsan Latif Pasha, 10th Hâfız Hakkı Bey, 11th Abdülkerim Pasha", "528f4855"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["96,550 combatants excluding gendarmerie and line-of-communication services (ATASE record, TDV)", "ee19f0a3"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Russian Caucasus Army", "6e782ede"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["General Myshlayevsky, then General Yudenich", "acaab900"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["Sarıkamış and Oltu groups, about 65,000 (TDV)", "f04414d1"]},
"p77b-sirp-hirvat-sloven-kralligi-yugoslavya-1918":{"baslik": ["The Kingdom of the Serbs, Croats and Slovenes: how was Yugoslavia founded, and why did it crack from the start?", "68326557"], "sebep.b": ["The dissolution of the Austro-Hungarian Empire and the decisions of the South Slav assemblies to join Serbia", "6d99c153"], "sonuc.b": ["The founding of the Kingdom of the Serbs, Croats and Slovenes (named the Kingdom of Yugoslavia after 1929)", "396f82d1"], "bag": ["Serbia, one of the states that profited most from the war, united with the Slav lands that had broken away from the empire; but the question of what kind of state the union would create was postponed.", "71b45a53"], "metin": ["■ The root of the idea\nThe name Yugoslavia means the south (jug) Slavs. The idea was born in the 1830s from the Illyrian movement, which Croatian intellectuals founded against the Habsburg Monarchy's policy of Magyarisation and Germanisation. The movement's thesis rested on language: the South Slavs speak the same language in different dialects and belong to the same or similar peoples, and therefore have the right to unite. In 1850 in Vienna, Croatian, Serbian and Slovene intellectuals agreed to adopt a common Serbo-Croatian written language. But the aims were different: the Croats envisaged union within the Habsburg realm or in an independent South Slav state, while Serbia wished first to unite all Serbs.\n\n■ THE WAR: THE IDEA BECOMES A GOAL\nIn the first stage of the First World War, Yugoslavism became the official programme of the Serbian government; the government worked together with the Yugoslav Committee, which Croat, Serb and Slovene politicians who had fled from the Habsburgs founded in Italy and which was active in London. On 20 July 1917 the Corfu Declaration was adopted between the Serbian prime minister Nikola Pašić and the Yugoslav delegation: the new state would be the Kingdom of the Serbs, Croats and Slovenes, it would proclaim a constitutional and parliamentary monarchy, the Karađorđević dynasty would stand at its head, and three separate nations would be recognised.\n\n■ AUTUMN 1918: THE DECISIONS ON UNION\nIn October 1918 the National Council of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs was formed in Zagreb; on 29 October the Croatian Sabor severed all ties with Vienna and Budapest. In Montenegro the Podgorica assembly decided to put an end to the Petrović-Njegoš dynasty and join Serbia; similar decisions were taken in Bosnia and Herzegovina and in Novi Sad (for Baranja, Bačka and Banat). In the end, on 1 December 1918 the Kingdom of the Serbs, Croats and Slovenes, governed under a constitution, was established. According to information from the founding period, a population of 12 million lived on a territory of about 250,000 km².\n\n■ THE CRACK THAT WAS THERE FROM THE START\nThe TDV openly enumerates the source of the problem: in this state, in which the king was a Serb, the strengthening in the 1920s of the Serbs' privileged position over the other nations, the fact that the Croats and Slovenes were Catholics, and the remaining of Croatia and Slovenia under Serbian domination led to internal disorder. No element other than the three nations promised at Corfu was officially recognised: Macedonia was divided up under the name of Southern Serbia, the Albanians and Macedonians in Kosovo and Macedonia were not counted as separate elements, and the Muslim Bosniaks of Bosnia and Herzegovina were registered as Serbs or Croats. In 1928 a Serbian Radical deputy shot three Croat deputies in parliament. On 6 January 1929 King Aleksandar abolished the constitution and established a royal dictatorship, and on 3 October 1929 he changed the name of the state to the Kingdom of Yugoslavia; on 9 October 1934 he was killed in Marseilles in an assassination organised by the Croatian Ustaše and Macedonian radicals.", "525b6044"]},
"p77b-son-meclis-i-mebusan-neden-dagitildi":{"baslik": ["Why was the last Ottoman Chamber of Deputies dissolved?", "eef1b841"], "sebep.b": ["The proclamation of the National Pact by a parliament in which the supporters of the Defence of Rights predominated, and the official occupation of Istanbul on 16 March", "07b89d8b"], "sonuc.b": ["The dissolution of the Chamber of Deputies by imperial decree and, twelve days later, the opening of the Grand National Assembly in Ankara", "c22035fd"], "bag": ["The decree that closed the parliament was the sultan's, but what in effect ended it were the occupation forces; the parliament itself had already suspended its sessions in protest.", "6fc7aafe"], "metin": ["■ The short life of a parliament\nThe fourth and last elections of the Second Constitutional Era were held in a troubled atmosphere. The government of Ali Rıza Pasha (2 October 1919), which was on good terms with the National Forces in Anatolia, decided to hold elections. The Greeks and Armenians did not take part in the elections of the first half of December 1919. The parliament opened on 12 January 1920 with seventy-two deputies who had been able to reach Istanbul under wartime conditions; Vahideddin did not attend the opening, pleading illness, while Mustafa Kemal Pasha sent a message of congratulation to the presidency of the Chamber. The majority of the parliament were supporters of the Defence of Rights.\n\n■ WHY IT BECAME A TARGET: THE NATIONAL PACT\nThe most important achievement of this parliament was the proclamation of the National Pact (Misak-ı Milli): the text, signed by 121 deputies on 28 January, was read in the Chamber on 17 February 1920 and adopted unanimously, translated into French and sent to all governments and parliaments. This document, the Ottoman parliament's answer to the peace proposals of the victorious powers, met with a hostile reaction.\n\n■ THE THREE STEPS OF THE DISSOLUTION\n① OCCUPATION AND RAID: The Entente powers officially occupied Istanbul on 16 March; the British raided the parliament, arrested a number of Defence of Rights deputies, headed by Rauf (Orbay) Bey, and exiled them to Lemnos and Malta.\n② THE PARLIAMENT'S OWN DECISION: According to the TDV, the parliament, wishing to assert its existence, suspended its sessions of its own will on 18 March. In other words, the parliament refused to function as a token institution under occupation.\n③ THE SULTAN'S DECREE: On 5 April Damad Ferid Pasha became grand vizier again; on 8 April he visited the British admiral de Robeck, said that the national forces could be suppressed only by force, and asked for help. In the same days as his proclamation declaring the leaders of the National Forces rebels, the Chamber of Deputies was dissolved by imperial decree on 11 April 1920.\n\n■ THE AFTERMATH\nTwelve days later, on 23 April 1920, the Grand National Assembly opened in Ankara, and some of the deputies in Istanbul moved to Ankara and joined the new assembly. Thus the last link in the Ottoman parliamentary tradition and the first parliament of the new state were joined to each other.\n\n■ A PARALLEL\nThe first Ottoman Chamber of Deputies had also closed at a time when a foreign army was standing before the capital: at the end of the War of '93, on 13 February 1878, Abdülhamid II had suspended the parliament indefinitely, using the power the constitution gave him. The difference between the two closures is that in 1920 the parliament left behind a national programme before it closed.", "15a0690f"]},
"p80-saray-ovasi-ilhak-1448":{"baslik": ["The annexation of the Saray plain (1448) — a plain taken before the kingdom fell", "fac39fee"], "metin": ["The plain on which present-day Sarajevo stands came under Ottoman administration in 1448. The Kingdom of Bosnia, however, was conquered only fifteen years later, in 1463. How could a plain right in the middle of a kingdom become Ottoman before the kingdom had fallen?\n\n■ THE LANDS IN BETWEEN: NOT HOSTILE, BUT VASSAL\nThe key to this question is the relationship of the surrounding states with the Ottomans. According to the TDV, after the accession of King Tvrtko II (1420-1443), the Bosnian kings were made tributary in 1428-1429 by the Ottomans, who had seized many towns and placed garrisons in them. Stjepan Tomaš, on the throne from 1443, also continued to pay tribute to the Ottomans, although he relied on the support of the West. According to the TDV, the Serbian Despotate to the south-east was likewise an Ottoman vassal; in times of war it sent auxiliary troops to the Ottomans, and between 1427 and 1456 it was ruled by Đurađ Branković. In other words, most of the lands between the Ottoman frontier march at Skopje and the Saray plain were not a foreign and hostile country, but the lands of vassal lordships paying tribute to the Ottomans.\n\n■ ANNEXATION STEP BY STEP\nAccording to the TDV article on Sarajevo, the Ottomans first, between 1428 and 1435, seized the town of Hodidjed near the present city. The vilayet of Hodidjed, together with the Saray plain, came completely under Ottoman administration in 852 (1448). The region, along with the suburb called Vrhbosna, became a strong frontier zone and was administered by Isa Bey, son of İshak Bey and bey of Skopje, who bore the title of voivode. Since there were Bosnian lords around who were Ottoman vassals, the region was under control from two sides.\n\n■ THE RULING FAMILY: FROM SKOPJE TO BOSNIA\nThe geographical path of the annexation can be read in the career of one family. İshak Bey was the adopted son of Pasha Yiğit, the conqueror of Skopje, and had taken over the administration of Skopje. His son Isa Bey fought together with his father in Kosovo, Albania, Macedonia, Serbia and Bosnia; according to the TDV, it is mentioned in the correspondence of Dubrovnik that the family was related to the Kosača and Pavlović families, aristocratic houses of Bosnia. In other words, the Saray plain was administered as a branch of the Skopje frontier march extending to the north-west.\n\n■ FROM PLAIN TO CITY\nIn the land survey register (tahrir defteri) dated 1455 the Saray plain appears as the name of a district, but there was not yet any settlement of that name. Before 1462 İshak Bey or his son Isa Bey founded the first Muslim quarter here; İshak Bey had a palace (saray) built and, in the name of the Conqueror, the Hünkâr Mosque (862/1458). Because of this first Ottoman palace, built in the village of Brodec, the place began to be called Saray, the Saray plain or the town of Saray; according to Evliya Çelebi, when the name of the River Bosna was added, \"Bosna-Saray\" came into being. When Bosnia was conquered in 1463, this place became one of the four vilayets of the new sanjak of Bosnia, and Isa Bey served as sanjak governor of Bosnia from 1463 to 1469. Isa Bey is remembered as the first founder of Sarajevo.\n\n■ CONCLUSION\nThe sources consulted do not speak of a directly administered corridor linking the Saray plain to the Ottoman mainland; the lands in between belonged to the vassal Bosnian and Serbian lordships that paid tribute to the Ottomans. Establishing a directly administered frontier zone within a vassal kingdom was preparation for conquering that kingdom later, and fifteen years afterwards that is exactly what happened.", "c32a8ad5"]},
"p80-sehzade-halil-sebep-etki-sonuc":{"baslik": ["The abduction of Prince Halil (1357) — causes and consequences", "f84d9cd3"], "metin": ["The abduction in 1357 of Halil, Orhan Gazi's young son, looks at first sight like a pirate raid. The TDV article on Orhan, however, reads it as one of the most powerful diplomatic cards in Byzantium's hand.\n\n■ CAUSES\n① The Ottoman presence in Rumelia: by taking Çimbi and Gallipoli, the Ottomans had established themselves on the Rumelian side; people coming from Karesi had begun to found villages there. For Byzantium this was a permanent threat at the gates of the capital. ② The Byzantine civil war: until then Orhan had carried out his expansion in Rumelia in cooperation with the Kantakouzenos family; Emperor John V, however, was fighting for the throne against Kantakouzenos's son Matthew and wanted to detach Orhan from this alliance. ③ The hope of a Crusade: in 1355 John V had applied to the pope for a Crusade against the Turks; the papal legate Pierre Thomas was in Istanbul in 1357. Byzantium aimed, with Crusader help, to cut off the Straits by sea and destroy the Turks in Rumelia, and for this it needed to gain time. ④ Orhan's weak point: Orhan was old and ill; Halil was his much-loved son by Theodora. According to the TDV, the course of events shows that the abduction was a plot by the Byzantine court to force Orhan to make peace.\n\n■ EFFECTS — WHILE THE CAPTIVITY LASTED (1357-1359)\nTo rescue his son, Orhan signed a treaty with the emperor: he would halt every kind of attack on Byzantine territory in Thrace, bear the cost of the ships to be sent to Phocaea to rescue Halil, cancel the debts the emperor had incurred up to that time, and give up helping Matthew Kantakouzenos and support Emperor John instead. According to the TDV, this treaty put the handful of Ottomans on the Rumelian side in a desperate position; Ottoman expansion in Rumelia stopped until Halil was rescued. In those same days Süleyman Pasha, the commander-in-chief in Rumelia, died as the result of an accident or an assassination; according to the TDV, before his death Süleyman had fiercely resisted proposals to evacuate Gallipoli. Prince Murad, sent in his place, waited inactive together with his tutor (lala) Şahin until Halil was rescued. Left on his own, Matthew Kantakouzenos was captured by the Serbs in 1358 and handed over to the emperor; the situation in Thrace turned in Istanbul's favour.\n\n■ CONSEQUENCES\n① A costly rescue for the Ottomans: Orhan paid a ransom of 30,000 Venetian gold coins and accepted Byzantium's new terms. ② Byzantium's short-lived gain: the emperor betrothed Halil to his daughter Irene and obtained a promise that he would succeed to the throne after Orhan; he hoped through Halil for a period of peace and balance. ③ The promise did not hold: the Ottomans had no law of succession, and Halil's status as heir was forgotten. Prince Murad in Rumelia was opposed to this policy; together with the ghazi beys from Karesi and his tutor, he was determined on a policy of holy war (ghaza) and expansion. ④ The resumption of the conquests: as soon as Halil was handed over in September-October 1359, a systematic campaign of conquest began in Thrace under the command of Murad and Lala Şahin; this campaign extended to the conquest of Edirne. ⑤ The throne: when Orhan died in 1362, Halil's men moved to take the throne, but Çandarlı Kara Halil, the qadi of Bursa, took control of the situation and Murad came to the throne.\n\n■ ASSESSMENT\nThe two years that Byzantium gained also brought the Crusader help it had expected: according to the TDV article on Murad, in 1359 the Crusader fleet of the Papacy and Byzantium tried to make a landing at Lapseki and in the Gulf of Saros, but Prince Murad in Rumelia prevented this landing. The abduction halted the Ottoman advance for two years; it did not change its direction.", "a92088f1"]},
"p80b-tahrir-defterleri-nasil-okunur":{"baslik": ["The tahrir register: who wrote it, what did they write, how does the historian read it — and why is the 1431 register 'the oldest'?", "8af60b60"], "metin": ["■ What it is\nIn the dictionary, tahrir means writing, entering into a register. As a term in Ottoman finance it denotes the surveys carried out to establish, name by name, the taxes and the people who paid them, and the registers in which these surveys were recorded. Censuses were also known in Rome, in medieval Europe and in other Islamic states; according to TDV, what set the Ottoman tahrir apart was that it was carried out on a general scale and was distinctive in its detail and order.\n\n■ WHY IT WAS KEPT\nIn the Ottoman documents' own words, the purpose was for the state to know where the reaya (tax-paying subjects) lived and every particular of their work, the sources of their goods and produce, the revenues of the timar holders, and the disputes between the reaya and the timar holders. Alongside this, the aims also included preventing practices contrary to the law (bid'at), knowing the number of households when the avarız levy was imposed, and setting out the condition of the endowments. The tahrir was directly tied to the timar system: it was carried out only in the provinces and sanjaks where the timar system was applied (those without salyane); it was not carried out in salyane provinces such as Egypt, Yemen and Baghdad, or in the hükümet sanjaks governed by local lords. The sentence most often repeated in the orders sent to the provincial surveyors was that not a single person and not a single grain should be left uncounted.\n\n■ WHEN\nRepetition every thirty years is cited as the general rule, but TDV stresses that this figure does not fully reflect reality. It was also customary to survey a region immediately after its conquest, to carry out a general survey on the accession of a new sultan, and to survey in periods of financial difficulty in order to find revenues that had not been entered in the registers. Because the conquest survey relied on older records, it was sometimes repeated within a few years. In Hungary it has been established that a survey was carried out roughly every ten years between 1540 and 1590.\n\n■ WHO KEPT IT\nA commission consisting of a high-ranking emin (provincial surveyor, muharrir) appointed from the centre and the clerk accompanying him went out on inspection together with the holders of dirlik (revenue grants) or their representatives and the kadis; the kadis were obliged to help the emin while their own districts were being recorded. The emins were experienced statesmen: in the 15th century Timurtaş Bey, Umur Bey, Mihaloğlu Ali Bey and Tursun Bey; in the 16th century, for example, the former nişancı Celalzade Mustafa. The clerks were chosen from among those of the finance department (defterdarlık), the Defterhane (registry) or the standing army (kapıkulu) corps. The commission worked with a copy of the old register in hand: it struck off the dead, added those who had come of age, and entered changes in marital status and physical condition. For the tax on produce the average of the last three years was taken, but the value in akçe and the total revenue figure of the villages were determined at the centre. The emin and the clerk collected two akçe each from every household. Under normal circumstances the work took one or two years.\n\n■ WHICH SERIES\nThree kinds of register came out of a survey. ① MUFASSAL (detailed): the towns and villages of the sanjak, the name and father's name of every adult male in a village (Ali son of Mehmed), his landholding status (çift, nim çift, ekinlü bennak, caba bennak) and marital status, the village's taxes (resm-i çift, ispence, tithes on wheat, barley, vineyards and honey, occasional revenues such as bad-ı heva), the numbers of sheep and beehives, mezraas (outlying fields) and mills. ② İCMAL (summary): the names of the dirlik holders, which are not found in the detailed register, and their revenue totals; from the end of the 15th century arranged by rank, beginning with the sultan's has estates. ③ EVKAF (endowments): the revenue of places assigned to a vakıf and where this revenue was spent. Of the two fair copies made, one was kept in the Defterhane and the other at the office of the beylerbeyi. The new register was called defter-i cedid, the preceding one defter-i atik, and the one before that defter-i köhne. Secrecy was fundamental in the Defterhane records; the maxim reported by TDV runs: one may give up one's head, but one may not reveal the secret. Today the Ottoman Archive holds 1110 registers in the Tapu-Tahrir catalogue and 851 in the Defterhane-i Amire catalogue, and the Kuyud-ı Kadime (Old Records) Archive of the Land Registry and Cadastre holds 203 detailed, 162 summary and fifty-one endowment registers.\n\n■ WHY IT ENDED\nAt the end of the 16th century, when two-front wars, the Celali rebellions, peasants abandoning their homes, the growing importance of soldiers with firearms and the central treasury's need for cash weakened the timar, the classical tahrir was abandoned as well. According to Selaniki, Ferhad Pasha banned provincial surveys during his first grand vizierate (1591-1592). Its place was taken by avarız surveys; the first independent avarız register is that of Tokat, dated 1600-1601. The population surveys carried out for Mahmud II's new army, for their part, are regarded as the beginning of modern censuses.\n\n■ WHY THE 1431 REGISTER IS 'THE OLDEST'\nThe claim belongs to TDV's entry on the tahrir, and its sentence must be read carefully: although it is not known for certain on what date the Ottomans began surveys of this kind, it is understood from the Register of the Sanjak of Arvanid dated 835 (1431), which contains the oldest survey to have come down to our day, and from certain other documents that the register procedure existed in the 14th century. That is, the epithet 'oldest' does not describe the first time a survey was carried out but the fact that the register HAS SURVIVED TO OUR DAY; older surveys were carried out, but their registers have not reached us. TDV's entry on Albania also links the oldest records relating to Albania under Ottoman rule to the surveys made after Bayezid I's campaigns of 1394 and 1397. According to TDV, vilayets such as Ergirikasrı, Klisura, Kanina, Berat, İskarapar and Akçahisar were attached to the sanjak of Arvanid-ili. The register also shows the change that the registers of the early period underwent: here the military obligations of the dirlik holders are recorded; this information is not found in the later summary registers. The register was published by Halil İnalcık in Ankara in 1954 under the title Hicrî 835 Tarihli Sûret-i Defter-i Sancak-i Arvanid.\n\n■ ITS VALUE FOR THE HISTORIAN — AND ITS LIMITS\nAccording to TDV, the tahrir registers were first studied systematically by Ömer Lütfi Barkan, who demonstrated their agricultural, social and demographic importance; the Hüdavendigâr Livası Tahrir Defterleri (Survey Registers of the Sanjak of Hüdavendigar), published in 1988 with Barkan's unfinished assessment at the beginning, is regarded as an example of how these registers should be published. The registers are first-hand sources because they do not narrate events: they are records the state wrote for its own business, village by village and name by name; no other source tells us with this density that a village existed in 1431, how many men and how many sheep it had, and what it produced. But TDV itself issues four warnings together. ① The tax in the register is not the tax collected but the ESTIMATED tax. ② Those recorded are the tax-paying adult males (and in some places widows who headed households) and the exempt; women and children are not counted — the total population is derived by estimate. ③ It was common for the reaya to conceal their produce, to leave their village and so escape being entered in the register, and for timar holders to hide their reaya from the survey. ④ Measures, weights and terms vary from sanjak to sanjak; a register can be read only together with the kanunname (law code) at its head. Comparison of the summary registers with the detailed ones has also shown that significant errors were made when entries were copied into the summary registers.", "006b3bd5"], "kisa": ["Tahrir registers are an inventory of a sanjak village by village, man by man, field by field; they are the richest source of the classical Ottoman era, but they record not the actual tax but the estimated tax, and not the whole population but the tax-paying men.", "059a9f97"], "not": ["The chronology entry to which this card is linked carries only the year (835/1431); the sources read do not give the month or day of the register. TDV's entry on Albania does not explain whether the records belonging to the surveys of 1394 and 1397 survive as a separate register or as a reference in a later document; this card does not count them as 'a register that has survived to our day'.", "18b5a895"]},
"padisah2-abdulaziz-gures-ney-sirt":{"baslik": ["Abdülaziz: The wrestling heir apparent, the ney-playing composer and the sultan who said 'let it pass over my back'", "db049721"], "metin": ["During the reign of his elder brother Abdülmecid, Abdülaziz lived a fairly free life as heir apparent. At his villa in Kurbağalıdere he went hunting, wrestled, swam and played jereed (cirit, a mounted javelin game). He led a simple life, far from drink and dissipation. With his powerful and imposing build, he became the hope of those who were weary of his brother's extravagance. Some saw him as a future Selim the Grim.\n\nMusic was his real passion. He had taken lessons from the ney player and composer Yusuf Pasha. He played the ney and the lute very well. He also has a place among the Ottoman sultans as a composer. He loved the hicazkâr and şehnaz modes (makams) so much that many songs and marches of his time were composed in these modes. Of his own works, one hicaz sirto and two songs have survived to the present day. On coming to the throne he abolished the palace orchestra and bands that Abdülmecid had established and replaced them with an ensemble of Turkish music. Instead of opera and theatre he watched orta oyunu (traditional Turkish improvised theatre). But on returning from his European tour in 1867 he changed his mind and had an orchestra and a band set up in the palace again.\n\nTDV also writes that he painted in his spare time. It is clear from his works that he was also a good calligrapher in the celi thuluth script.\n\nHis best-known saying, however, dates from a dispute over a railway. There were objections to the line that was to connect Istanbul to Paris passing through the palace garden. Abdülaziz replied that, so long as the railway was built, 'let it pass over my back if it likes'. During his reign the railway network in the country rose from 452 kilometres to 1344 kilometres.", "e5da85db"]},
"padisah2-abdulhamid1-halil-hamid-komplosu":{"baslik": ["Abdülhamid I: Forty-three years in the kafes, a dismissal in the middle of the night and the Halil Hamid Pasha plot", "f906ad11"], "metin": ["Abdülhamid I was five years old when his father Ahmed III was deposed in 1730. He lived under surveillance in the palace until the death of his elder brother Mustafa III. When he came to the throne on 21 January 1774, at the age of forty-nine, he had, in TDV's words, no experience whatever in state affairs. He had spent forty-three years of his life in closed apartments.\n\nThe darkest event of his reign is the end of Halil Hamid Pasha, one of his most successful grand viziers. The sultan had repeatedly shown his satisfaction with the pasha's service. Yet on 31 March 1785 the pasha was suddenly dismissed towards dawn, exiled to Gallipoli and had all his property confiscated. He was first given the province of Habesh, but while he was making preparations for the journey on Tenedos and Kos he was removed from this post as well. He was executed on 27 April 1785. His severed head was brought to Istanbul and displayed at the palace gate.\n\nThis sudden anger suggested that there was an extraordinary reason behind it. According to the common explanation, the pasha had planned to depose the elderly sultan and replace him with the young Prince Selim, that is, the nephew who would later be Selim III. The Shaykh al-Islam, a former agha of the Janissaries and a vizier who were found to be involved in the plan were removed from office. Two of them were later killed. This strengthened the allegation.\n\nBut Kemal Beydilli's article in TDV looks for the real reason elsewhere. His silence in the face of Russia's annexation of the Crimea had angered public opinion. His reforms of military muster inspections had set a powerful group against him. The sultan's favourite, Cezayirli Gazi Hasan Pasha, was also his enemy. The accusation of 'deposing the sultan' may have been nothing more than a pretext.", "2230a3d1"], "kisa": ["He dismissed his favoured grand vizier one day towards dawn, and a few weeks later had his head brought to him. The grounds were a plan to depose him and bring in his nephew Selim. But did this plan really exist?", "ba6f5df6"]},
"padisah2-abdulmecid-elcilik-balosu":{"baslik": ["Abdülmecid: The first sultan to attend an embassy ball and to accept a foreign decoration", "481c7a48"], "metin": ["In TDV's words, Abdülmecid was brought up 'just like a European prince'. He learned enough French to speak it and to understand what he read. He followed European publications and held discussions with the foreigners he met. He admired Western music and the Western way of life.\n\nThe years of the Crimean War were the years in which he broke with palace custom. It was not customary for foreign ambassadors to discuss political matters directly with the sultan. Abdülmecid began to receive ambassadors by appointment. The British ambassador Stratford Canning, however, could enter without an appointment. His influence was so great that diplomats called him the 'uncrowned sultan'. Until that day, visits by members of European dynasties had not been returned. Abdülmecid, however, went in person to the French embassy and returned the visit of Prince Napoleon. Ottoman sultans bestowed decorations but did not accept them. He accepted the Légion d'Honneur from Napoleon III. On this occasion, on 4 February 1856, he also attended the ball given by the French ambassador. Two weeks later the Reform Edict of 1856, which the allies wanted, was proclaimed.\n\nTDV writes that he had an emotional and touchy temperament. He readily yielded to the suggestions of his consorts, daughters and sons-in-law. In his twenty-year reign he changed grand viziers twenty-two times. He had the Dolmabahçe, Beykoz and Küçüksu palaces and pavilions built partly with foreign loans. Like his father, he died of tuberculosis, on 25 June 1861, at the age of only thirty-nine, at the Ihlamur Pavilion. One of the first acts of his brother Abdülaziz, who succeeded him, was to disband the palace orchestra he had established.", "4b91024c"]},
"padisah2-ahmed2-sakiz-hattat":{"baslik": ["Ahmed II: The calligrapher sultan who never missed the Divan and the Chios victory he did not live to see", "7d815871"], "metin": ["On the death of his elder brother Suleiman II, Ahmed II ascended the throne in Edirne on 23 June 1691, at the age of forty-nine. His years in the kafes had left him with a sensitive and irascible temperament. TDV presents him as a sultan who had a taste for poetry and music and was also a calligrapher. He also had a side that readily yielded to the suggestions of those around him. For example, under the influence of the Chief Black Eunuch, he dismissed a grand vizier returning from campaign.\n\nYet in affairs of state he showed surprising diligence. When the business of the Imperial Council (Divan-ı Hümayun) increased, he raised the council meetings from two to four days a week, in conformity with the law code of Suleiman the Lawgiver. He made it a habit to attend the meetings in person and did not give this up even during his illness. The sources write that he always put the rights of the tax-paying subjects (reaya) first. The malikâne system, under which revenue sources were leased for life in order to protect the treasury, also began in his time.\n\nThe last blow of his reign came from the sea. When the armies were shifted to the Austrian front, the island of Chios was left unprotected. In September 1694 Venetian, papal, Florentine and Maltese ships besieged and captured the island. Ahmed II was greatly grieved by this news and ordered preparations for the recovery of the island. But on 6 February 1695, at the age of fifty-two, he died of dropsy in Edirne. Sixteen days later, on 22 February 1695, Ottoman troops landed on Chios and easily retook the fortress. His body was brought to Istanbul and, like his brother's, buried in the tomb of Suleiman the Lawgiver.", "335a5c3d"]},
"padisah2-bayezid1-emir-sultan-meyhane":{"baslik": ["Bayezid I (the Thunderbolt): A dervish son-in-law, soldiers turned to dried meat and the legend of 'a tavern in each of the four corners'", "9af58a4d"], "metin": ["The son-in-law of Bayezid I the Thunderbolt was the sayyid Emir Sultan, who had come from Bukhara to Bursa. Around this marriage and the Bursa Ulu Mosque there arose some of the best-loved Ottoman pious legends. The TDV article on Emir Sultan relates them, but also distinguishes which are legend and which are history.\n\nAccording to the hagiographies (menakıb books), Bayezid's daughter Hundi Hatun, acting on dreams she had seen, married Emir Sultan without her father's consent while he was on campaign in Rumelia. On his return the enraged sultan sent a detachment of forty men to kill them both; but through the sheikh's miraculous power the soldiers became 'kadîd', that is, stiff like dried meat. It is told that the name of the Kaditler Cemetery in Bursa comes from this. It is also related that Molla Fenari wrote a letter warning the sultan; TDV considers it unlikely that this letter is a genuine document. Mecdi and Beliğ, for their part, write that the sultan gave his daughter in marriage of his own consent. According to TDV, this must be the truth.\n\nThe most famous legend concerns the Ulu Mosque. During its construction Bayezid asks his son-in-law what he thinks of the mosque. Emir Sultan says that nothing is lacking except a tavern in each of its four corners. The sultan, who understands the pointed remark, repents of drinking. Some sources attribute the same scene to Somuncu Baba.\n\nThe information outside the legend is plainer. According to TDV, Emir Sultan, together with scholars such as Molla Fenari, prevented Bayezid from having Timur's envoys killed. According to Hammer, the tradition of girding the sultans with a sword also began with his girding Bayezid the Thunderbolt with a sword.", "cb45c05d"], "kisa": ["According to the legend, Bayezid the Thunderbolt wanted to have his daughter, who had married without permission, and his son-in-law killed; at the Ulu Mosque, a single sentence from his son-in-law made him repent of drinking.", "49dcc244"]},
"padisah2-mahmud1-kuyumcu-tamburi":{"baslik": ["Mahmud I: The goldsmith in the kafes, a chess enthusiast and the sultan who gave Taksim its name", "367165f2"], "metin": ["When Mahmud I was a small child, his father Mustafa II was deposed in 1703. He too, together with his brothers, was shut up in the closed apartments of the palace. According to TDV, he spent these twenty-seven years of life in the kafes mostly occupying himself with goldsmithing. When the Patrona Halil revolt deposed his uncle Ahmed III in 1730, his turn came. According to the sources, the uncle who was the first to pledge allegiance to him also gave him a piece of advice: he should rule the state himself and trust no one.\n\nThe new sultan followed this advice. After eliminating the ringleaders of the rebels within two months, he reigned for twenty-four years. TDV presents him as a gentle, peace-loving and dignified ruler. He enjoyed jereed (cirit, mounted javelin game), horse racing and swimming, and especially outings to watch the moonlight. He was very fond of tulips and other flowers, and it is also reported that he was a chess enthusiast. He wrote poetry under the pen name 'Sebkati'. He was probably an instrumentalist who played the tanbur, and some of his compositions have survived to the present day.\n\nHis curiosity was not limited to entertainment. He summoned İbrahim Müteferrika to his presence and asked why they were losing wars and what needed to be done. Müteferrika's treatise Usulü'l-hikem was written in response to this question.\n\nHis traces in Istanbul are still alive. He had water brought to the Galata and Beyoğlu side and distributed it to more than a hundred fountains. Because of the maksem (water distribution chamber) where the water was divided, the name 'Taksim' has been in use since his reign. He had the Cağaloğlu Bath built to provide income for the Hagia Sophia Library. He opened libraries at Hagia Sophia, Fatih and Süleymaniye.", "362e7adc"]},
"padisah2-mehmed1-rehine-duzmece":{"baslik": ["Mehmed I (Çelebi): A deathbed bargain and the birth of the nickname 'Düzmece' (the Impostor)", "dfd149da"], "metin": ["When Mehmed Çelebi fell ill in the last year of his life, he had a single concern: that his eldest son Murad should come to the throne without a fight. The greatest obstacle was his own brother Mustafa. Mustafa, who had been held in Samarkand, had been released in 1416 by the Timurid ruler Shah Rukh; some Ottoman beys still regarded him as sultan, and the Byzantine emperor too held him in hand as a trump card.\n\nAccording to Halil İnalcık's articles in TDV, Mehmed made an agreement reported by the Byzantine historian Doukas: Murad would succeed his father in Edirne, his son Mustafa would take Anatolia, and his two little sons Yusuf and Mahmud, aged eight and seven, would be sent to Istanbul as hostages to Emperor Manuel II. The emperor would receive an annual payment for their upkeep and in return would not release Mustafa.\n\nMehmed died in Edirne on 25 June 1421. The viziers, fearing that Mustafa would make a move, kept the death secret for some time. Murad, who had ascended the throne in Bursa, refused to give his brothers up as hostages. Thereupon Byzantium released Mustafa, whom it had been holding on Lemnos, and landed him with ships off Gallipoli.\n\nThe truly surprising detail is hidden in the nickname. According to İnalcık, Murad's supporters spread the rumour that Mustafa had long been dead and that the man who had appeared was a fraud. The name 'Düzmece' (the Impostor), which passed into the history books, was thus born as a weapon in a succession struggle. The TDV article on Bayezid I the Thunderbolt, however, counts Mustafa among Bayezid's surviving sons.", "c0ce0617"], "kisa": ["To leave the throne safely to his son, he consented to give two little princes to Byzantium as hostages; the nickname 'Düzmece' (the Impostor) that stuck to his brother was born as propaganda.", "f35b875c"]},
"padisah2-mehmed3-kralice-orgu":{"baslik": ["Mehmed III: Queen Elizabeth's organ with a chiming clock and a dervish's prophecy", "2b7dfc39"], "metin": ["Foreign observers describe Mehmed III as short, stout, fair-skinned and black-bearded. He loved hunting, was skilled at making arrows, and never kept his sword and bow far from his side. Contemporary sources present him as naive, credulous, extremely devout and quickly saddened.\n\nThe most astonishing object in his palace was a gift. Queen Elizabeth I of England had sent a large organ with a chiming clock on it. The sultan had this organ set up in the palace and listened to it in admiration. TDV also writes that he tried to follow developments in Europe through reports and that he attached importance to relations with England and France.\n\nOne of the first things he did upon taking the throne is also noteworthy. He removed from the palace the dwarfs, mutes and other entertainers who had filled the palace of his father Murad III and meddled in state affairs. He wrote poetry under the pen name 'Adli'.\n\nThe last year of his reign was dark. He had his eldest son Mahmud killed on suspicion that he coveted the throne. Then bad news such as the fall of Tabriz arrived from the east. According to TDV, on 27 October 1603, as he was going to the palace, a dervish approached him. He said that in fifty-six days a great event would occur and that the sultan should not be caught unawares. The sultan's illness grew worse in December, and he died about two months later. One source attributes his death to a stomach ailment linked to obesity, others to a heart attack. His tomb is in the garden of Hagia Sophia.", "b29409b6"]},
"padisah2-mehmed3-vaiz-gozyasi-sefer":{"baslik": ["Mehmed III: The sultan who wept at a preacher's words and set out on campaign", "8684ec17"], "metin": ["In the summer of 1595, bad news kept arriving one piece after another from the Hungarian front. On 2 September 1595 the greatly prized fortress of Esztergom fell. According to the contemporary historian Selaniki, there was heavy pressure on the sultan in Istanbul to go on campaign in person. One Friday, while the sermon was being delivered at the Süleymaniye Mosque, many of the ulema rose to their feet. They asked why the sultan and the great officers of state were not going to holy war and demanded general mobilisation. The Janissaries too were now saying that they would go on campaign only with the sultan.\n\nThe real scene took place in the Old Palace. Muhyiddin Efendi, the preacher of Hagia Sophia, who had been summoned to deliver a sermon, said that with the fortresses falling one after another into enemy hands, the land of Islam was being trampled underfoot. At this Mehmed III shed tears and announced that he would go on campaign in person. He then made his most trusted lala (tutor), Mehmed Pasha, grand vizier. But the pasha, who was ill, died nine days later. The sultan was deeply shaken by this death and mourned for days.\n\nStill, the following year he set out at the head of the army. In the autumn of 1596 the fortress of Eger was taken and victory was won at Mezőkeresztes (Haçova). He thus became a sultan who went on campaign at the head of the army after a long interval.\n\nSo was this campaign the result of pressure? Feridun Emecen's article in TDV describes the pressure in detail. But the conclusion it reaches is different: the sultan's going to holy war was the result 'more of his own wish than of being forced'. Moreover, it appears that he hoped, through the success he would win by going on campaign in person, to take back power from the circle of his mother Safiye Sultan.", "1c6a64c3"], "kisa": ["In the mosque the ulema rose and asked 'why is the sultan not going to holy war'; Mehmed III, moved to tears by a single sentence from a preacher, took the head of the army the following year.", "a8301314"]},
"padisah2-mehmed6-malaya-son-gece":{"baslik": ["Mehmed VI (Vahideddin): The last night before boarding the Malaya", "b29ae411"], "metin": ["The morning of Friday 17 November 1922 was fixed for the last Ottoman sultan's departure from Istanbul. Vahideddin spent that last night in the Cihannüma Pavilion at Yıldız.\n\nThe details that TDV relates resemble a settling of accounts more than a farewell scene. He took care not to take any valuables or jewels apart from his personal belongings. He had his men burn bundles upon bundles of papers in the fireplace. He took with him the receipt for a very valuable jewel casket that he had returned to the palace treasury the day before. His aide-de-camp Zeki Bey suggested that they also take the sacred relics belonging to the caliphate. The sultan rejected this suggestion, because these were his ancestors' gift to the Turkish nation.\n\nEarly in the morning General Harington, commander of the British occupation forces, came in person to collect him from Yıldız Palace. The sultan and the ten people of his suite were seen off with the salute of a British battalion. When he boarded the British ship named Malaya, the commander of Britain's Mediterranean Fleet welcomed him in the name of the king and said that he was now on British soil and safe. He asked whether there was any place he wished to go. Vahideddin had no preference at all. Malta was suggested, and he accepted. His only request was that Malta's name should not be mentioned in the announcement to be made and that the statement should be published in the afternoon.\n\nHe had 20,000 pounds sterling with him. This money would not be enough even for the first few years of exile.", "d7f4a03b"]},
"padisah2-mehmed6-san-remo-haciz":{"baslik": ["Mehmed VI (Vahideddin): The last sultan, whose funeral was placed under distraint", "5dde74cc"], "metin": ["Vahideddin spent the first months of exile in Malta, the Hejaz and Egypt. Wherever he went, he was soon obliged to leave. In May 1923 he came to Italy and settled in the Villa Nobel in San Remo. His applications to settle in a Muslim country came to nothing. He lived alone for sixteen months and was reunited with his family only in March 1924, when the dynasty was exiled. With his now crowded household he moved to the Villa Mamolya (Villa Magnolia). When those who had fled also gathered in San Remo, a little Istanbul came into being there.\n\nThe 20,000 pounds sterling he had brought from Istanbul melted away quickly. According to TDV, he lost part of it to swindlers. Another part was spent in gambling houses by Zeki Bey, his former brother-in-law and aide-de-camp. He sold everything that would fetch money. Finally he also put up for sale the insignia of his sultanate. When he learned that the medal was a fake, he was saddened as he remembered those who had played this trick on him in the very first days of his reign. During the months he spent in Taif he composed songs expressing longing for his homeland. The scores of forty-one of his sixty-three works have survived to the present day.\n\nWhen he died on 16 May 1926, the creditors came to the door. He owed the tradesmen of San Remo 60,000 lire. The bailiffs locked and sealed, along with all the furnishings in the villa, even the room in which the body lay. The Italians did not permit burial until the debt had been paid in full. It took a month to find the money. The body was taken by train to Trieste and from there by ship to Beirut. On 3 July 1926 he was buried beside the Süleymaniye Mosque in Damascus.", "24774767"]},
"padisah2-murad3-ruya-mektuplari":{"baslik": ["Murad III: The sultan who had his dreams interpreted by letter by his sheikh", "372b129d"], "metin": ["One of the most influential people in the palace of Murad III was neither a vizier nor a pasha. This person was an interpreter of dreams. According to the TDV article on Murad III, the Halveti sheikh Şüca won Murad's confidence by interpreting one of his dreams while Murad was still a prince. When Murad came to the throne, he became the 'sultan's sheikh'. The sultan regularly sent him his dreams to have them interpreted. Rumours spread that the sheikh exploited this closeness to take bribes in return for pushing people's affairs through, and that he amassed a great fortune.\n\nThere was also a fear that fed Murad's inner world: the Hijri year 1000 (1591-1592). The expectation that great disorders would break out that year appears to have affected the sultan's melancholic disposition. The sources criticise him for shutting himself up more and more in the palace and for neglecting even the Friday processions for long periods.\n\nYet the same sultan was an inquisitive reader. The German traveller Schweigger writes that he read history books in particular with curiosity. He also adds that the sultan inspected the streets in disguise. Tarih-i Hind-i Garbi, which describes the Spanish discovery of America, and a history of France are among the works prepared in response to his curiosity about world history. It was also he who permitted the construction of the observatory.\n\nHe wrote poetry under the pen name 'Muradi'. In one of his ghazals recorded by Selaniki, he laments that he cannot find a statesman such as he would wish. The ghazal opens with the line 'If only there were a man for me such as my heart desires'. It is also recorded that he was a fine calligrapher in the thuluth, naskh and ta'liq scripts.", "05b0eaf7"]},
"padisah2-osman2-hur-esler-seken-kursun":{"baslik": ["Osman II (Young Osman): A ricocheting bullet and a marriage, against custom, to the daughter of the Shaykh al-Islam", "37da2205"], "metin": ["While returning from the Khotyn campaign in 1621, Young Osman received news in Edirne that he had a son, and he was overjoyed. Venetian sources refer to the child's mother, Ayşe Sultan, as being of Russian or Ukrainian origin. They also write that this woman, whom the Chief Black Eunuch Mustafa Agha had manumitted and raised like his own daughter, had great influence over the sultan. Osman had his consort and the prince, who was named Ömer, brought to him.\n\nIn the first days after the return to Istanbul, at his consort's request, a show re-enacting the battle of Khotyn was staged. During the performance a bullet that ricocheted from a musket struck the little prince. This is how Osman II lost his son in February 1622.\n\nThe marriages he made a short time later shook the palace and the ulema. First he married a girl from the family of Pertev Pasha, and then Akıle, the daughter of the Shaykh al-Islam Esad Efendi. According to Venetian reports, the sultan wanted to marry four women of free birth in keeping with Islamic conditions and to change the harem system radically. Esad Efendi, reminding him that previous sultans had always married slave women, strongly opposed this marriage. In the end he was obliged to give his consent. TDV thinks this marriage may have been a calculated move to win the offended ulema over to his side.\n\nThe calculation did not succeed. The ulema, whose arpalık revenues (office-holders' stipend lands) had been cut off, and the Janissaries, who feared for the future of their corps, joined forces. Three months later, in May 1622, Young Osman lost his throne and his life. What remained of him were the poems he wrote under the pen name 'Farisi', which TDV says carry great literary power.", "1330d38e"]},
"padisah2-osman3-gumus-civili-ayakkabi":{"baslik": ["Osman III: Are the silver-nailed shoes true? The strange habits of a sultan who came out of the kafes", "e22ec49c"], "metin": ["The most often told story about Osman III is this: the sultan, who did not like women, supposedly wore shoes with silver-nailed heels so that concubines in the harem would not cross his path, and whoever heard his footsteps would get out of his way. The story is widespread, but the TDV article on Osman III makes a definite remark: no source has been found for this repeated tale.\n\nThe sultan whom the sources actually describe is even more interesting. When his father Mustafa II was deposed in 1703, Osman III was shut up in the closed princes' apartments. He was not able to ascend the throne until 1754 and was the prince who stayed longest in those apartments in Ottoman history. The only thing known to have remained from that half century is that he made small portable writing desks and drawers. When he became sultan, he had the walls of the apartments where he had spent his years lowered and the windows opened, and had a garden, a pool and a fountain made inside.\n\nAs for the harem: the sources mention four consorts and four ikbals (favourites) belonging to him. He had no children. The Osman III of the sources was not a man who fled from the harem but a sultan who often mingled with the city in disguise. He usually wore the dress of the ulema and introduced himself as 'Osman Agha of Edirne'. He watched ambassadorial processions among the people. He ate on the road the fruit he had bought. He was fond of early-season fruit and of coffee.\n\nContemporary historians agree that he was hasty. He would decide suddenly and afterwards regret it. He also hated bribery and lying. In his three-year reign he changed grand viziers six times.", "4b5793e9"], "kisa": ["It is told that he went about the harem in silver-nailed shoes so as not to encounter concubines. TDV, however, writes that this story is not found in any source.", "298c24f5"]},
"padisah2-suleyman2-tahtirevan-son-yolculuk":{"baslik": ["Suleiman II: An accession bonus paid with palace plates and a sick sultan carried to the army in a litter", "85b49ec6"], "metin": ["When Suleiman II was taken out of the kafes (the princes' confinement quarters) and placed on the throne in 1687, the state treasury was empty. Not enough cash could be found for the accession bonus that had to be distributed to the troops. Thereupon the gold and silver vessels, swords and other objects in the treasury of the Enderun (inner palace service) and in the palace stables were sent to the mint and turned into money. Even this was not enough, and an additional tax was collected from the wealthy. Until the payments were completed, ruffians plundered the streets of Istanbul.\n\nThe end of his reign is no less striking than its beginning. In the spring of 1691 the sultan was badly swollen from the dropsy (istiska, an accumulation of water in the body) from which he had long suffered, and hope for his cure had been given up. At this time some of the ulema and leading men of the military corps were planning to bring Mehmed IV or one of his sons to the throne if the sultan died. When Grand Vizier Fazıl Mustafa Pasha heard of this, he decided to take the sultan to Edirne. The gravely ill Suleiman II was persuaded by being told that it would raise the troops' morale. He was carried in a litter to the army camp at Davudpaşa. Those who had started the rumours were exiled to Cyprus.\n\nFor their last meeting the grand vizier came to the Yonca Fountain, an hour from Edirne. According to the sources, the sultan received him alone and, weeping, prayed for the army's victory. A week later, on Friday 22 June 1691, he died. He had six consorts but no surviving children. In his youth he had been an accomplished calligrapher in thuluth and naskh. His body was brought to Istanbul and buried in the tomb of Suleiman the Lawgiver, on his right side.", "e46c17fe"]},
"patrona-halil-derdi-neydi":{"baslik": ["What was Patrona Halil's grievance: the rebels' demands and the calculations behind them", "86e739d9"], "metin": ["The declared cause. On 25 September 1730 the rebels rose up saying 'Our case is one under the sharia'. A few days before the revolt, in notes they sent to the Shaykh al-Islam and the preacher of Hagia Sophia, and in a paper they left at the Orta Mosque, they had hinted at their intention by writing 'We want a sultan who is Mahmûdü'l-hısâl (of praiseworthy character)'.\n\nTHE DEMANDS, in order.\n① The surrender of thirty-seven persons, headed by the grand vizier, the Shaykh al-Islam and the steward (kethüda) of the grand vizierate.\n② After the executions, the deposition of Ahmed III for the sake of their own safety.\n③ From the new sultan, the abolition of the excessive taxes laid on the people and of the malikâne (life-term tax farm) system.\n④ Then offices: those of kul kethüdası (steward of the Janissary corps), grand vizier, deputy grand vizier (kaymakam) and Kapudan Pasha (grand admiral).\n\nTHE SOURCE of the anger. According to TDV, the revolt was a reaction to a series of developments reaching back to Passarowitz: the loss of Belgrade and Temeşvar, the grand vizier's falling out with the war party because of his peaceable policy, the failure in the Persian war that had been opened reluctantly, the imdadiyye (aid) taxes that became permanent, the tax burden on the artisan guilds, the unemployment created by migration from village to city and the extravagant spending of statesmen. Details: lale-devri-tepkiler.\n\nTHE CALCULATION behind it. TDV attributes the preparation of the revolt to Kaymakam (Kapudan Pasha) Mustafa Pasha, who coveted the grand vizierate: he gathered supporters through gifts and entrusted the job to Patrona Halil, whom he had earlier saved from execution. İbrahim Pasha, for his part, pointed to this son-in-law as responsible for the events and asked for his execution. The absence of Mustafa Pasha's name from the rebels' first list increased suspicions; in the end he too was handed over together with the other two viziers.\n\nWasn't it a PERSONAL cause? Halil's refusing the 100,000 gold coins offered to him by saying 'All the wealth of Istanbul is already mine' looks like a stance of principle; but TDV also writes that within a few weeks he acquired a fortune of 3000 purses and had appointments given to his supporters.\n\nTWO readings. TDV's article on the Tulip Era stresses that part of the ulema stoked the discontent but that the uprising broke out for political reasons; the article on the Patrona Revolt brings forward the economic and social accumulation. The two do not exclude each other.", "a9ec4d22"], "kisa": ["They came out saying 'Our case is one under the sharia' and asked first for the heads of 37 people, then for 'a sultan of fine character'; but according to TDV the strings of the revolt lay in one vizier's ambition for the grand vizierate.", "a190dd46"], "bag": ["Significance: the demands were proclaimed in the language of the 'sharia', but the concrete requests concerned taxes, persons and offices — showing that the revolt carried a political-economic rather than an ideological calculation.", "066cb274"]},
"patrona-isyancilarin-yaptiklari":{"baslik": ["The rebels' Istanbul: looting, dragged corpses, demolished kiosks (October–November 1730)", "9f774219"]},
"rusiran-hazar-seferi-1722":{"baslik": ["Peter I's Caspian Campaign: How the Russians Came to Derbent (1722-1735)", "c7aa3495"], "metin": ["Cause. In 1722 the Safavid State was on the point of collapse because of the Afghan invasion. Peter I, who had just ended the Great Northern War, wished to seize the western and southern shores of the Caspian — Shirvan and Gilan, through which the silk trade passed — before the Ottomans.\n\nROUTE: Sea + land. Peter reached Astrakhan in July 1722; the fleet put to sea on 18 July (by the Russian calendar; 29 July by today's calendar). The army was landed on 8 August (28 July by the Russian calendar) at Agrakhan Bay, south of the Terek; the cavalry came overland from Tsaritsyn. From here the march continued by land, along the coast: the advance guard attacked the Kumyk beys at Endirey and made them swear an oath; the army crossed the Sulak river; Adil-Girey, the Shamkhal of Tarki, received Peter in his palace and became a Russian subject; when the Utemish sultanate on the route resisted, the village was burned. Derbent surrendered without a fight on 3 September 1722 (23 August by the Russian calendar). The campaign did not continue as far as Shamakhi, its intended goal: in September Peter turned back from Derbent, and in October he was in Astrakhan. The following year General Matyushkin came by sea and took Baku (6 August 1723; 26 July by the Russian calendar); Russian units were also landed in Gilan.\n\nWHOSE were the lands in between. The area between Derbent and the Terek was in three parts. (1) The Shamkhalate of Tarki was a Russian subject; when the shamkhal rebelled in 1725, Tarki was burned, in 1726 the shamkhal was arrested and exiled and the shamkhalate was abolished — the coast remained under direct Russian military administration until 1735. (2) The Holy Cross fortress, founded on the Sulak in 1722, and the Agrakhan fortification were Russian garrisons. (3) The usmiat of Kaitag between Tarki and Derbent, however, was autonomous and its loyalty wavered: it submitted in 1722, and in 1725 it incited the shamkhal to revolt. So there was an overland link to Derbent, but this link ran through vassal and semi-independent principalities; part of the gap that makes Derbent appear on the map as a detached piece is real (Kaitag), and part is not (the Tarki–Sulak belt).\n\nLEGAL basis and outcome. By the Treaty of St Petersburg of 23 September 1723, the envoy of Shah Tahmasp ceded Derbent, Baku, Gilan, Mazandaran and Astarabad to Russia; but Tahmasp did not ratify the treaty. In 1724 at Istanbul the Ottomans and Russia divided northern Iran between themselves (see `antlasma4-iran-mukasemenamesi-1724`). This occupation, in which more than thirty thousand soldiers died of disease, proved costly: by the Treaty of Rasht in 1732 Russia returned Gilan, Mazandaran and Astarabad, and by the Treaty of Ganja of 21 March 1735 (10 March by the Russian calendar) it also returned Baku and Derbent to Nader's Iran. The Russian forces withdrew northward; the Holy Cross fortress was demolished, and the Kizlyar fortress on the Terek was established in 1735 as the new frontier post.", "6bf02b40"]},
"rusiran-iliskiler-genel":{"baslik": ["Russian–Iranian Relations: From Trade to Spheres of Influence (1556-1921)", "4e6b6841"], "metin": ["The age of trade (16th-17th CENTURIES). Moscow's capture of Kazan in 1552 and of Astrakhan in 1556 made the Volga a Russian river from end to end and turned the Caspian into a sea of commerce between the two states: Iranian silk went to Russia and Europe by way of Astrakhan. The relationship was largely one of embassies and trade. The events that disturbed it came not from the states but from the margins: in 1668-69 Stenka Razin's Cossacks plundered Iran's Caspian coasts.\n\nTHE FIRST expansion (1722-1735). During the Safavid collapse Peter I took Derbent, Baku and Gilan; Russia was able to hold these lands for thirteen years and returned them in 1732-1735 (see `rusiran-hazar-seferi-1722`).\n\nTHE LOSS of the CAUCASUS (1783-1828). Russian interest returned at the end of the eighteenth century by way of Georgia: by the Treaty of Georgievsk of 1783, Kartli-Kakheti came under Russian protection. Zubov's campaign of 1796 was cut short by the death of Catherine II, and Paul I recalled the army. At the beginning of the nineteenth century two wars ended Iran's presence in the Caucasus:\n· 1804-1813: Russia took Ganja in January 1804, and the war began before Yerevan in June 1804; in 1806 Baku, Quba and Derbent fell. The Treaty of Gulistan of 24 October 1813 (12 October by the Russian calendar) left to Russia the Caucasian khanates it had taken in the war; the treaty was ratified in Tbilisi in September 1814.\n· 1826-1828: The war, which began with the jihad fatwa of the Iranian ulema in June 1826, ended with the Treaty of Turkmenchay of 22 February 1828 (10 February by the Russian calendar). Yerevan and Nakhchivan passed to Russia, and the frontier between the two states settled on a line close to its present position. A year later, on 11 February 1829, the Russian envoy and poet Alexander Griboyedov was killed in Tehran in a popular uprising.\n\nCENTRAL Asia and the 'GREAT game' (1837-1881). Having lost the Caucasus, Iran turned east, to Herat; Russia supported this move as a card to play against Britain, which was defending India. The siege of Herat of 1837-1838 was lifted in September 1838 after the British occupied Kharg Island in the Persian Gulf in June 1838. Russia's capture of the Turkmen fortress of Geok Tepe in 1881, and its drawing of a frontier with Iran along the Atrek in the same year, also fixed Iran's northeastern frontier in its present position.\n\nSPHERES of influence (1907-1921). The Anglo-Russian Convention of 31 August 1907 divided Iran into a northern (Russian) and a southern (British) sphere of influence. The Persian Cossack Brigade, commanded by Russian officers, shelled the Majlis building on 23 June 1908; in December 1911, as a result of Russian pressure, the Majlis was dissolved and the American financial adviser Morgan Shuster was dismissed. This phase of the relationship closed only after the Russian Revolution: on 26 February 1921 the Soviet-Iranian Treaty was signed in Moscow.\n\nTHE PATTERN. From Iran's point of view, Russia was a different kind of neighbour from the Ottomans: border disputes with the Ottomans moved back and forth for centuries within the same mountain belt; Russia, by contrast, pushed the frontier permanently southward with each war, and the land lost could never be recovered. The restoration of 1735 is the only exception to this pattern.", "810fdd93"]},
"rusiran-iran-paylasimi-1724-neden":{"baslik": ["Why the Ottomans and Russia Divided Iran (Partition Treaty of Istanbul, 1724)", "784e5e3e"], "metin": ["Vacuum. In 1722 the Afghan Mahmud Hotaki routed the Safavid army at Gulnabad and in October took Isfahan; Shah Sultan Husayn lost his throne; his son Tahmasp II maintained his claim to be shah but could not rule most of the country. A vast country stretching from the Caucasus to the Persian Gulf was suddenly left without a master, and two neighbours turned to this vacuum at the same time.\n\nWhy RUSSIA entered. According to Encyclopaedia Iranica, Peter I's real fear was that the Ottomans would take a weak Iran and appear on Russia's southeastern frontier. This was accompanied by an economic aim: the silk of Gilan and the resources of the Caspian coastal provinces; in 1717 Peter's envoy Volynsky had obtained from Iran the right of free silk trade for Russian merchants. A pretext was also at hand: in a raid by some Caucasian tribes on Shirvan, Russian merchants had been killed and their goods plundered. Peter took Derbent in 1722; the treaty he signed with Tahmasp's envoy in St Petersburg in 1723 ceded Derbent, Baku, Gilan, Mazandaran and Astarabad to Russia in return for military aid.\n\nWhy the OTTOMANS entered. For the Ottomans the collapse in Iran was both a threat and an opportunity: no order remained on the eastern frontier, and Russia too was moving down from the Caspian coast into the Caucasus. Russia's agreement with Tahmasp caused Istanbul further anxiety. The Ottoman army took Tbilisi in the spring of 1723; at the end of 1723 and the beginning of 1724 it entered Eastern Georgia and Eastern Armenia and began to threaten Russia with war.\n\nWHY partition rather than war. Neither side could afford a new war: Russia's garrisons on the Caspian coast were suffering heavy losses from disease, and the revenue expected from Gilan silk was not coming in; the Ottomans, for their part, did not want to open a Russian front in the north before they had even consolidated their newly taken lands. Through the mediation of the Marquis de Bonnac, the French ambassador in Istanbul, a treaty was signed in Istanbul on 24 June 1724 between the Russian envoy Neplyuyev and Grand Vizier Nevşehirli Damat İbrahim Pasha (see `antlasma4-iran-mukasemenamesi-1724`).\n\nPROVISIONS. The treaty consisted of a preamble, six articles and a conclusion. The Ottomans received the lands of Eastern Georgia and Eastern Armenia in their hands, and the khanates of Tabriz, Qazvin and Shamakhi; into Shamakhi, however, they were not to send troops or build fortresses unless there was a rebellion. Russia kept the cities and provinces it had acquired by the Treaty of St Petersburg. The rest of Iran was left to Tahmasp II, and the two empires would recognise him as legitimate shah on condition that he accepted these territorial losses.\n\nOUTCOME: A partition on paper. In 1724-1725 the Ottoman army largely filled out its share by taking Hamadan, Yerevan, Tabriz and Ganja; Russia, however, sent no troops at all to Mazandaran and Astarabad, and in Gilan too could not get beyond Lahijan. What undid the partition was Iran's recovery: with the rise of Nader, the Ottomans gave back what they had taken by the Ahmed Pasha treaty of 1732 and the Treaty of Istanbul of 1736, and Russia by the Treaties of Rasht (1732) and Ganja (1735). Within twelve years both empires had withdrawn from the territory they had divided in 1724.\n\nDEBATE. The partition treaty is an early example of two neighbours dividing the territory of a third state without asking that state; in this respect it recalls the Partitions of Poland half a century later. The difference is this: Poland could never get back on its feet, whereas Iran did so within ten years and the partition lapsed of its own accord.", "31967466"], "kisa": ["Two empires entered the collapsing Safavid country from two separate ends; when they came face to face, they chose to divide the map rather than fight each other.", "5bea1168"]},
"rusiran-nadir-ruslari-cikardi":{"baslik": ["How Iran Drove the Russians Out: A Recovery Without War (1729-1735)", "c1bf1e8d"], "metin": ["The only clash. Throughout the Russian occupation, the only noteworthy result Iran won against the Russians by arms came in 1724: the officers of a Russian battalion that had established itself at the mouth of the Kura were killed on 11 September 1724 (31 August by the Russian calendar) at a banquet given by the naib of Salyan, and Tahmasp's troops entered Salyan. The Russians returned to the area a few years later. The really wearing enemy was not war but the climate: in Gilan, in the first three months of 1732 alone, more than a thousand soldiers died of disease; according to Iranica, the number of those who died during the occupation approached one hundred thousand, and the revenue expected from Gilan silk never came.\n\nTHE RISE of NADER. Tahmasp Qoli Khan (Nader), who in 1729 defeated the Afghans and recovered Isfahan, became within a few years the real master of Iran. In August 1732 he deposed Tahmasp II and became regent in the name of the infant Abbas III. Russia now faced not a disjointed country but a commander capable of defeating Ottoman armies.\n\nFIRST step: Rasht (1732). To win Iran's friendship against the Turks, Russia signed the Treaty of Rasht at the beginning of 1732: it gave up its rights over Mazandaran and Astarabad and agreed to evacuate Gilan and Talysh. General Levashov's corps crossed the Kura on 2 September 1732 (22 August by the Russian calendar) and withdrew northward. Derbent and Baku remained in Russian hands for the time being. That same winter the Ottomans too were handing back most of western Iran by the Ahmed Pasha treaty.\n\nSECOND step: the threat (1734). Nader, who had defeated the Ottomans before Yerevan in May 1734, rejected the offer of help made by the Russian envoy Golitsyn and declared that unless the Caspian provinces and Derbent were handed over at once, he would make a separate peace with the Ottomans. On the threshold of a new Ottoman war, St Petersburg did not want to push Iran into the opposing camp: Empress Anna's order of 9 November 1734 (29 October by the Russian calendar) commanded the evacuation of all positions, beginning with Baku and Nizovaya.\n\nTHIRD step: Ganja (1735). The treaty signed on 21 March 1735 (10 March by the Russian calendar), during Nader's siege of Ganja, provided for the return of Baku and Derbent within two months. Baku was handed over on 1 May and Derbent on 8 May 1735. Russia's new frontier was the Terek river: the Holy Cross fortress on the Sulak was demolished in August 1735, and Levashov chose the site of the Kizlyar fortress on the Terek.\n\nWHY without war. The reason given by the Russian sources is clear: Russia was on the eve of the war with the Ottomans that would begin in 1736 and wanted Nader not as an enemy but as an ally; a coastal strip wasted by disease and yielding no revenue was worth less than this alliance. Iran's 'victory' was therefore won not on a battlefield but by carrying to the negotiating table the victories Nader had gained over the Ottomans.", "10d42799"]},
"rusiran-takvim-farki":{"baslik": ["Why Russian Dates Are Written in Two Ways: The Julian and Gregorian Calendars", "ba5f2aee"], "metin": ["Two calendars. In 1582 Pope Gregory XIII corrected the Julian calendar, which had drifted away from the solar year, by moving it forward ten days. Catholic countries adopted the new (Gregorian) calendar at once; Orthodox Russia, however, used the old calendar until the beginning of 1918. The difference between them grew over the centuries: 11 days in the eighteenth century, 12 days in the nineteenth and 13 days in the twentieth.\n\nHow it appears in the ATLAS. Sources based on Russian archives give the Julian day; European and Iranian sources (as the equivalent of the Hijri date) give the Gregorian. Russian encyclopaedias often write both together: the surrender of Derbent appears as '23 August (3 September) 1722'. Taking a single figure and mixing it up with the other calendar makes the same event look like two different days. Exactly this happened in this atlas: the Treaty of Turkmenchay stands twice, in two separate chronology entries, as 10 February and 22 February 1828; the capture of Derbent and the Treaty of Ganja have also been written according to the Russian calendar.\n\nHOW to tell them apart. (1) If the source is in Russian or based on Russian archives and predates the October Revolution, the day is most probably Julian. (2) If a Hijri date is given, its Common Era equivalent is Gregorian: for Gulistan, Iranica writes '29 Shawwal 1228 = 24 October 1813'; by the Russian calendar the same treaty is dated 12 October. (3) If the difference between two days is exactly 11 or 12 days, this is not a contradiction but a difference of calendar. The same yardstick also explains why the Russian 'October Revolution' falls in November (7 November 1917) by the Western calendar.", "f076c8a6"], "kisa": ["Russia used the old Julian calendar until 1918; that is why the same treaty appears in one book as 10 February and in another as 22 February 1828.", "b236cef9"]},
"ruslara-niye-direnilemedi-1806-1812":{"baslik": ["Why could the Ottomans not hold out against the Russians in 1806-1812 — was it the army, technology, logistics or internal strife?", "25c658f9"], "ozet": ["In the summer of 1810 Sohum was lost, and in the autumn Rusçuk (Ruse). There is no one-sentence answer to the question \"Was the Russian army larger, or technically more advanced?\": the sources consulted point to at least five separate causes, and these feed one another. The card does not declare any of them to be the sole cause.", "3f5aaac8"], "metin": ["Cause ① — the throne changed hands twice while the war was going on. The war began in November 1806; in May 1807 the Kabakçı Revolt overthrew the sultan, in July 1808 a counter-coup overthrew the second sultan and brought in a third, and in November 1808 the grand vizier was killed. The TDV article `mustafa-iv` records how this was reflected at the front: \"The events in Istanbul spread here too and caused an upheaval; the Janissaries rebelled, the grand vizier was chased away, the army's senior officials fled, the agha of the Janissaries, Pehlivan Agha, who was handling these affairs, was killed by his own comrades, and Çelebi Mustafa Pasha was appointed grand vizier, but order could not be restored. The turmoil within the army led to a great weakness in the face of the Russians.\" Moreover, the time gained was spent not on the enemy but on internal score-settling: the one-year armistice made at Slobozia on 24 August 1807 was \"used not for taking the measures necessary to bring the war to a successful conclusion, but for the internal struggle caused by the change of sultan.\"\n\nCAUSE ② — the only modern army available was not committed to the war, and was then destroyed. Selim III had his New Order (Nizâm-ı Cedîd) army; although there were more than 10,000 trained soldiers in Istanbul in 1807, the sultan did not use them even against the revolt and abolished the corps with his own hand (TDV `kabakci-isyani`). A study examining the organisation of the campaign through the sharia court registers identifies the same gap: \"Considering that this war came on the eve of the New Order system introduced by Selim III, one would expect New Order soldiers to have taken part in it. However, Selim III did not use these soldiers in this war\" (Karakaya 2020, p. 116). As for its successor, the new Sekban corps (Sekbân-ı Cedîd) founded in 1808, it lived for three months: on 16 November its barracks were burned, its aghas were killed and the survivors scattered (TDV `sekban-i-cedid`). The TDV article `yeniceri` states the real structural deadlock in a single sentence: between the new and old armies a \"relentless enmity\" had developed, \"so much so that even in times of need it was never possible to use these two forces together.\"\n\nCAUSE ③ — who raised the soldiers: not the centre but the provinces. The manpower as measured by the same study: \"In the research carried out on the 1806-1812 Ottoman-Russian war, it is seen that the average number of soldiers remained around 60,000\", and at the front \"alongside the soldiers of the standing army (kapıkulu), it is seen that the troops of the ayan in particular were used in the war\" (Karakaya 2020, p. 116). The contribution of one Anatolian district (Safranbolu) is entered in the court registers in the form of demands for soldiers, avârız and imdâd-ı seferiyye taxes (extraordinary and campaign-aid levies), mules and sheep — that is, the army was assembled not through a central system of mobilisation but from soldiers and provisions gathered district by district. This is also the reason for the political weight of the ayan: the state depended on them for taxes, public order and soldiers (TDV `ayan`).\n\nCAUSE ④ — money. The revenue of the New Revenue Treasury (Îrâd-ı Cedîd Hazinesi), set up to finance the reform, had reached 64,500 purses in 1797-98; but because the growth of the new army \"increased the burden on the population of Anatolia and Rumelia, and the people were crushed under heavy taxes\", it fed the opposition, and when the New Order was abolished, \"one of the first things done was the plundering of the revenues transferred to the New Revenue Treasury\" (TDV `nizam-i-cedid`). In other words, in the middle of the war the army's source of finance was broken up.\n\nCAUSE ⑤ — several fronts and diplomacy at the same time. The war was not on a single front: the pilgrimage routes had been closed by the Wahhabi revolt, the British fleet had appeared before Istanbul in February 1807 (TDV `kabakci-isyani`), and in the Caucasus local powers had changed sides — in Abkhazia, Sefer Bey, the son of Keleş Bey and a Russian subject, wrote letters to the tsar in 1808 and 1809 stating that he wished to submit to him, and on 17 February 1810 the Russians proclaimed that Abkhazia had been joined to Russia (TDV `sohum`).\n\nAND the outcome can be read in two cities. Sohum Fortress \"fell into Russian hands on 11 July 1810 because sufficient military support could not be provided at that time\" (TDV `sohum`). Rusçuk, by contrast, \"withstood a long and bloody siege carried out by the Russian armies under the command of Count Kaminski; on 26 September 1810 it surrendered on condition that the garrison and the civilians could leave the city in safety\" (TDV `ruscuk`). Read together, the two do not yield the picture that \"Russian soldiers were better\": Rusçuk held out for months, and the following year the Russians were forced to withdraw \"after the heavy defeats and setbacks suffered in June 1811\". The issue, then, is not technical superiority in a single battle but the capacity to sustain a war: an uninterrupted supply of soldiers, money, command and political stability.\n\nWhat THIS card does not say. No quantified comparison of the Russian army with the Ottoman army of the period in terms of muskets, artillery, training and the education of officers was found in the sources consulted. The statement \"their weapons were better\" is too strong a claim to be written without being measured; the five causes above rest on measurable records, while the sixth (technical superiority) could not be measured.", "04d3cab8"], "bag": ["View: The cause was military — the old army was untrained and mutinous, and the new army was destroyed almost as soon as it was established. Counter-view: The cause was political — a state that went through two depositions, one killing of a ruler and an assassination of a grand vizier within six years could not possibly have maintained continuity at the front; the army was a result of this instability, not its cause. Third reading: both are secondary, and the real issue was financial and logistical capacity — soldiers were being gathered district by district, money was dissipated through the plundering of the reform treasury, and distant fortresses (Sohum) were falling because they could not get support. The sources present these three readings not as substitutes for one another but as intertwined.", "0b65dbbc"]},
"savas-93-harbi-1877-1878":{"baslik": ["The War of '93 — the Russo-Ottoman War of 1877-1878", "8a0cf459"], "tarih_metin": ["Declaration of war 24 April 1877 · Fall of Plevna 10 December 1877 · Treaty of San Stefano 3 March 1878", "8df6f08b"], "yer": ["Balkan (Danube) front — Plevna, Shipka Pass; Caucasus front — Kars, Erzurum", "59d469ce"], "oncesi": ["After the Treaty of Paris (1856), Russia, pursuing a Pan-Slavist foreign policy, encouraged the Balkan peoples to revolt against the Ottomans; the allegation that tens of thousands of Bulgarians had been killed in the suppression of the Bulgarian Uprising that broke out in May 1876 gave the war a religious/humanitarian legitimacy in the eyes of European public opinion. In addition, the growing burden of the Ottoman foreign debt had also turned European public opinion against the state. Russia declared war on 24 April 1877.", "bbea8a50"], "akis": ["On the Danube front, the Ottoman forces under the command of Gazi Osman Pasha established a fortified position at Plevna and resisted for months; in September 1877 the repeated joint assaults of the Russian and Romanian forces came to nothing. But at the end of the long siege provisions and ammunition ran out, and Osman Pasha was forced to surrender on 10 December 1877. This resistance delayed the course of the war but could not change its outcome; the Russian army crossed the Balkans and advanced as far as Edirne, and on the Caucasus front as far as Erzurum.", "7c436b2a"], "sonuc": ["The guns fell silent with the Armistice of Edirne of 31 January 1878, and on 3 March 1878 the Treaty of San Stefano (Ayastefanos) was signed: a \"Principality of Greater Bulgaria\" with wide borders was established, the independence of Serbia, Montenegro and Romania was officially recognised for the first time, a large territory in eastern Anatolia including Erzurum was ceded to Russia, and the Ottomans agreed to pay Russia a war indemnity of 802,500,000 francs. The human cost of the war was heavy: hundreds of thousands of Muslim refugees (muhacir) fleeing from Bulgaria and the Caucasus sought shelter in Istanbul and Anatolia. Britain, disturbed by such a great Russian expansion, exerted diplomatic pressure to soften the harsh provisions — in return it took over the administration of Cyprus; some of the harsh provisions were revised a few months later at the Congress of Berlin (1878).", "742b09c1"], "tartisma": ["Only the original provisions of San Stefano have been described in this card; the extent to which the Treaty of Berlin changed San Stefano (such as the reduction of Greater Bulgaria) has not been separately verified, as the body of the TDV text that was read does not give this detail.", "56bc0f69"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman State", "86202f73"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["At Plevna, Gazi Osman Pasha", "a89259b4"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Russia, Romania, Serbia, Montenegro", "b57f43c4"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"]},
"savas-aden-1516":{"baslik": ["The siege of Aden (2-10 August 1516)", "d56938a4"], "tarih_metin": ["3 Rajab 922 · 2 August 1516 (arrival at Aden) · 11 Rajab 922 · 10 August 1516 (withdrawal)", "9ba164a2"], "yer": ["Aden and the castle of Sira, on the south coast of Yemen", "107160fa"], "oncesi": ["Faced with the Portuguese threat, the sultan of Gujarat and the Yemeni Sultan Amir II asked the Egyptian Sultan Qansuh al-Ghawri for help. According to TDV, Selman Reis, who went to Egypt on behalf of the Ottoman state, became captain of the fleet of fifty galleys prepared at Suez in 1511; the commander-in-chief was Hüseyin Bey. In the summer of 1515 the fleet went to India and made an alliance with the sultan of Gujarat at Diu, but Goa, which the Portuguese had fortified, could not be taken. Returning to Yemen, the fleet built a fortress on the island of Kamaran. When Sultan Amir, who had summoned them, did not help them with provisions, they plundered the coasts; with the help of the Zaydis, Hüseyin Bey took Zabid on 20 June 1516. A month later the fleet put to sea to conquer Aden.", "81d396bc"], "akis": ["The fleet reached Aden on 2 August 1516. Selman Reis landed and pitched tents near the fortress; among the galleons leaving the harbour, he seized the goods of the one belonging to Sultan Amir and sent these ships to Gujarat with a letter announcing that he had conquered Yemen. Hüseyin Bey and Selman Reis took up positions below the castle of Sira, which belonged to Aden, and bombarded the fortress of Aden, but the fortress, which had a great many soldiers and much ammunition, did not surrender thanks to the vigorous defence of Murjan al-Amiri. In the first sortie from the fortress, Selman Reis was wounded in three places. When Amir's brother Abd al-Malik came to the rescue with an army from Taiz, the siege was lifted.", "0124520c"], "sonuc": ["The fleet left Aden on 10 August 1516 and returned to Jeddah by way of Zeila, Mocha and Kamaran. Hüseyin Bey was dismissed on the complaint of the Sharif of Mecca and, while on his way to Egypt, was killed by being thrown into the sea. Selman Reis, however, was summoned by Sultan Selim the Grim after the Ottoman conquest of Egypt, and in April 1517 he successfully defended Jeddah against a Portuguese fleet. Aden was to be taken only on 3 August 1538 by an Ottoman fleet of seventy-six ships under Hadım Süleyman Pasha.", "3ae2e8f7"], "tartisma": ["The TDV articles consulted do not give the losses in the siege. The status of the fleet: it had been prepared on the order of the Mamluk sultan; TDV states that Selman Reis went to Egypt on behalf of the Ottoman state.", "fd0e02cc"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Red Sea fleet in Mamluk service", "b0ddbd3e"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Selman Reis · Hüseyin Bey (Hüseyin al-Kurdi)", "fe90b5d1"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["twenty-two galleys and two barques", "ce7ebdd5"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Tahirid Sultanate of Yemen (fortress of Aden)", "aaaf6b4e"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["Murjan al-Amiri, governor of Aden · Abd al-Malik b. Abd al-Wahhab, who came to the rescue", "bbfa5c2f"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"]},
"savas-adranos-1323":{"baslik": ["The Adranos (Orhaneli) campaign (1323-1324)", "d7b7cdd9"], "tarih_metin": ["Campaign 1323 · according to the TDV article on Orhan, the castle was taken in 724 (1324)", "7cf823a5"], "yer": ["Castle of Adranos (Atranos) — present-day Orhaneli, in the province of Bursa", "606b0efb"], "oncesi": ["The tekfur (Byzantine lord) of Adranos had been a member of the alliance of tekfurs of the Bursa plain formed against Osman in 1303; after the defeat at Dimbos he had taken refuge in his castle. As Osman, because of his illness, increasingly left affairs to his son, he was preparing Orhan to lead the principality by sending him on campaigns alongside experienced commanders such as Akça Koca, Konuralp and Köse Mihal. Orhan, who had been at the head of the army since 1305, had cut off the aid going to İznik by taking the castles along the Sakarya.", "c195618e"], "akis": ["TDV writes that the last campaign of his father's time was against the castle of Adranos, and that Köse Mihal and Turgut Alp were again at Orhan's side. The course of the campaign and the details of the siege are not related in the TDV articles consulted.", "33ec0589"], "sonuc": ["The castle was taken in 724 (1324). Osman died in these same years: he is alive in the endowment deed of Asporça Hatun dated September 1323, whereas by March 1324 Orhan is at the head of the principality. TDV believes that the capture of Adranos must have been the source of the dispute with the principality of Karesi; a few years later the Byzantine emperor would make an agreement with the lord of Karesi. Bursa surrendered in 1326.", "97e58d23"], "tartisma": ["The TDV articles consulted refer to the campaign as Osman's last campaign, but do not give the day on which the castle surrendered; 1323 is the year of the campaign and 1324 the year the castle was taken.", "737cb2e5"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman principality", "d8cb2121"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Orhan Bey · with Köse Mihal and Turgut Alp at his side", "f18fdb3c"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Tekfurate of Adranos", "33905ae1"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["The tekfur of Adranos (his name is not given in the source)", "275a2ef4"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"]},
"savas-akka-1291":{"baslik": ["The conquest of Acre and the Crusader withdrawal from the Syrian coast (5 April – 28 May 1291)", "b580e90d"], "tarih_metin": ["6 March 1291 (the Mamluk army leaves Cairo) · 5 April (arrival before Acre) · 17 Jumada al-Awwal 690 · 18 May 1291 (the city is taken) · 28 May (the Templar building falls)", "fb19298c"], "yer": ["Acre, north of the Bay of Haifa — then Tyre, Sidon, Tartus, Beirut, Haifa and Jubayl", "925f71fd"], "oncesi": ["Acre had been in Crusader hands since 1104; Saladin took it in 1187, and the Third Crusade recovered it in 1191. The city was for a long time the centre of the Crusader Kingdom of Jerusalem and, especially after 1229, the headquarters of the Knights of Saint John. The Mamluk sultan Qalawun wanted to put an end to the Crusader presence in the region, which was cooperating with the Ilkhanids, but he fell ill and died while setting out on the campaign against Acre (November 1290). On his deathbed he had his son Khalil promise that he would complete the campaign. Khalil prepared the siege equipment and the army; he refused to receive the envoys who came from Acre, headed by Philippe Mainboeuf, and had them thrown into a dungeon.", "877cf840"], "akis": ["The Mamluk army left Cairo on 6 March 1291 and arrived before Acre on 5 April; the famous historian Abu al-Fida was among the troops of Hama. The Templar and Hospitaller knights gathered in the city, and King Henry of Cyprus sent forces with his brother Amaury. At the start of the siege shiploads of old people and children were carried off to safety in Cyprus. Henry arrived with forty ships on 4 May and assumed supreme command, but the force he brought was not enough to change the outcome. By the evening of 18 May the whole city except the building of the Templar order had passed into Muslim hands; on 28 May that too was taken.", "8008ed3b"], "sonuc": ["The sultan had the city demolished so that the Crusaders could not use it as a base for a new attack on Syria. The Mamluk forces then took Tyre, Sidon, Tartus, Beirut, Athlit, Haifa and Jubayl and cleared the Syrian coast of the Crusaders. According to the TDV article on the Mamluks, with the capture of Acre the Crusader presence in the region, which had lasted two hundred years, came to an end. The conquest had great repercussions in the Islamic and Christian worlds. Not until the eighteenth century would Acre again become the centre of the region.", "3635bfea"], "tartisma": ["The TDV articles consulted do not give the number of soldiers on either side; the stages of the assaults during the siege are not described.", "813a8e33"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Mamluk Sultanate", "1684f166"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Sultan al-Malik al-Ashraf Khalil b. Qalawun", "0641fad3"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source; TDV says 'superior in every respect'", "7ebc670c"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["The Crusaders in Acre: Templar and Hospitaller knights, Cypriot forces", "6273e65e"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["King Henry of Cyprus (supreme commander from 4 May)", "08f6b4a6"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["King Henry's fleet of forty ships; no figure given for the troops", "c8199861"]},
"savas-alman-seferi-guns-1532":{"baslik": ["The German Campaign and the siege of Güns (Kőszeg) (1532)", "12b5f344"], "tarih_metin": ["25 April 1532 (departure from Istanbul) · August 1532 (Güns) · September 1532 (turn towards Graz) · 22 November 1532 (celebrations of the return to Istanbul)", "10ea9d13"], "yer": ["Güns (Kőszeg), 60 miles from Vienna, western Hungary · then towards Graz (Styria)", "0506ef82"], "oncesi": ["The siege of Vienna in 1529 had been lifted after seventeen days. Ferdinand kept up his pressure on Buda and besieged the city in 1531; this made a new relief campaign necessary. This time Suleiman the Lawgiver named the Habsburg Emperor Charles V himself as his target; in the sources the campaign is referred to as being 'directed against the King of Spain'. The sultan left Istanbul on 25 April 1532; Ferdinand's envoys came to the army camp at Niš, and the French envoy Rincón on 5 July.", "dd48da40"], "akis": ["The Ottoman army entered Hungary and advanced through Ferdinand's lands, but no trace of the imperial army was seen. The sultan besieged Güns, 60 miles from Vienna, and waited for Charles V to come against him. The very large imperial army was standing at Brigittenau near Vienna and did not move. Having taken Güns, the Ottoman army turned towards Graz in September 1532 and, as the season was advanced, turned back from there.", "5b55bad1"], "sonuc": ["The imperial armies were unable to mount a counter-operation against the Ottoman army that had entered their own lands. On returning to Istanbul, Suleiman the Lawgiver had celebrations held that lasted five days and five nights (22 November 1532). Peace negotiations were then held with the Habsburg envoys in Istanbul in 1533; according to TDV, İbrahim Pasha conducted these negotiations entirely, and in protocol the Ottoman grand vizier was considered Ferdinand's equal.", "12e8928a"], "tartisma": ["The TDV articles consulted do not give the duration of the siege of Güns or the details of the defence; they say that the fortress was 'taken'. The real aim of the campaign was not a fortress but a pitched battle with the emperor.", "c88fafcc"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman Empire", "86202f73"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Sultan Suleiman the Lawgiver · Grand Vizier İbrahim Pasha", "7f0cc987"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Habsburgs (fortress of Güns; the imperial army before Vienna)", "1c62fef8"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["Charles V · Ferdinand", "6e8cf39d"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source; TDV describes the imperial army as 'very large'", "9cb23a2b"]},
"savas-ankara-1402":{"baslik": ["Battle of Ankara (28 July 1402)", "ccf16167"], "tarih_metin": ["most probably Friday, 28 July 1402", "58731819"], "yer": ["Plain of Çubuk, Ankara", "7d961634"], "oncesi": ["When Timur's conquests reached Anatolia, a clash with the Ottomans, who laid claim to the same lands, became inevitable. Sultan Ahmad Jalayir and Qara Yusuf of the Qara Qoyunlu, fleeing from Timur, who took Baghdad in 1399, sought refuge with Bayezid; Bayezid handed back neither of them, and the two rulers exchanged letters full of harsh words. Timur took and destroyed Sivas, and Bayezid in turn took Erzincan and Kemah to punish the emir of Erzincan, who had submitted to Timur. In the spring of 1402 Timur set out conditions such as the return of Kemah, the restoration of the lands taken from the beyliks and the surrender of Qara Yusuf; when all of them were rejected, he marched on Anatolia. Bayezid lifted the siege of Istanbul and went out to face him.", "c4fbe6c0"], "akis": ["With an army consisting mostly of infantry, Bayezid had planned to meet Timur in the mountainous country in the direction of Tokat. Learning of this through his spies, Timur came to Ankara by another route and besieged the citadel. After a rapid march Bayezid came down into the plain of Çubuk by an unexpected route, but because he did not attack at once, Timur established a new front in a single night. During the fighting the Black Tatars, whom Timur had won over in advance through his spies, changed sides; the soldiers of some beyliks, Germiyan above all, also joined their former beys at Timur's side. The Ottoman army disintegrated. Bayezid, who continued to resist with the 3,000 men who remained with him, was finally taken prisoner.", "27d30dc8"], "sonuc": ["Bayezid died in captivity at Akşehir on 8 March 1403. Timur's emirs plundered Anatolia from end to end; the beyliks that the Ottomans had abolished came back to life. The struggle for the throne that began among Bayezid's sons, that is, the Interregnum, led to the loss of many places and to the brothers shedding one another's blood. According to the TDV, this defeat broke up the political unity established in Anatolia and delayed the Ottoman conquests and the conquest of Istanbul by nearly half a century.", "d74cab5a"], "tartisma": ["The day of the battle cannot be established with certainty from the sources; the TDV gives 28 July 1402 as 'most probably'. The strength of the armies varies by source.", "c2a6419d"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman army", "e562e57a"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Bayezid I the Thunderbolt · with a Serbian contingent of 20,000 on his side", "df836b82"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["70,000 according to Timur's proclamation of victory (fetihname), 90,000 according to Behişti", "da62a322"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Timur's army", "67d6faca"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["Timur", "eb7ab532"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["160,000, mostly cavalry; more than thirty elephants", "700b34ea"]},
"savas-aras-serdrud-1406-1408":{"baslik": ["Qara Yusuf's victories at the Aras and Sardrud (1406, 1408)", "f6dd6946"], "tarih_metin": ["2 Jumada al-Awwal 809 · 15 October 1406 (Aras) · 16 Dhu al-Qadah 810 · 13 April 1408 (Sardrud)", "bf617952"], "yer": ["The bank of the Aras river · Sardrud, near Tabriz", "5ab8cba8"], "oncesi": ["Qara Yusuf had fought against Timur together with the Jalayirid Sultan Ahmad and been defeated, and had taken refuge first with the Ottomans and then with the Mamluks. On orders from Cairo, the governor of Damascus had the two imprisoned; they were released only after Timur's death in 1405. Qara Yusuf took Avnik and regained possession of his lands in eastern Anatolia. Timur's grandson Abu Bakr Mirza, who was regarded as master of Azerbaijan and the two Iraqs and overlord of the local dynasties around them, had also taken Baghdad.", "1f2593d3"], "akis": ["Qara Yusuf marched against Abu Bakr Mirza and on 15 October 1406 defeated him in a battle on the bank of the Aras river. The second encounter took place on 13 April 1408 at Sardrud near Tabriz; Qara Yusuf won this battle as well. The TDV article consulted does not describe the stages of the two battles.", "2dbc8e4e"], "sonuc": ["The victory at Sardrud won Azerbaijan for the Qara Qoyunlu; Qara Yusuf then charged his commander Bistam Bey with the conquest of Persian Iraq. When his former ally Sultan Ahmad Jalayir attacked Tabriz in 1410, he was defeated and killed in the battle near the village of Asad; Baghdad and a large part of Iraq passed to the Qara Qoyunlu. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia, the Qara Qoyunlu in effect put an end to two centuries of Mongol domination in Iran and Iraq and played an important role especially in the Turkification of Azerbaijan.", "a3e94a70"], "tartisma": ["The victory at the Aras did not by itself give Tabriz to the Qara Qoyunlu; the battle that won Azerbaijan was Sardrud (TDV, Qara Qoyunlu).", "ba9938ff"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Qara Qoyunlu", "9b2f4a30"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Qara Yusuf", "781e331d"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Timurids (Azerbaijan and the two Iraqs)", "790aead6"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["Abu Bakr Mirza, Timur's grandson", "ec97e366"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"]},
"savas-arnavutluk-seferleri-1466":{"baslik": ["The Conqueror's Albanian campaigns against Skanderbeg (1466-1467)", "dabdf2e6"], "tarih_metin": ["Spring 1466 (first campaign) · June 1467 (second campaign)", "169c3734"], "yer": ["Central Albania — Krujë (Akçahisar) and Elbasan (İlbasan)", "c50f12ff"], "oncesi": ["Skanderbeg, who had been sent to the Ottoman court as a hostage when a child, fled from the camp of the beylerbey of Rumelia during the Zlatitsa (İzladi) war of 1443, seized his father's lands and Krujë, and in 1444 united with the Albanian lords at Lezhë (Leş). Murad II besieged Krujë in 1450 but could not take it; in 1451 Skanderbeg became a vassal of the king of Naples. For a time the Conqueror made a truce with him, but when the Ottoman-Venetian war began in the summer of 1463, Skanderbeg came to an agreement with Venice and Venetian troops took up quarters in Krujë. Balaban Pasha's attacks of 1464-1465 produced no result.", "3a35abdf"], "akis": ["In the spring of 1466 the Conqueror came to Albania with a large army; he tried to cow the rebels with heavy raids and to have Skanderbeg, who made sudden attacks, pursued in the mountains. When he arrived before Krujë, he saw how difficult it would be to take a fortress in such a very steep location and left the siege to Balaban Pasha. To keep the rebels in central Albania under pressure, he had the fortress of Elbasan built in twenty-five days. Skanderbeg went to Rome and Naples for help; on 12 December 1466 the Pope gave him 5000 ducats. Returning in April 1467, he made raids on the Ottoman forces besieging Krujë and besieged Elbasan. In June 1467 the Conqueror entered Albania again and held the Buzurşek defile, and the raiders (akıncı) advanced as far as the coast; he then besieged Krujë, but this time too the steep fortress could not be taken, and a force was left to continue the siege.", "8f4017a3"], "sonuc": ["The area around Elbasan became a new sanjak; Elbasan, which the Conqueror founded in 1466, soon turned into a Muslim centre. Skanderbeg died in 1468. According to TDV, the complete conquest of Albania was achieved only after his death, at the end of the wars waged against the Venetians, who claimed to be his heirs on the basis of the agreement of 1463: Krujë was taken in 1478, Shkodër in 1479 and Durrës (Durazzo) in 1501.", "4c7de850"], "tartisma": ["The TDV articles consulted do not give the days or the losses of the two campaigns. The information that Skanderbeg's force did not exceed 3000 is in the TDV article on Skanderbeg.", "e00e88af"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman Empire", "86202f73"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Sultan Mehmed the Conqueror · Balaban Pasha, sanjak governor of Ohrid", "2d80a6e4"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Skanderbeg and the Albanian forces, with Venetian support", "5fc69ce6"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["Skanderbeg", "98995f47"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["according to TDV, Skanderbeg's forces never exceeded 3000", "bf35dc58"]},
"savas-azak-1736":{"baslik": ["The Opening of the 1736-1739 War: From Azov to the Austrian Front (March 1736 - July 1737)", "5aa40dba"], "tarih_metin": ["Attack on Azov Dhu al-Qadah 1148 / March 1736 (TDV) · decision for war 20 Dhu al-Hijjah 1148 / 2 May 1736 · Austrian attack Safar 1150 / June 1737", "f116f420"], "yer": ["Azov, Crimea (Or Kapı, Gözleve, Kinburn), Ochakov (Özi); the Serbia-Bosnia-Wallachia front", "ee33ee56"], "oncesi": ["Russia, which together with its ally Austria had brought the wars of the Polish succession to a close in its own favour, was building new fortresses on the frontier of Ukraine and Podolia and massing troops around Azov. Russia regarded as a violation of its own territory Mahmud I's order that the Crimean forces bound for the Iranian front should pass through Kabarda in the Caucasus; although the order was withdrawn, Russia used it as a pretext.", "8be5b342"], "akis": ["In Dhu al-Qadah 1148 (March 1736) the Russians attacked the fortress of Azov without declaring war, and then marched on Or Kapı (Perekop), the gateway to the Crimea. The council that met in Istanbul on 2 May 1736, encouraged also by the French ambassador Villeneuve, decided on war against Russia; Grand Vizier Seyyid Mehmed Pasha became commander-in-chief (serasker), and the fleet was sent to the Crimean coast. During the summer Azov, Or Kapı, Gözleve and Kinburn fell, and Bakhchisaray and Akmescid were devastated. Despite this, the government kept up its hope of peace and did not take seriously the reports from the frontier that Austria was preparing for war. When peace talks had been agreed upon at Nemirov, Austria attacked in three columns in June 1737; the emperor's formal declaration of war is dated 14 July 1737.", "b987b3d1"], "sonuc": ["In July 1737 the Russians took Ochakov (Özi); the Austrians pressed hard on Niš, Banja Luka and Zvornik, entered Wallachia and seized Bucharest. Mahmud I changed his grand vizier. The victory won at Banja Luka in Bosnia in August 1737 and the recovery of Niš in the autumn of the same year turned the balance in the Ottomans' favour; the war closed in 1739 with the recovery of Belgrade.", "a3ba365a"], "tartisma": ["The sources differ on the day of the attack on Azov: TDV's article on Mahmud I says only 'Dhu al-Qadah 1148 (March 1736)'; Güler (Tarih İncelemeleri Dergisi 23, 2008), on whom the atlas relies, gives 11 Dhu al-Qadah 1148 / 24 March 1736; another Turkish academic source says 10 April 1736. Since the month of Dhu al-Qadah 1148 extends from mid-March to mid-April, both days fall within TDV's range of the month. TDV also gathers the fall of Azov, Gözleve, Or Kapı and Kinburn into a single day (13 July 1736); that these fortresses were taken on different days was not separately checked in this session. On Austria's entry, TDV places the actual attack in June 1737; the day of the formal declaration (14 July) rests, in the atlas entry, on Hambly and was not reread in this session.", "7130ab07"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman State and Crimean Khanate", "763d1b7a"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Mahmud I · Grand Vizier Seyyid Mehmed Pasha (serasker) · later Grand Vizier Muhsinzade Abdullah Pasha", "455fd01e"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Russia", "4af42a63"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["Empress Anna", "bc2a490e"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.2.ad": ["Austria (from 1737)", "2771b0ac"], "taraflar.2.komutan": ["Emperor Charles VI", "49ceacf4"], "taraflar.2.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"]},
"savas-banaluka-1737":{"baslik": ["The Battle of Banja Luka (4 August 1737)", "8458d77e"], "tarih_metin": ["4 August 1737 (TDV). The chronicle of the qadi Ömer Efendi, who lived through the event, places the battle on a day in July.", "0f860b45"], "yer": ["Fortress and plain of Banja Luka, bank of the Vrbas River, Bosnia", "4eb15903"], "oncesi": ["When Austria entered the war on Russia's side in June 1737, it attacked Bosnia in five columns; the largest column headed for Banja Luka. The Austrians, building bridges over the Sava, established themselves at Gradiška and besieged the fortress. The council of the governor of Bosnia, Hekimoğlu Ali Pasha, decided that, although Bužim and Cetin on the frontier were also under siege, Banja Luka in the interior should be relieved first.", "e6f9e7fc"], "akis": ["The besiegers surrounded the fortress with trenches and bombarded it from nine points; the defenders answered with raids. The governor approached the Vrbas with his army not directly but by a roundabout route along the hills; the unit under Mehmed's command also crossed the river and took up position on a hill farther up, and the next day the two forces joined. The Austrians realised that the Ottomans had crossed the river only when they saw them coming down from the hills. In the attack the following morning four positions fell; some of those who tried to withdraw were driven into the river and drowned. The fighting lasted until the afternoon.", "27d089cf"], "sonuc": ["The Austrian army, its bridges cut, was trapped against the Vrbas and broke up; those left behind fled towards the Sava. According to the chronicle, twelve cannon, three mortars and more than two thousand tents were captured; in the town a palace was turned into a hospital and 1,200 wounded were lodged there. This victory halted Austria's Bosnian campaign at its first major step and brought Hekimoğlu Ali Pasha great fame.", "de0c9f10"], "tartisma": ["① Day: TDV gives 4 August 1737. The chronicle of Ömer Efendi, translated by Fraser, however, places the battle in July and begins the advance on 'the 23rd of July'; the translator's note also admits that there may be errors in the chronicle's month names. The chronicle's use of the Julian-based Rumi month names could explain the 11-day difference, but this is an inference and is not stated in the source. ② Commander: TDV's article on Mahmud I says that 'Grand Vizier Abdullah Pasha at Banja Luka and Hekimoğlu Ali Pasha in Bosnia' forced the Austrians to withdraw; TDV's Banja Luka article and Ömer Efendi name Hekimoğlu Ali Pasha as the deliverer. ③ The figure of 100,000 for the Austrian force is an Ottoman number resting only on the statements of prisoners; no independent figure was found in this session.", "40b94699"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman State (Province of Bosnia)", "9255fe84"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Hekimoğlu Ali Pasha, governor of Bosnia · Captain Mehmed on the right wing", "239a804a"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Austrian (Habsburg) army", "0c2e163a"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["Prince Hildburghausen", "a0978b0e"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["according to Ömer Efendi, on the statements of prisoners, 80,000 soldiers and 20,000 peasants who followed them for plunder; this is the figure of a single Ottoman chronicle", "f97faa97"]},
"savas-belgrad-1440":{"baslik": ["The first siege of Belgrade (1440)", "3e40e547"], "tarih_metin": ["844 (1440) — lasted six months", "42c8ecf4"], "yer": ["Belgrade, where the Sava joins the Danube", "13b03eaa"], "oncesi": ["The Ottomans and Hungary were contending over the inheritance of the Serbian Despotate. Murad II, citing Olivera's marriage to Bayezid I the Thunderbolt, considered himself the legitimate heir; King Sigismund, however, came and took Belgrade, while the Ottomans held Golubac (Güvercinlik). Murad II seized Serbia in March 1439 and took the despot's capital, Smederevo, on 27 August 1439. The strong point of the Serbian inheritance that remained in Hungarian hands was Belgrade.", "9708c337"], "akis": ["The siege began in 1440 by land and from the Danube and lasted six months. The fortress held out, an epidemic broke out in the army and the siege was lifted. The TDV article on Murad II writes that gunfire from handguns, used against the Turks for the first time, appears to have been one of the reasons for this failure.", "cc3da6f5"], "sonuc": ["In the same year the new beylerbey of Rumelia, Şehabeddin Şahin Pasha, took Novo Brdo, an important silver-mining centre, but TDV regards the failure at Belgrade as a turning point for Murad II. Belgrade was besieged a second time by Sultan Mehmed the Conqueror (1456); this siege, in which he took part in person and was wounded, also ended without success. The fortress was not taken until 1521, during Suleiman the Lawgiver's Hungarian campaign; part of its population was brought to Istanbul and settled in the places known today as the Belgrade Forest and the Belgrade Gate.", "87910716"], "tartisma": ["The two TDV articles consulted stress different reasons for the failure: the article on Belgrade highlights the resistance and the epidemic, the article on Murad II the handgun fire.", "7a7cc8af"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman Empire", "86202f73"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Murad II", "e377c226"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Kingdom of Hungary (garrison of Belgrade)", "166c12af"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["not named in the TDV articles consulted", "694f7305"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"]},
"savas-birinci-inonu-1921":{"baslik": ["First Battle of İnönü (6–11 January 1921)", "4939421d"], "tarih_metin": ["6–11 January 1921; the day the Greek offensive was broken at İnönü, 10 January (TDV milli-mucadele; Uca, relying on the Turkish War of Independence series)", "83549aec"], "yer": ["The İnönü positions, between Bozüyük and Eskişehir; the Greek offensive set out from the directions of Bursa and Uşak", "6017e015"], "oncesi": ["In the autumn of 1920 the Greeks had taken İnegöl and Yenişehir. When it became clear that this advance could not be stopped with militia, the western front was divided in two: the north was given to İsmet Bey and the south to Refet Bey, and the irregular bands were disbanded and absorbed into the regular army. Çerkez Ethem and his brothers resisted this; on 27 December operations against them began, and on 5 January 1921 fighting broke out at Gediz. According to Uca, the new Greek government formed after the elections needed a success and believed that it could bring down Ankara while the Turkish forces were occupied with Ethem.", "de4b3e54"], "akis": ["On 6 January the Greek army went on the offensive from the directions of Bursa and Uşak; in the north three divisions marched on Eskişehir. The substantial Turkish forces at Gediz were shifted north to meet these divisions. The Greek forces, superior in numbers and equipment, were defeated at İnönü on 10 January and on 11 January withdrew to their former positions. Çerkez Ethem and his brothers, all their forces broken at Gediz, took refuge with the Greeks; the cavalry sent after them captured Ethem's forces between 14 and 22 January.", "1bd18791"], "sonuc": ["This first victory of the regular army gave Ankara breathing space; İsmet and Refet Beys were promoted to the rank of mirliva (brigadier general). On 13 January, from the rostrum of the Assembly, Mustafa Kemal Pasha hailed the victory with lines by Namık Kemal, and on 20 January he also said that although the army had the strength to pursue as far as Bursa, they had chosen to halt. Following this success the Allies agreed to convene the London Conference and to have Ankara too sit at the table.", "d4d648c2"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Western Front of the Grand National Assembly of Turkey (regular army)", "8d5c86dc"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Colonel İsmet Bey (İnönü)", "ab920758"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Greek army", "62d0bc46"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["no name given in the source", "cd262d77"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["according to the Nutuk (Mustafa Kemal's Speech) (Uca), three divisions marched on Eskişehir", "9293fc95"]},
"savas-bogdan-1788":{"baslik": ["The Moldavian Front Opens: The Crossing of the Dniester and the Rout at Galați (July 1788 - May 1789)", "12f31e56"], "tarih_metin": ["Crossing of the Dniester 20 June Old Style = 1 July 1788 (ESBE) · rout at Galați 1 May 1789 (TDV; ESBE 20 April Old Style)", "d725b3e1"], "yer": ["The Dniester crossing at Mogilev, Moldavia; Bârlad, Maximeni and Galați", "6ec3b99d"], "oncesi": ["In the spring of 1788 Russia formed two armies: Potemkin's was to besiege Ochakov, while Rumyantsev's Ukrainian army was to threaten Bender between the Dniester and the Bug and maintain the link with Austria. Austria too had entered the war in February 1788. Rumyantsev detached a column to the Austrians besieging Khotyn and, with his main forces, crossed the Dniester at Mogilev on 1 July 1788 and entered Moldavia.", "06324317"], "akis": ["The summer of 1788 passed in manoeuvres without any major engagement. With the fall of Khotyn and Jassy in September 1788, Moldavia came under occupation; Yusuf Pasha prepared to retake Moldavia with the forces he had assembled at Giurgiu (Yergöğü). In the spring of 1789 two columns were sent across to the left bank of the Danube; the aim was to defeat Coburg and the Russian advance units separately and to take Jassy. Coburg withdrew to Transylvania; Derfelden's division, however, defeated the Ottoman forces in succession at Bârlad, at Maximeni and finally at Galați on 1 May 1789.", "f8ca7e9a"], "sonuc": ["The rout at Galați brought about the end of Koca Yusuf Pasha. Selim III already did not trust him; this defeat became the visible reason for his dismissal, and on 7 June 1789 Kethüda Hasan Pasha, who had won success on the Austrian front, was appointed in his place. The Russian advance that began in Moldavia was to extend over the summer to the routs of Focșani and Râmnic.", "ae46fb0b"], "tartisma": ["The dates are consistent once the calendar difference is taken into account: ESBE uses the Russian Old Style calendar (the Dniester 20 June, Galați 20 April); adding 11 days gives 1 July 1788 and TDV's 1 May 1789. The force figures come only from the Russian source ESBE; they have not been compared with an Ottoman source.", "aacea888"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman army", "e562e57a"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Grand Vizier and commander-in-chief (serdar-ı ekrem) Koca Yusuf Pasha", "21434cd9"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["according to ESBE, in the spring of 1789, 30,000 men in two columns on the left bank of the Danube and a reserve of 10,000 men at Galați", "3c21ed91"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Russian Ukrainian army (together with Austria)", "900eb0e5"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["Field Marshal Rumyantsev; in 1789 General Derfelden's division · on the Austrian side Prince Coburg", "12354d89"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["according to ESBE, the Ukrainian army numbered up to 37,000 in 1788 and 35,000 in the spring of 1789", "e48d8589"]},
"savas-budin-1541":{"baslik": ["Capture of Buda and Its Conversion into a Province (1541)", "f3e5c13e"], "tarih_metin": ["arrival before Buda 26 August 1541 (4 Jumada al-Awwal 948) · the sultan's entry into the city 2 September 1541", "bf0b7710"], "yer": ["Buda, on the bank of the Danube, central Hungary", "b57b0283"], "oncesi": ["After the defeat at Mohács, Buda had handed over its keys to Suleiman the Lawgiver on 11 September 1526, while the Hungarian nobles put forward two separate candidates for the throne: some elected János Szapolyai (10 November 1526), others the Habsburg Ferdinand, brother of Charles V (17 December 1526). When Ferdinand took Buda in August 1527, Szapolyai asked the sultan for help; while marching on Vienna, Suleiman recovered the city after a brief struggle and left it to Szapolyai. The balance was upset in 1540. When Szapolyai died on 20 July 1540, he left behind a son only a few days old; but two years earlier he had secretly come to an agreement with Ferdinand, accepting that if he died without an heir the throne would pass to him. Relying on this agreement, Ferdinand claimed the whole of Hungary and in May 1541 besieged Buda. The Ottoman government held that the frontier should lie not on the Danube but to the west and north of Buda; the sultan decided to march on Hungary again.", "49f098e3"], "akis": ["Suleiman the Lawgiver arrived before Buda on 26 August 1541; outside the city the sultan was met by Sigismund, Szapolyai's infant son, the child's widowed mother and Bishop Martinuzzi. The sources do not describe what sort of fighting took place with Ferdinand's besieging forces. The decisive moment was not a pitched battle but a seizure: while the sultan had invited the heir and the leading Hungarian nobles to his tent outside the walls, the Janissaries occupied the key points of the city. It was announced that Buda would remain in Ottoman hands only until the heir came of age; despite this, a beylerbey was appointed to the city at once and a new province attached to the central government was established. The administration of the Transylvanian side was left to the little king. The sultan entered the city on 2 September, had ceremonies held symbolising that Buda was now an Ottoman city, and made the administrative appointments.", "29f86e44"], "sonuc": ["Buda ceased to be the capital of a dependent Hungarian kingdom and became the seat of a directly governed beylerbeylik; it marks Suleiman the Lawgiver's transition in Hungary from a policy of protection to permanent annexation. The Habsburgs' attempt in 1542 to retake it with a large army failed. According to the pay registers (mevacib defters), there were 2,965 Ottoman guards in the fortress in 1543. The city remained under Ottoman rule until it was lost in the siege of 1686.", "07051965"], "tartisma": ["① Day: the TDV article on Suleiman I gives the arrival before Buda as 26 August and the entry into the city as 2 September 1541; the widely cited date of 29 August 1541 does not appear in these articles. ② The TDV article on Buda gives the establishment of the province only at the level of the year (948/1541). ③ How the siege was lifted when the Ottoman army arrived, and the strength of the two sides, are not given in the sources.", "e2726e64"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman army", "e562e57a"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Sultan Suleiman the Lawgiver", "5c81178d"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Habsburg forces (the side besieging Buda)", "ded20a33"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["Ferdinand (Habsburg)", "1f806e1f"], "taraflar.2.ad": ["Szapolyai's circle in Buda", "ab8c52f9"], "taraflar.2.komutan": ["Szapolyai's infant son, the little king Sigismund · his mother, the widowed queen · Bishop Martinuzzi", "2d03c23e"]},
"savas-budin-savunmasi-1684":{"baslik": ["The first defence of Buda (July – 29 October 1684)", "94e6d34a"], "tarih_metin": ["summer 1684 (siege) · 20 Dhu al-Qadah 1095 · 29 October 1684 (the Austrians begin to withdraw)", "e7567f40"], "yer": ["Buda, on the bank of the Danube", "2ae0ce82"], "oncesi": ["After the defeat at Vienna in 1683, Merzifonlu Kara Mustafa Pasha was executed on 25 December 1683; his successor, Kara İbrahim Pasha, was not of the calibre to direct the war; he sent Bekri Mustafa Pasha to the front and himself remained in Belgrade. After the defeat at Vác, Hungary was lost except for Pest, Buda and Temeşvar. İbrahim Pasha, who had earlier been imprisoned for lifting the siege of Chyhyryn, had been appointed guardian of Buda and then governor of the province, and he fortified the city in a short time.", "d3788234"], "akis": ["Large Austrian armies besieged Buda for months. İbrahim Pasha resisted with few troops and little ammunition; through the sorties he ordered from time to time, he seized powder and supplies from the Austrian camp and defended the fortress for nearly three months. To the envoy who brought a summons to surrender, he said that the fortress held food enough for five years and 100,000 soldiers, in an attempt to damage the enemy's morale. The Austrians made several more assaults but achieved nothing and suffered heavy losses.", "034a35a6"], "sonuc": ["With the rain that fell during the last sortie also playing a part, the Austrians began on 29 October 1684 to lift the siege and withdraw; the news that some 40,000 Crimean troops were on the way also played a large role in this. Mehmed IV rewarded İbrahim Pasha and his commanders and changed his nickname 'Şeytan' (the Devil) to 'Melek' (the Angel). His fame grew so much that the army wanted him to become grand vizier; the envious Kara İbrahim Pasha plotted against him. The deliverance of Buda was short-lived: the city fell in the second siege on 2 September 1686.", "838b6bde"], "tartisma": ["The TDV articles consulted give neither the day the siege began, nor the forces of the two sides, nor the Austrian commander; the starting date of 14 July in the atlas entry does not appear in the texts consulted.", "9be474d7"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman State (garrison of Buda)", "718c9d77"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["İbrahim Pasha (Şeytan, later Melek), governor of Buda", "f3674bc8"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["TDV says 'few troops and little ammunition'; no figure", "71ce2661"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Habsburg (Austrian) army", "d706d63e"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["not named in the TDV articles consulted", "694f7305"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["TDV says 'very numerous'; no figure", "78979748"]},
"savas-bursa-1326":{"baslik": ["The Surrender of Bursa (6 April 1326)", "2789bfbd"], "tarih_metin": ["2 Jumada al-Awwal 726 · 6 April 1326", "814cf92f"], "yer": ["Bursa (Prusa), at the northern foot of Mount Uludağ", "9f2737c8"], "oncesi": ["When the alliance of the tekfurs (Byzantine lords) of the Bursa plain was defeated at the Dimbos (Dinboz) pass, the city was left on its own. Instead of attacking the walls, Osman Gazi chose to cut the city off from its surroundings: he had two blockade towers named Aktimur and Balabancık built to keep watch on Bursa, gave one of them to his nephew Aktimur and withdrew his forces. The Turkmen and Kızık villages on the slopes of Uludağ are thought to have been founded during these long years of blockade. Unable to obtain help from outside, the townspeople were gradually driven into hunger and poverty.", "35db8ed6"], "akis": ["The decisive outcome was brought not by a pitched battle but by negotiations for surrender. Orhan, who had taken over the headship of the beylik in the spring of 1326, came before the walls with all his forces and called on the city to surrender. According to the TDV article on Orhan, the agreement (ahidname) reached with the tekfur had three conditions: the soldiers entering the city would not plunder or take captives; those who wished to leave could take their property and depart under the protection of Ottoman soldiers; and 30,000 gold coins would be paid to Orhan. As Aşıkpaşazade relates it, when the tekfur left the city, Ahi Hasan was the first to climb onto the tower of the walls, and then the Muslims settled in the city.", "53986afa"], "sonuc": ["The Byzantine commander was allowed to go to Istanbul; his chief adviser, who had brought about the surrender, stayed in the city and entered Ottoman service, and the metropolitan of Bursa also continued in his office. The Greek population was moved to the quarters below the citadel, and only Turks were settled inside the citadel. Orhan converted the monastery within the citadel into a mosque, had the Bey's Palace (Bey Sarayı) built on the Tophane ridge and in 1327 had the first silver akçe of the beylik struck here. The complex consisting of a mosque, soup kitchen, madrasa, bathhouse and inn that he founded east of the citadel in 1339-1340 became the nucleus of the city's new market centre. Bursa was made the centre of the beylik, and the tombs of the early rulers from Osman Gazi to the Conqueror also rose here.", "b333ed51"], "tartisma": ["The beginning of the blockade is given differently in two TDV articles: the article on Osman I places the battle of Dimbos in 1303 and says that the city remained under siege for twenty-three years; the article on Bursa, however, places the tekfur's defeat at the Dinboz pass and the beginning of the siege in 1308 and speaks of a blockade lasting \"more than ten years\". The day of the surrender (6 April 1326) is the same in both articles.", "4617aae7"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman Beylik", "d8cb2121"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Orhan Bey", "6726cdef"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["The fortress of Bursa, subject to Byzantium", "624fdda3"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["The tekfur of Bursa (his name does not appear in the sources)", "5b66491e"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"]},
"savas-buyuk-taarruz-1922":{"baslik": ["The Great Offensive and the Commander-in-Chief's Pitched Battle (26 August – 9 September 1922)", "fb596fe4"], "tarih_metin": ["Offensive 26 August 1922 · Afyon 27 August · Commander-in-Chief's Battle and Kütahya 30 August · surrender of Trikoupis's column on the evening of 1 September, captivity 2 September · Eskişehir 2 September · Aydın 7 September · İzmir 9 September 1922 (TDV; Çaykıran)", "308b2213"], "yer": ["The Afyonkarahisar front; Dumlupınar–Adatepe; Kütahya, Uşak, Manisa, İzmir", "ac46a1fc"], "oncesi": ["After Sakarya the Greek army had withdrawn to the Eskişehir–Afyon line. The Treaty of Ankara concluded with France freed the units in the south. The Western Front was organised into two armies, and reserve officers and men born in 1315-1317 were called up. Weapons, ammunition and aircraft were bought with money sent by the Indian Caliphate Committee and by Bukhara. The British would not meet Fethi Bey, who had gone to Europe for a last attempt at peace; Greece, for its part, proclaimed the autonomy of Ionia and attempted to march on Istanbul.", "d769f3ee"], "akis": ["The offensive began on the Afyon front on the morning of 26 August; within two days half of the Greek forces were put out of action and the army was encircled. Afyon was liberated on 27 August. On 30 August, around Adatepe, six divisions surrounded the Greek forces on all four sides; this Commander-in-Chief's Pitched Battle, directed by Mustafa Kemal Pasha in person, completed the encirclement. The same day a cavalry division entered burnt Kütahya. With the order “Armies, your first objective is the Mediterranean”, the pursuit began. The encircled column of Trikoupis raised the white flag on the evening of 1 September; Trikoupis learned from Mustafa Kemal Pasha that he had been appointed Greek commander-in-chief. On 9 September Nureddin Pasha's units raised the Turkish flag over the government house in İzmir.", "16304458"], "sonuc": ["Mustafa Kemal Pasha entered İzmir the next day; a fire that began in the Armenian quarter on 12 September lasted three days in various parts of the city. The march of the Turkish army towards Çanakkale gave rise to the Straits crisis, and the Armistice of Mudanya, signed on 11 October 1922, returned Eastern Thrace without fighting. The military victory prepared the ground for the negotiating table at Lausanne.", "5b00f5b6"], "tartisma": ["How Trikoupis was taken prisoner is disputed; Çaykıran tries to resolve these disputes using works translated from Greek held at ATASE (the Turkish General Staff's military history and strategic studies directorate). The number of those who surrendered appears in Greek sources as 84 officers and 1,600 enlisted men, and in another Greek source as 1 general, 1 divisional commander, 84 officers and 1,500 enlisted men.", "85ebe98e"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Army of the Grand National Assembly of Turkey (Western Front: First Army and Second Army)", "7a6a5ac7"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Commander-in-Chief Mustafa Kemal Pasha · First Army Nureddin Pasha · Second Army Yakup Şevki Pasha", "068cd171"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["according to the TDV, for the first time a force of nearly 200,000 was assembled on the western front; an air squadron of ten aircraft", "df34263e"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Greek Army of Anatolia", "86599892"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["in the field, Trikoupis and Digenis, commanders of the I and II Corps (Çaykıran)", "90413263"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"]},
"savas-caldiran-1514":{"baslik": ["Battle of Chaldiran (23 August 1514)", "b6edb82b"], "tarih_metin": ["23 August 1514", "46740159"], "yer": ["Plain of Chaldiran, Iranian Azerbaijan", "0d15772a"], "oncesi": ["The caliphs (deputies) whom Shah Ismail sent to Anatolia were carrying out extensive Shiite propaganda, and uprisings such as those of Şahkulu and Nur Ali Halife caused great loss of life. Selim I, who came to the throne in 1512, demanded the extradition of Prince Murad, who had taken refuge with the shah; when the envoy he sent was killed, he decided to settle the matter by war. Fatwas were obtained, and according to the TDV, before the campaign some 40,000 people in Anatolia identified as supporters of the shah were eliminated. The army marched east in hardship because of the crops and pastures burned on the shah's orders; at Eleşkirt the Janissaries grew so restless that they fired bullets at the sultan's tent.", "08020f5b"], "akis": ["When the army reached Chaldiran on 23 August, it took up battle order at once by Selim's decision, despite the statesmen's proposal of a day's rest. In the centre were the sultan, the Janissaries and the guns; on the right were the troops of Anatolia, on the left those of Rumelia. In front of the guns, which were chained to one another, a barricade of carts and camels had been set up. The battle began with an attack by the elite Safavid cavalry on both wings at once. On the right wing Sinan Pasha drew back his troops according to plan and brought the enemy in front of the guns; the Safavid wing broke up and Ustaclu Muhammad Khan was killed. On the left wing, however, the azebs (light infantry) could not withdraw in time, Hasan Pasha, the beylerbey of Rumelia, was killed and the ranks gave way. The intervention of the Janissaries changed the balance. The horse of the shah, who had been wounded by a musket shot, fell to the ground. At that moment Mirza Ali, a close companion of the shah who closely resembled him, presented himself as the shah to the Ottoman horsemen bearing down on him and surrendered; thanks to this the real shah was able to escape.", "c85b0950"], "sonuc": ["With the shah's flight the Safavid army broke up, and its camp and treasury fell into Ottoman hands. On the Ottoman side too, the beylerbey of Rumelia and ten sanjak beys were killed. Selim entered Tabriz on 5 September and a week later turned back without wintering there, because of the Janissaries' objections. The following year Kemah was taken and the Dulkadirid lands were annexed; through the efforts of İdris Bitlisi, southeastern Anatolia from Diyarbekir to Mosul passed under Ottoman administration. The silk road running from Tabriz to Aleppo and Bursa came under Ottoman control.", "25919fad"], "tartisma": ["The strength of the two armies is given only approximately in the sources; the exact number is not known.", "2d901584"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman army", "e562e57a"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Sultan Selim the Grim · beylerbey of Anatolia Hadım Sinan Pasha · beylerbey of Rumelia Hasan Pasha", "8879d8fc"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["approximately 100,000; tired from the long march", "4b3a570d"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Safavid army", "ef0f17cb"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["Shah Ismail · Ustaclu Muhammad Khan", "cd8d3514"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["at least as large as the Ottoman army; mainly cavalry and fresh", "75a90392"]},
"savas-canakkale-bozgunu-1656":{"baslik": ["The Dardanelles Rout (26 June / 13 July 1656)", "818d581e"], "tarih_metin": ["26 June 1656 (the day the naval battle began) — the losses/withdrawal continued until 13 July 1656 (the atlas's date of change)", "832807db"], "yer": ["The Dardanelles, the narrow passage between Kilitbahir and Kal'a-i Sultaniyye", "08080fdf"], "oncesi": ["In the tenth year of the Cretan War (1645-), Venice was continuing its strategy of regularly blockading the Dardanelles in order to cut off Ottoman supplies to the island. In 1654 Kapudan Pasha (Grand Admiral) Kara Murad Pasha had defeated the Venetian fleet in the narrow passage between Kilitbahir and Kal'a-i Sultaniyye and temporarily lifted the blockade — but this victory did not last; Venice gathered its strength and massed once more before the strait.", "b08492aa"], "akis": ["On 26 June 1656 the two fleets met in the strait. The TDV article `canakkale-bogazi` does not give the details of the engagement (tactics, duration); it records only the result: the Ottoman fleet of eighty-six vessels was routed by the Venetians. In the period that lasted until 13 July, the card's own date of change, the fleet dispersed, Istanbul's sea link to the Mediterranean was in effect cut, and Venice occupied Tenedos (Bozcaada) and Lemnos.", "9b1a0869"], "sonuc": ["When the Strait came under Venetian blockade, the flow of provisions (especially grain) reaching Istanbul by sea was cut off; prices rose in the city and unrest grew among the people. In this atmosphere of military and administrative crisis, on 26 September 1656 Turhan Sultan, mother of Mehmed IV, brought Köprülü Mehmed Pasha, an elderly and stern administrator, to the grand vizierate — according to the TDV article `koprulu-mehmed-pasa`, this appointment was an attempt to halt the systematic weakening of the Ottomans against Venice. As soon as Köprülü took office, he also launched a counter-operation at sea: in 1657 a new naval battle of the Dardanelles lasting three days ended in an Ottoman victory (after the enemy flagship blew up when hit by a cannonball fired from Kumburnu), the blockade was lifted, and in the same year Tenedos and Lemnos were retaken.", "7f08b982"], "tartisma": ["The TDV article that was read (`canakkale-bogazi`) does not give the tactical detail of the battle (how the rout came about); it reports only the result. For this reason the card does not answer the question \"how was it lost\"; it relates only \"what happened and what followed\".", "88e6e97c"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman State", "86202f73"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Kapudan Pasha (Grand Admiral) Sarı Kenan Pasha", "8dd1e8c4"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["eighty-six ships: fifty galleys, nine galleasses (mavna), twenty-seven galleons", "a51e2ab6"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Republic of Venice", "f30b04e7"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["not named in the TDV article that was read", "dfd87a77"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"]},
"savas-centla-1519":{"baslik": ["Battle of Centla (Cintla): Cortés in Tabasco (March 1519)", "48741442"], "tarih_metin": ["Arrival at the Tabasco river 12 March 1519 · pitched battle on the day of 'Our Lady's feast in March' (Bernal Díaz) — the Feast of the Annunciation, 25 March", "ea494389"], "yer": ["Mouth of the Grijalva (Tabasco) river, the town of Potonchán and the plain of Cintla one league inland — today the state of Tabasco, Mexico", "38593e5d"], "oncesi": ["After Cozumel, Cortés's fleet came to the Tabasco river, where Grijalva had been received in friendly fashion a year earlier. This time the people of the coast had taken up arms: neighbouring Potonchán and other towns had accused the Tabascans of cowardice for having given gifts to Grijalva and had incited them to resist the Spaniards; Melchorejo, the interpreter brought from Cotoche, had also escaped by night and joined them. The calls for peace made through Jerónimo de Aguilar, and the request to take on water and food, were rejected.", "fb036ee3"], "akis": ["The Spaniards landed on the shallow and muddy shore under a shower of arrows, got over the town's thick wooden palisades and took Potonchán. A few days later two companies scouting the surroundings were attacked; Cortés had the horses brought off the ships. The next morning, the day of Our Lady's feast, the infantry under Ordás marched to the plain of Cintla and found itself in the midst of rank upon rank of Maya troops; although the cannon and firearms inflicted losses, the encirclement did not break. When Cortés and thirteen horsemen, who had gone round by a separate route because of the swamps, struck from the rear, a rout began in the Maya ranks. The fighting lasted about an hour.", "b5f225f6"], "sonuc": ["Bernal Díaz writes that he counted more than eight hundred dead on the field and that the Spaniards buried two of their wounded. The lords of Tabasco sued for peace; they presented cloaks, gold ornaments and twenty women. The women were baptised; one of them took the name doña Marina and, as Cortés's interpreter, played a key role in the conquest. In memory of the day of victory, the town later founded there was named Santa María de la Victoria. From there the fleet headed to San Juan de Ulúa, into Aztec lands.", "04cc3e55"], "tartisma": ["The dating differs: the atlas entry is at 1519-03-25, and its internal note assigns the battle to 14 March and the founding to 25 March; yet Bernal Díaz places the pitched battle itself on the day of 'Our Lady's feast in March' and also links the name of the town to that day — this testimony favours 25 March. Bernal Díaz rejects the report passed on by Gómara that a saint on a white horse (Santiago or Saint Peter) appeared in the battle, saying that he did not see it with his own eyes; he writes that the man he saw was Francisco de Morla. The figures rest on a single witness, Bernal Díaz, who wrote about fifty years after the event.", "7dc8f8ab"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Spanish expeditionary force (from Cuba)", "31ce368c"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Hernán Cortés · Diego de Ordás in command of the infantry · Mesa, artillery", "a67a178d"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["total not given in this source; 13 horsemen in the battle (Bernal Díaz lists them by name)", "85b3c3d0"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["The lords of Tabasco (Chontal Maya) and the towns attached to them", "3c7d5fc2"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["name not given in the source", "6550879c"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["according to Bernal Díaz, 'more than twelve thousand' warriors had gathered in the town", "b601c7b1"]},
"savas-cerbe-1560":{"baslik": ["Naval Battle of Djerba and Capture of the Fortress (1560)", "a22f4cea"], "tarih_metin": ["naval battle 11–14 May 1560 · capture of the fortress 30 July 1560", "fa4bb04c"], "yer": ["Island of Djerba, Gulf of Gabès, east of Tunisia", "23cd5f48"], "oncesi": ["Djerba was a base that Turgut Reis used for his raids in the western Mediterranean. Because of storms and epidemics, the allied Crusading fleet, whose real objective was Tripoli, was able to reach the island only in early 1560; on 12 March 1560 it seized the island and had a strong fortress built there. Turgut Reis had reported the preparations to Istanbul in advance. Piyale Pasha left Istanbul on 28 March with a fleet of 120 galleys, took on supplies at Modon, plundered the island of Gozo near Malta and then headed for Djerba.", "8e5e56e9"], "akis": ["The clash between the two fleets began on 11 May and lasted three days. A manoeuvre in which the Ottoman fleet split into two scattered the large allied fleet on 14 May. According to an account based on an archival record, nineteen allied galleys were captured, twenty-six barques were destroyed and some of the ships took refuge at the fortress on the island. After the defeat at sea, the allied soldiers shut themselves up in the fortress of Djerba that they themselves had built. The fortress was besieged by Turgut Pasha's forces from the land and by the fleet from the sea. Defended by Spanish, Italian, German and Maltese soldiers, it held out for about two months and fell on 30 July 1560.", "682f024a"], "sonuc": ["The island was attached to the beylerbeylik of Tripoli and its fortress was repaired. Piyale Pasha returned to Istanbul at the end of September; the prisoners, captured ships and captive admirals he brought with him were received before the eyes of the people and the sultan. In recognition of this success the Pasha was married to Gevherhan Sultan, daughter of Prince Selim. In the TDV's assessment, Djerba was the victory that consolidated the Ottoman supremacy in the Mediterranean that Barbarossa had begun at Preveza.", "0b55a841"], "tartisma": ["Allied losses differ between two TDV articles: the 'Djerba' article reports, as a tradition, that close to seventy ships were sunk and about twenty captured; the 'Piyale Pasha' article, relying on an archival document, gives nineteen galleys and twenty-six barques. The naval victory is placed on 11 May in some chronologies and on 14 May in others; the two are the beginning and the end of the same three-day engagement.", "8ff4c33c"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman fleet and the forces of Tripoli", "f80de5f5"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Kapudan Pasha (Grand Admiral) Piyale Pasha · beylerbey of Tripoli Turgut Pasha (Reis) · Uluç Ali Reis with the fleet", "cc055828"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["120 galleys", "dfcebbf5"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Allied Christian fleet under Spanish direction", "5b6934bd"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["more numerous than the Ottoman fleet", "9162711f"]},
"savas-cesme-1770":{"baslik": ["Raid on Çeşme (6–7 July 1770)", "c047caff"], "tarih_metin": ["first engagement north of the harbour 5 July 1770 (11 Rabi al-Awwal 1184) · attack in the harbour 6–7 July 1770", "1a3934ff"], "yer": ["Çeşme Harbour and the waters to its north (Oinousses Islands (Koyun Adaları), Toprak Island), İzmir coast", "ef2b804b"], "oncesi": ["In the Ottoman–Russian war that began in 1768, Russia, with British support, sent its fleet into the Mediterranean in order to rouse the Greeks of the Morea to revolt; in early 1770 the squadron began operating off the Morea. Strengthened in the spring by the arrival of the Orlov brothers, who came overland through Europe, the Russian fleet worsted off Nauplion (Anabolu) and in the waters off Hydra (Suluca) the squadron of Hüsameddin Pasha, which had been sent to help Muhsinzade Mehmed Pasha, serasker (commander) of the Morea. The Ottoman fleet withdrew from among the Aegean islands, passed through the Samos Strait and, on reaching the Oinousses Islands north of Çeşme, again encountered Spiridov's squadron. On 5 July battle order was formed in a crescent north of Çeşme harbour, southeast of Toprak Island. During the engagement, when the ship of Cezayirli Hasan Bey and that of Spiridov caught fire, the other ships, fearing that the flames would spread to them, drew away from the scene of battle on both sides.", "a6a047af"], "akis": ["Although Cezayirli Hasan Bey opposed it, Hüsameddin Pasha and Cafer Bey took the fleet into Çeşme harbour, which had no room for manoeuvre; the ships anchored very close to one another under the protection of guns placed on shore and prepared for a defensive fight. The Russian squadron did not miss this opportunity: on 6 July it closed the mouth of the harbour and sent fireships inside. About thirty of the closely anchored Ottoman ships went up in flames. By the morning of 7 July all that remained of the fleet was the flagship galley (baştarda) of the Kapudan Pasha (Grand Admiral) Hüsameddin Pasha; it too escaped by fleeing to the island of Chios.", "4b90ca40"], "sonuc": ["The Ottoman side lost almost the whole of its fleet and around 5,000 of its men. The wounded Cafer Bey and Cezayirli Hasan Bey returned to Istanbul overland, and Hüsameddin Pasha was removed from the office of Kapudan Pasha. Catherine II gave Alexei Orlov the title 'Chesmensky' and had a monument erected in Russia in memory of the victory. With no obstacle left before it, the Russian squadron roamed the Aegean freely and blockaded the Dardanelles; however, it did not dare to force the well-fortified strait, contenting itself with landing troops on Lemnos and besieging its fortress, and the island was saved by the timely arrival of Cezayirli Hasan Bey. The blockade harmed Ottoman trade only partially; but the presence of the Russian fleet in the Aegean and the Mediterranean until 1774 was an important factor in the signing of the Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca.", "deb1cf9f"], "tartisma": ["① Strength: the sources do not give the numbers of ships and men in the two fleets. ② Date: the TDV article on the Çeşme Incident describes the first engagement as 5 July, the attack in the harbour as 6 July and the outcome as the morning of 7 July; the article on Mustafa III dates the attack to '6–7 July 1770' — the two articles are consistent. ③ Responsibility: the TDV attributes the decision to take the fleet into the harbour to Hüsameddin Pasha and Cafer Bey, and writes that Hasan Bey opposed it.", "bb6b3d8b"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman fleet", "370d7222"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Kapudan Pasha (Grand Admiral) Hüsameddin Pasha · commander of the right wing Cezayirli Hasan Bey · Cafer Bey, Bey of Rhodes", "3e64c012"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["total not known; the ships burned in the harbour numbered 'about thirty'", "6baa9051"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Russian fleet", "dce69dc9"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["Admiral Spiridov · the British Admiral Elphinston · Alexei Orlov and his brother Theodore Orlov", "bfd82567"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["not known; the squadron had entered the Mediterranean with British support", "f390c93d"]}
});
